const e=[{id:1,code:"GOV-101",icon:"DoorOpen",accent:"#2563eb",title:"Emergency evacuation plan",subtitle:"Emergency evacuation plans",summary:"Creating an evacuation plan for government offices and buildings",blocks:[{body:"The emergency evacuation plan is one of the most important documents of any government office. This plan protects the lives of staff and visitors during incidents such as fires, earthquakes or chemical hazards."},{heading:"1. Steps for creating the plan",body:"A successful plan requires these steps:",points:["Floor plan: prepare a complete floor plan of the building showing the evacuation routes and the locations of fire extinguishers.","Evacuation routes: every floor must have at least two exit routes located far apart from each other (NFPA 101), and exit doors must open from the inside without a key.","Assembly point: a safe location outside the building, at a recommended distance of at least 50 meters.","Responsibility: designate an evacuation warden for every floor.","Alarm system: a fire bell or siren that can be heard on all floors.","Drills: a practical evacuation drill once every 3 months."]},{heading:"2. Rules for safe evacuation",body:"When the alarm sounds:",points:["Walk calmly and in an orderly manner — do not run.","Do not use the elevator — exit by the stairs only.","If there is smoke, stay low and crawl beneath the smoke.","Before opening a door, test it with the back of your hand — if it is hot, do not open it and find another route.","Help people who need assistance and close doors behind you.","Go directly to the assembly point and remain there."]},{heading:"3. The role of wardens",body:"Every floor needs a fire warden:",points:["Fire Warden: the person with primary responsibility for the floor.","Checks all rooms (including restrooms and storerooms) so that no one is left behind.","Directs people to the evacuation routes.","Counts people at the assembly point and informs the 115 team."]},{heading:"4. Visitors and people who need assistance",body:"The plan must cover everyone, not only staff:",points:["Visitors do not know the routes: counter staff take them to the assembly point.","Everyone who uses a wheelchair or has limited mobility has a named buddy and a planned route.","People who cannot hear the alarm (deaf or hard of hearing) are alerted by touch or by light signals.","The staff list and the visitor log go with the warden to the assembly point."]},{heading:"5. After a drill",body:"Every drill is a chance to improve the plan:",points:["Was the alarm heard in every room, including restrooms and storerooms?","Were the exit routes and doors open and clear?","Was the count at the assembly point quick and accurate?","Write down the problems, name a person to fix each one and check it in the next drill."]}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/gov_evacuation.png",title:"Evacuation floor plan",description:"Example of a floor plan"}],quiz:[{question:"How far from the building should the assembly point be?",options:[{text:"10 meters",correct:!1},{text:"25 meters",correct:!1},{text:"50 meters",correct:!0},{text:"100 meters",correct:!1}],explanation:"At least 50 meters, so that it is away from falling glass, debris and smoke and does not block the route of emergency vehicles."},{question:"During an evacuation you reach a closed door. What do you do before opening it?",options:[{text:"Open it quickly to see if there is smoke",correct:!1},{text:"Test it with the back of your hand",correct:!0},{text:"Force it open with your shoulder",correct:!1},{text:"Touch the handle with your palm",correct:!1}],explanation:"Before opening a door, test it with the back of your hand; if it is hot, do not open it and find another route."},{question:"What does the fire warden of a floor do during an evacuation?",options:[{text:"Stays at the desk to guard the office files",correct:!1},{text:"Goes back inside to collect forgotten belongings",correct:!1},{text:"Checks every room so no one is left behind",correct:!0},{text:"Puts out every fire on the floor before leaving",correct:!1}],explanation:"The fire warden checks all rooms, including restrooms and storerooms, so that no one is left behind, directs people to the evacuation routes and counts them at the assembly point for the 115 team."},{question:"During an evacuation, thick smoke has filled the main stairway. What is the best action?",options:[{text:"Use the second exit route and close doors behind you",correct:!0},{text:"Hold your breath and run down the main stairway",correct:!1},{text:"Take the elevator because the stairway is full of smoke",correct:!1},{text:"Wait inside the stairway until the smoke clears",correct:!1}],explanation:"Every floor has two routes far apart so that one can replace the other; both the elevator and the smoke can kill."},{question:'At the assembly point a colleague says, "My laptop is on my desk, I will run back for it." The fire is not out yet. What do you do?',options:[{text:"I stop him and say no one goes in until the 115 team allows it",correct:!0},{text:"I let him go if no smoke can be seen at the main entrance",correct:!1},{text:"I go with him so he is not alone and we come back fast",correct:!1},{text:"I let him go if his floor is far from the fire",correct:!1}],explanation:"Smoke spreads quickly through the whole building, even to distant floors. Nothing is worth a life; going back in happens only when Civil Defense allows it."},{question:"A manager wants to lock the rear exit door with a key during working hours so that nobody leaves early. What is the safe answer?",options:[{text:"The door must open from the inside without a key; control it some other way",correct:!0},{text:"Locking is fine if the guard near the door keeps the key all day",correct:!1},{text:"Locking is fine as long as the main entrance stays open during working hours",correct:!1},{text:'Locking is fine if a "door closed" sign and the evacuation plan are posted on it',correct:!1}],explanation:"Exit doors must open from the inside without a key; in a fire nobody has time to look for a key, and the main route may be blocked."}],steps:[{title:"Raise the alarm",detail:"If you see fire or smoke, use the manual alarm button and warn the people near you out loud. Call 115 from a safe place, or make sure someone else has called.",critical:!0},{title:"Stop work and leave",detail:"When the alarm sounds, leave your work and go out by the nearest safe exit. Use the stairs, not the elevator, and close doors behind you so that smoke does not spread.",critical:!0},{title:"The warden sweeps the floor",detail:"The floor warden quickly checks the rooms, restrooms, storerooms and meeting rooms on the way out, helps visitors and anyone who needs assistance, and then leaves. The warden does not go into thick smoke.",critical:!0},{title:"If smoke blocks the way",detail:"Use the second exit route. If you must pass through smoke, stay low and test every door with the back of your hand before opening it. If both routes are blocked, go into a room with a window, close the door, block the gap under it with cloth, call 115 and say where you are.",critical:!0},{title:"Stay at the assembly point",detail:"Go to the assembly point and report to your floor warden so that you are counted. The warden gives the 115 team the name and last known location of anyone who is missing."},{title:"Return only when allowed",detail:"No one goes back into the building until Civil Defense or the competent authority says it is safe. After every incident or drill, review the plan and fix what did not work."}],warnings:["Do not use the elevator during a fire: it may stop and trap you, or take you to the floor that is burning.","Do not go back into the building for your belongings or to search for someone: report the missing person to the 115 team, because they go in with protective clothing and equipment.","Do not lock exit doors with a key and do not store boxes or furniture in stairways and corridors: a blocked route becomes a trap in an emergency."],tips:["Set up a personal plan and a named buddy for every staff member who needs assistance (wheelchair, illness, pregnancy).","Keep a visitor log at the main entrance so that the warden at the assembly point knows how many visitors were inside.",'Post the evacuation plan with a "You are here" mark near the stairs and elevators.',"In drills, treat the main route as blocked from time to time so that staff use and learn the second route."],activity:{prompt:"You are at the service counter and several citizens are waiting in line. The fire alarm sounds and you smell smoke from the corridor. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I finish serving the first citizen, then I leave",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: every minute of delay lets smoke block the route; the paperwork can be done later."},{text:"I calmly ask the citizens to come with me and lead them down the stairs to the assembly point",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: visitors do not know the routes; staff lead them calmly along the safe route and they are counted at the assembly point."},{text:"I go to my office to take the important files with me",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: lives come before documents; important documents should already be in a safe or copied before an incident."},{text:"I send the elderly citizen down in the elevator so he does not fall on the stairs",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: the elevator may stop and trap him; instead go down the stairs slowly with him or ask for help."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"You are the warden of the second floor. A fire has started in a storeroom on the first floor and the alarm is sounding. Smoke is entering the main stairway, but the second stairway at the end of the corridor is clear. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"I direct people to the second stairway and close the doors of the main stairway",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: this is why there are two routes far apart; closing the doors keeps smoke off the floor."},{text:"I take people quickly down the main stairway because it is closer",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: there is smoke in the main stairway, and breathing it can make a person pass out within a few breaths."},{text:"I tell everyone to stay in their offices until the 115 team arrives",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: staying in a room is only for when both routes are blocked; here a clear route exists."},{text:"I go down to the first floor to put out the storeroom fire with an extinguisher",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: a fire whose smoke has reached the stairway is no longer small; the warden's job is to clear the floor."}]},{prompt:"Everyone is at the assembly point. During the count, an employee from the archive is missing. A colleague says she may be in the restroom near the archive on the second floor. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I go back into the building to search the restroom myself",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: without protective clothing you become another victim; the 115 team goes in with equipment."},{text:"I tell the 115 team at once her name and her last known location",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: clear information (who, which floor, which room) shortens the search and saves lives."},{text:"I wait, because she may have left by another door and will turn up",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: waiting loses precious time; report at once, and if she turns up, tell the team."},{text:"I send two young colleagues to check the restroom quickly",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: being young does not protect anyone from smoke; you would put two more people in danger."}]}],realCase:"In a government office, a small fire started in a first-floor storeroom and smoke reached the main stairway. Staff had used the second stairway several times in drills, so the floor wardens calmly led people to it and closed the smoke doors. Visitors at the service counter left with a staff member. At the assembly point, the visitor log and the staff list were used, and it was clear that no one was left inside. Afterwards, the manager cleared old paper and boxes out of the storeroom. Lesson: a second route that has been practised saves lives when the main route is blocked."},{id:2,code:"GOV-102",icon:"FileLock",accent:"#059669",title:"Document protection",subtitle:"Document protection",summary:"Procedures for protecting important documents from fire and water",blocks:[{body:"Government documents are a valuable asset. Losing them to fire or water can halt the work of an office and destroy people's rights. For this reason, protecting and copying them is essential."},{heading:"1. Classifying documents",body:"Determining the importance of documents:",points:["Level A — very important: contracts, financial, legal.","Level B — important: reports and official letters.","Level C — routine: unofficial correspondence."]},{heading:"2. Physical protection",body:"Physical protection measures:",points:["Fire-resistant safes: for Level A documents.","Locked metal cabinets, raised above the floor.","Waterproof plastic bags and boxes.","Environmental control: a cool, dry location with stable temperature and humidity."]},{heading:"3. The 3-2-1 rule for backup copies",body:"The 3-2-1 rule:",points:["3 copies of all important data.","2 different types of storage (media).","1 copy at another location, outside the building."]},{heading:"4. Work continuity plan",body:"Every department must know how it will restart its work after a fire or flood:",points:["A list of vital records: the documents without which work stops.","Where their copies are and who can restore them quickly.","Where and how services continue if the building cannot be used.","The plan is practised by actually restoring copies, not only on paper."]},{heading:"5. After a fire or flood",body:"Saving documents safely:",points:["First of all, go in only with permission from Civil Defense.","Move wet documents gently to a cool, dry, well-ventilated place; do not use heaters.","Save Level A first, and ask records-conservation specialists for help.","Record everything lost or damaged before anything is thrown away."]}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/gov_backup.png",title:"Backup copies",description:"The 3-2-1 rule"}],quiz:[{question:"What does the 3-2-1 rule mean?",options:[{text:"3 computers, 2 servers, 1 locked server room",correct:!1},{text:"3 copies, 2 types of media, 1 off-site",correct:!0},{text:"3 days",correct:!1},{text:"3 backups a day, 2 kept for a week, 1 kept for a month",correct:!1}],explanation:"3 copies on 2 types of media, and 1 copy at another location outside the building."},{question:"Where should Level A documents, such as contracts, be kept?",options:[{text:"On open shelves near the office window",correct:!1},{text:"In a fire-resistant safe",correct:!0},{text:"In a cardboard box under the desk",correct:!1},{text:"On the floor of the basement storeroom",correct:!1}],explanation:"Level A documents (contracts, financial, legal) are very important and are kept in fire-resistant safes; other documents go in locked metal cabinets raised above the floor."},{question:"Which storage conditions suit important documents?",options:[{text:"Warm, next to the heaters, to keep the paper dry",correct:!1},{text:"In the basement, close to the water pipes",correct:!1},{text:"Sunny, by the windows, so the air stays fresh",correct:!1},{text:"Cool, dry, stable temperature and humidity",correct:!0}],explanation:"Keep documents in a cool, dry place with stable temperature and humidity, away from water: losing them to fire or water can halt an office's work and destroy people's rights."},{question:"The contracts database and its backup are both on one server in the same building as the archive. What is the main weakness?",options:[{text:"There is no copy at another location outside the building",correct:!0},{text:"The server is too old and should be replaced",correct:!1},{text:"The backup is made daily instead of hourly",correct:!1},{text:"The paper contracts are in a different archive room",correct:!1}],explanation:"Under the 3-2-1 rule one copy must be outside the building; a single fire or flood can destroy the original and the copy together."},{question:"Your office makes backups every week but has never tested whether they can be restored. What should be done?",options:[{text:"Restore a copy for real from time to time and record the result",correct:!0},{text:"Only increase the backups to twice a week",correct:!1},{text:"Nothing is needed; if a backup was made, it works",correct:!1},{text:"Print the backups as well and put them in the archive",correct:!1}],explanation:"A backup that has never been restored may be damaged or incomplete; only a test shows that it will work in an emergency."},{question:"Water from a burst pipe is coming into the archive and getting close to the electrical outlets. What is the first action?",options:[{text:"Stay out of the water, get people out and alert whoever can cut the power",correct:!0},{text:"Walk into the water and lift the Level A boxes off the floor first",correct:!1},{text:"Pull every plug out of the outlets with your wet hands to cut the power",correct:!1},{text:"Quickly photograph the damage from inside the room for the manager's report",correct:!1}],explanation:"Water and electricity together mean a risk of electric shock; lives come before documents. The responsible person cuts the power and water, and then the files are saved."}],steps:[{title:"List and classify the documents",detail:"Make a list of each department's documents and data and mark each one as Level A, B or C. Write down where each one is kept and who is responsible for it."},{title:"Store them in the right place",detail:"Level A documents go in fire-resistant safes; the rest go in locked metal cabinets raised above the floor, away from water pipes, heaters and electrical outlets."},{title:"Copy and test the copy",detail:"Scan the important documents and apply the 3-2-1 rule. From time to time, actually restore a copy to make sure it works; an untested copy may fail in an emergency."},{title:"Protect the archive from fire",detail:"Keep the archive room tidy and closed: no cigarettes, heaters or extension cords inside, and no paper on the floor or in front of doors and exit routes."},{title:"In an emergency: lives first",detail:"When the fire alarm sounds or water comes in, leave the documents and get out. If the safe is within arm's reach and closing it costs no time, close it; otherwise go straight out.",critical:!0},{title:"After the incident: restore the work",detail:"Go back in only after Civil Defense allows it. Restart services from the copies kept off-site, move wet documents to a cool, dry place and ask a specialist for help, and record what was lost."}],warnings:["Never go back into a building with fire or water in it for documents: no paper is worth a life, and that is what copies are for.","Do not keep documents on a basement floor or under water pipes: water from a burst pipe or from firefighting will ruin them.","Do not keep the only copy of important data on one computer or in the same building: one fire destroys the original and the copy together."],tips:["Label boxes and files with their level (A, B, C) so that they can be recognized quickly in an emergency.","Name a records officer in each department who knows the Level A list and where its copies are.","Keep the off-site copy in a place that the same event (fire, flood) would not reach.","Check the ceiling and pipes above the archive after rain or snow and report any leak at once."],activity:{prompt:"In the morning you see that after the night's rain, water is dripping from the archive ceiling onto several boxes of files on the floor. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I move the boxes to a dry, raised place and report the leak to the manager and maintenance",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: you stop the damage early and the cause is fixed so it does not happen again."},{text:"I put a heater next to the boxes so they dry quickly",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: a heater near paper is a fire risk, and heat makes wet paper worse."},{text:"I cover them with plastic and leave them where they are on the floor",correct:!1,feedback:"Not enough: plastic helps for a short time, but the boxes stay on the floor and the leak is not reported."},{text:"I do nothing, because this is the maintenance department's job",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: protecting documents is every employee's responsibility; every hour of delay makes the damage worse."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"You are in the archive room. Several Level A contracts are on your desk and the safe is in the next room. The fire alarm sounds and smoke appears in the corridor. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I leave the contracts, close the door and get out",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: life comes first; closing the door slows fire and smoke, and copies of the contracts should be elsewhere."},{text:"I take the contracts to the next room and lock them in the safe",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: going to another room while there is smoke wastes escape time; the safe only if it is within arm's reach."},{text:"I carry all the Level A boxes out with me",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: heavy boxes slow you down, fill your hands and may block the way for others."},{text:"I phone the manager to ask what to do with the files",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: the plan should have answered this before the incident; now it is time to get out."}]},{prompt:"The fire is out, but the archive is soaked with firefighting water. The manager wants to restart services for citizens tomorrow. What is the best way?",options:[{text:"We restart from the off-site copies and ask a specialist to help with the wet files",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: this is the purpose of the 3-2-1 rule; work continues and the wet files are handled properly."},{text:"We dry the wet files with heaters inside the room so they are ready tomorrow",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: heaters near paper risk a new fire and damage wet paper further."},{text:"We throw away all the wet paper at once and start again",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: many files can be saved, and throwing away official documents may destroy people's rights."},{text:"We go into the archive today before Civil Defense declares the building safe",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: after a fire, ceilings and wiring may be dangerous; going in happens only with permission."}]}],realCase:"One night a water pipe burst in the basement of a government office and the archive filled with water. All the boxes on the floor were soaked, but the files in raised metal cabinets and the contracts in the safe stayed dry. The office had already scanned its important contracts and kept a copy in another building, so services restarted quickly while a specialist helped with the wet files. Afterwards, the archive was moved to a higher floor. Lesson: raise, separate and copy."},{id:3,code:"GOV-103",icon:"Zap",accent:"#ca8a04",title:"Electrical safety",subtitle:"Electrical safety",summary:"Electrical hazards in offices and the inspection of equipment",blocks:[{body:"Electricity is one of the main causes of fires in offices. Old wiring, overloaded outlets and heating appliances must be inspected regularly."},{heading:"1. Electrical hazards",body:"Common hazards in offices:",points:["Overloading: too many devices on a single outlet.","Old cables with cracked or damaged insulation.","Water near electrical devices and outlets.","Extension cords: heavy and permanent use."]},{heading:"2. Safety rules",body:"Daily rules:",points:["Do not connect one extension cord to another extension cord.","Your hands must be dry before touching an outlet.","Plug heaters directly into the wall outlet, not into an extension cord."]},{heading:"3. Electrical fires",body:"If a fire starts in an electrical device:",points:["If you can do so safely, cut the power at the electrical panel and call 115.","If the fire is small and you are trained, use a CO₂ or dry powder (ABC) fire extinguisher, with your exit behind you; otherwise get out.","No water or foam unless the label shows a Class C rating or an electrical-test marking — there is a risk of electric shock."]},{heading:"4. Warning signs and reporting",body:"Report these signs through your office's channel before they turn into a fire:",points:["A warm outlet or plug, or brown marks around the outlet.","Lights that flicker or dim.","Breakers in the panel that trip again and again.","A crackling sound or the smell of burning plastic."]},{heading:"5. If someone gets an electric shock",body:"Help without becoming the second victim:",points:["Do not touch the person; first cut the power at the switch, plug or panel.","Call 122 even if the person seems fine, because an electric shock can affect the heart.","If the person does not respond and is not breathing normally, start CPR and use an AED.","Cool burns at the contact points with cool running water and put nothing else on them."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/gov_electrical.mp4",title:"Electrical safety",description:"Guidance"}],quiz:[{question:"Which fire extinguisher is best for an electrical fire?",options:[{text:"Water",correct:!1},{text:"Foam or a bucket of water",correct:!1},{text:"CO₂ or dry powder",correct:!0},{text:"A blanket or a wet cloth",correct:!1}],explanation:"CO₂ and dry powder do not conduct electricity; ordinary water and foam conduct electricity and can cause electric shock."},{question:"How should an electric heater be plugged in?",options:[{text:"Into an extension cord",correct:!1},{text:"Into one outlet shared with many other devices",correct:!1},{text:"Directly into the wall outlet",correct:!0},{text:"Into two extension cords joined together",correct:!1}],explanation:"Plug heaters directly into the wall outlet, not into an extension cord, and never connect one extension cord to another; overloading is a common hazard in offices."},{question:"A fire starts in an electrical device in your office. What do you do first, if you can do so safely?",options:[{text:"Cut the power at the panel and call 115",correct:!0},{text:"Pour a bucket of water on it to cool it",correct:!1},{text:"Open its cover to see which part is burning",correct:!1},{text:"Spray it with the nearest foam extinguisher",correct:!1}],explanation:"If you can do so safely, cut the power at the electrical panel and call 115; use a CO₂ or dry powder (ABC) extinguisher only if the fire is small, you are trained and your exit is behind you; otherwise get out."},{question:"A colleague has been shocked and his hand is still on the device's cord; he cannot let go. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"Cut the power at the switch or panel before touching him",correct:!0},{text:"Pull his hand away from the cord with force",correct:!1},{text:"Pour water over him to cool his body",correct:!1},{text:"Call 122 and wait without cutting the power",correct:!1}],explanation:"Until the power is cut, anyone who touches him will be shocked too. After cutting it, call 122 and start CPR if needed."},{question:"You notice that the outlet behind your desk is warm and has turned brown. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I stop using it and report it to maintenance",correct:!0},{text:"I move my devices to another outlet and tell nobody",correct:!1},{text:"I open the outlet cover with a screwdriver and check it",correct:!1},{text:"I wrap tape around it and keep using it",correct:!1}],explanation:"Heat and brown marks are signs of a loose connection or overload and can lead to a fire; only a qualified electrician should fix it."},{question:"Which of these office practices is unsafe?",options:[{text:"Running an extension cord under the carpet to the meeting table",correct:!0},{text:"Plugging the heater directly into the wall outlet",correct:!1},{text:"Keeping the panel clear and its switches labeled",correct:!1},{text:"Switching off unneeded devices at the end of the day",correct:!1}],explanation:"A cord under a carpet gets stepped on and its insulation breaks, and the carpet traps heat; this is a common cause of fires. The other three practices are safe."}],steps:[{title:"Look before you plug in",detail:"Check the cord, plug and outlet: cracked insulation, burn marks, heat or a burning smell. Do not use damaged equipment; label it and report it to maintenance."},{title:"Do not overload outlets",detail:"Plug heaters and high-power devices directly into a wall outlet, never connect one extension cord to another, and use extension cords only for short periods."},{title:"Keep water away from electricity",detail:"Do not put tea, water or plant pots on equipment or near outlets, and touch switches and plugs only with dry hands."},{title:"Report the warning signs",detail:"If you smell burning, see sparks or find a warm outlet: stop using it, switch the device off and unplug it if you can do so safely, and report it to maintenance. Do not repair it yourself; only a qualified electrician."},{title:"Electrical fire",detail:'Raise the alarm and call 115. If you can do so safely, cut the power at the panel. Use a CO₂ or dry powder (ABC) extinguisher only if every answer in the check before use is "yes"; otherwise get out and close the door.',critical:!0},{title:"Electric shock",detail:"Do not touch a person who is still in contact with electricity. First cut the power at the switch or panel, then call 122. If the person does not respond and is not breathing normally, start CPR and ask for an AED.",critical:!0}],warnings:["Do not touch a person who has been shocked until the power is cut: the current will pass through you too and you will become a second victim.","Do not pour water or foam on burning electrical equipment: water conducts electricity and there is a risk of electric shock.","Do not open the electrical panel or repair wiring unless you are a qualified electrician: faulty electrical work causes fires and shocks."],tips:["Keep the way to the electrical panel clear and label its switches with the names of rooms and floors.","At the end of the day, switch off and unplug heaters, kettles and devices that are not needed.","Do not run cords under carpets, through door frames or across walkways.","If there are not enough outlets, ask for a qualified electrician to install new ones instead of using permanent extension cords."],activity:{prompt:"In winter, a colleague plugs a heater into an extension cord that already powers a printer and a kettle, and the cord runs under the desk on the carpet. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I politely ask her to plug the heater directly into a wall outlet and I report it to maintenance",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: a heater is a heavy load; overloading the extension cord heats it, and the carpet traps the heat until a fire starts."},{text:"I say nothing, because the cord is not hot yet",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: the fire can start after hours of use, often when nobody is in the room."},{text:"I connect a second extension cord to it to share the load",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: joining two extension cords does not reduce the load; it all still goes through one wall outlet."},{text:"I cover the cord with paper so nobody trips over it",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: paper traps heat and burns easily; this makes the danger worse."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"A burning smell and a little smoke come from an extension cord behind the photocopier, and a small flame appears. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"I raise the alarm, call 115 and cut the power at the panel if it is safe",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the alarm protects everyone, and cutting the power removes the cause of the fire and reduces the risk of shock."},{text:"I pour the water from the kettle onto the cord",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: water on an electrical fire that is still live causes electric shock."},{text:"I pull the cord out of the outlet with my hands, even though they are wet",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: wet hands and a burning cord make shock more likely; cut the power at the panel."},{text:"I open the window and carry on working",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: a small fire grows quickly; fresh air feeds the flames."}]},{prompt:"The power is off and the fire is still small. You are trained and a CO₂ extinguisher is by the door behind you. You use it, but the fire does not go out and nearby paper starts to burn. What do you do now?",options:[{text:"I stop, get out, close the door and make sure 115 has been called",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: if the fire does not go out at once, it is time to leave; a closed door slows fire and smoke."},{text:"I fetch a second extinguisher and go back into the room alone",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: a spreading fire is no longer one for portable extinguishers; smoke can cut off your way out."},{text:"I use a water extinguisher on the paper because the power is off",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: the fire has spread; the goal now is a safe exit, not trying another extinguisher."},{text:"I leave the door open so the smoke can go out into the corridor",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: an open door spreads smoke into the exit routes and feeds the fire with air."}]}],realCase:"In a government office in winter, staff had plugged a heater, a kettle and a printer into one extension cord. An employee smelled burning plastic. He switched the heater off and unplugged it, because it was safe to do so, and reported it to maintenance. A qualified electrician found that the cord's insulation had melted. The manager had an electrician install new outlets and banned connecting extension cords together. No fire started. Lesson: a burning smell is a warning sign, not a small matter."},{id:4,code:"GOV-104",icon:"HeartPulse",accent:"#dc2626",title:"First aid program",subtitle:"First aid program",summary:"Forming a first aid team and preparing supplies",blocks:[{body:"Every office needs a first aid program. It is recommended to have at least one trained person on every floor and on every shift, and more in large offices."},{heading:"1. The first aid team",body:"Team composition:",points:["At least one trained person on every floor and on every shift.","An approved course in first aid, cardiopulmonary resuscitation (CPR) and AED.","Renewal of training and certification every 2 years."]},{heading:"2. The first aid kit",body:"Required supplies:",points:["Bandages and sterile gauze.","Antiseptic wound-cleaning solution.","Scissors and tweezers.","Disposable medical gloves.","A CPR mask for giving breaths safely."]},{heading:"3. Cardiopulmonary resuscitation (CPR)",body:"CPR steps for adults:",points:["1. If the person is unresponsive and not breathing normally: call 122 and ask for an AED; place the person on their back on a firm surface.","2. 30 chest compressions in the center of the chest: depth 5–6 cm, rate 100–120 compressions per minute.","3. 2 breaths (each about 1 second) if you are trained; if not, give chest compressions only.","4. Continue at a ratio of 30:2 until an AED or the ambulance arrives; use the AED immediately."]},{heading:"4. The office first aid plan",body:"The plan must answer these questions:",points:["Who is trained on each floor and on each shift?","Where are the first aid kit and the AED, and who checks them?","Who calls 122, and who waits for the ambulance at the door?","Where are incidents recorded, and who restocks the kit after each one?"]},{heading:"5. Common office emergencies",body:"Correct first aid:",points:["Severe bleeding: press directly on the wound with gloves on and call 122; if the dressing soaks through, put another on top.","Burns (from a kettle or hot water): cool under cool running water for 20 minutes; no ice, butter or toothpaste.","Choking (adults): 5 back blows between the shoulder blades, then 5 abdominal thrusts, and repeat until the object comes out or the ambulance arrives.","Unconscious but breathing normally: if no neck or back injury is suspected, use the recovery position and call 122."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/gov_cpr.mp4",title:"Cardiopulmonary resuscitation (CPR)",description:"How to perform CPR"}],quiz:[{question:"In CPR, how many chest compressions are given before the 2 breaths?",options:[{text:"10",correct:!1},{text:"15",correct:!1},{text:"30",correct:!0},{text:"50",correct:!1}],explanation:"The correct ratio for adults is 30:2 (AHA 2025)."},{question:"A coworker collapses, does not respond and is not breathing normally. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"Give them water to drink",correct:!1},{text:"Put them in the recovery position and wait",correct:!1},{text:"Wait a few minutes to see if they wake up",correct:!1},{text:"Call 122 and ask for an AED",correct:!0}],explanation:"If the person is unresponsive and not breathing normally, call 122 and ask for an AED, place them on their back on a firm surface and start chest compressions."},{question:"You are not trained to give rescue breaths. What do you do during CPR?",options:[{text:"Give breaths only, without compressions",correct:!1},{text:"Give chest compressions only",correct:!0},{text:"Stop CPR until someone trained arrives",correct:!1},{text:"Give 15 compressions, then rest",correct:!1}],explanation:"If you are not trained to give breaths, give chest compressions only, in the center of the chest at 100–120 per minute, and continue until an AED or the ambulance arrives."},{question:"You call 122 for an unconscious colleague. What is the first thing you tell them?",options:[{text:"The exact location: building, floor and nearest entrance",correct:!0},{text:"Your name, your phone number and your department",correct:!1},{text:"Your colleague's age, full name and job title",correct:!1},{text:"His past illnesses and the medicines he takes",correct:!1}],explanation:"If the call drops, the location is enough for the ambulance to come. Then say what happened, how many are hurt and what dangers there are, and do not hang up until you are told to."},{question:"A colleague has burned his hand with water from the kettle. What is the correct first aid?",options:[{text:"Cool it under cool running water for 20 minutes",correct:!0},{text:"Hold ice directly on the burn until the pain eases",correct:!1},{text:"Spread toothpaste or butter over the burn",correct:!1},{text:"Pop the blisters and wrap it tightly in a dry cloth",correct:!1}],explanation:"Cool running water draws the heat out of the skin and limits the damage. Ice harms the skin, butter and toothpaste trap heat, and popping blisters lets germs in."},{question:"A colleague with a severe allergy cannot breathe easily after eating. Her own prescribed adrenaline auto-injector is in her bag. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I help her use her own auto-injector and call 122",correct:!0},{text:"I give her an allergy tablet from the first aid kit",correct:!1},{text:"I give her water and wait to see if she gets better",correct:!1},{text:"I drive her home so she can rest there",correct:!1}],explanation:"A severe allergic reaction can kill quickly. First aid only helps a person use their own prescribed device; it does not give other medicines, and 122 is always called."}],steps:[{title:"Is the scene safe?",detail:"Look before you go closer: electricity, fire, smoke, gas or things that may fall. If the scene is not safe, do not go in; call 115.",critical:!0},{title:"Response and breathing",detail:"Shout to the person and gently tap their shoulders. Check whether they are breathing normally.",critical:!0},{title:"Call 122 and get the AED",detail:"Call 122, or tell a named person to call; give the location first (building, floor, entrance). Send someone else to bring the first aid kit and the AED, and a third person to meet the ambulance at the main entrance.",critical:!0},{title:"Not breathing normally: CPR",detail:"Place the person on their back on a firm surface and start chest compressions. When the AED arrives, switch it on at once and follow its voice prompts.",critical:!0},{title:"Breathing normally",detail:"If there is no suspected injury to the neck, back, hip or pelvis, put the person on their side (recovery position). Give nothing by mouth, stay with them and keep checking their breathing until the ambulance arrives.",critical:!0},{title:"After the incident",detail:"Record the incident (time, what happened, what was done), restock the first aid kit and make the AED ready for use. Briefly talk with the team about what went well and what needs to improve."}],warnings:["Give nothing (water, food, medicine) to a person who is unconscious or not fully alert: they may choke.","Do not move a person injured in a fall or by a blow to the head or back unless the place is dangerous or CPR is needed: moving them can make a spinal injury worse.","Do not give medicines from the kit; only help a person use their own device prescribed by a doctor (such as an adrenaline auto-injector or an inhaler)."],tips:["Post the names and numbers of trained staff and the location of the first aid kit and the AED on every floor.","Keep the AED in a visible, unlocked place that every employee knows and can reach quickly.","Check the first aid kit regularly and after every use, and replace anything that has expired.","Practise the 122 call as if it were real: location first, then what happened and how many people are hurt."],activity:{prompt:"In the meeting room an employee suddenly collapses, does not respond and is not breathing normally. You are trained and the AED is in the corridor. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I tell a named person to call 122 and bring the AED, and I start CPR at once",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: a direct request to a named person gets it done, and early CPR with an AED improves the chance of survival."},{text:"I go to fetch the AED myself and call 122 afterwards",correct:!1,feedback:"Not the best: there are other people in the room; share the tasks so CPR and the call are not delayed."},{text:"I put him in the recovery position and wait for the ambulance",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: the recovery position is only for someone breathing normally; this person needs CPR."},{text:"I splash water on his face to wake him up",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: water does not wake someone whose heart has stopped, and it wastes precious time."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"In the archive a colleague has fallen from a ladder. He is awake and breathing normally but says his back hurts badly, and he is lying on the floor. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I do not move him, I call 122 and stay with him and reassure him",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: after a fall with back pain, moving him can worsen a spinal injury; the place is safe, so you leave him still."},{text:"I help him stand up and sit on a chair",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: standing up can make a back injury worse; wait for the ambulance team."},{text:"I roll him onto his side (recovery position)",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: the recovery position is not used with a suspected back injury, and he is awake and breathing."},{text:"I give him a painkiller from the kit",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: first aid here does not include giving medicines; he may need surgery and doctors decide."}]},{prompt:"Before the ambulance arrives, your colleague suddenly stops responding and is not breathing normally. What do you do now?",options:[{text:"I put him on his back, start CPR and send for the AED",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: CPR is when moving him is allowed; without CPR he will not survive, back injury or not."},{text:"I leave him where he is because his back is injured and wait",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: if he is not breathing normally, waiting ends in death; CPR matters more than protecting the back."},{text:"I put him on his side to keep his airway open",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: someone not breathing normally needs CPR on their back, not the recovery position."},{text:"I splash cold water on his face to wake him",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: water does not restart the heart; every minute without CPR lowers the chance of survival."}]}],realCase:"In a government office an employee collapsed in the corridor and was not breathing normally. A trained colleague shouted for help. One person called 122 and gave the location first: the building, the floor and the nearest entrance. A second brought the AED from its visible place that everyone knew. A third colleague waited for the ambulance at the main entrance and showed the crew the way. CPR and the AED continued until the ambulance team took over. Lesson: knowing where the AED is and sharing the tasks saves precious time."},{id:5,code:"GOV-105",icon:"Flame",accent:"#ea580c",title:"Fire and fire extinguishers in offices",subtitle:"Fire prevention and extinguisher use",summary:"Preventing fires and using fire extinguishers in government buildings",blocks:[{body:"Fires in offices are a serious hazard. Paper, computers, old wiring and electrical overloading are the main causes of fire. A prevention and preparedness program protects the lives of staff and public property."},{heading:"1. Causes of fire in offices",body:"Main causes:",points:["Electrical overloading: too many devices on a single outlet.","Paper and documents: materials that burn easily.","Cigarettes and matches: a major cause of fires breaking out.","Stoves and portable heaters: a winter hazard.","Small kitchens: while making tea and coffee.","Batteries and chargers: overheating."]},{heading:"2. Types of fire extinguishers",body:"Offices need these types:",points:["Dry powder (ABC) fire extinguisher: for Class A, B and C fires — not for metals (D) and not for cooking oil (K).","CO₂ fire extinguisher: for fires in electrical equipment — does not damage computers.","Water fire extinguisher: for Class A only (paper, wood, cloth) — not for electricity or oil.","The UK code BS 5306-8 recommends at least two fire extinguishers on each floor (very small floors excepted); the rule for offices in Duhok is set by the Civil Defense Directorate and is not yet confirmed.","Place fire extinguishers near doors and along evacuation routes.","Monthly visual check (pin and seal intact, label readable, pressure gauge in the green if there is one; CO₂ extinguishers have no gauge, so lift one to feel that it is full) and yearly inspection and maintenance by a qualified person (NFPA 10)."]},{heading:"3. The PASS technique",body:`Before you use an extinguisher (every answer must be yes):
• The fire is small and just starting, like a fire in a wastebasket or a pan.
• The extinguisher is the right type for this fire.
• Your exit is behind you and the fire is not between you and the exit.
• The alarm has been raised: others are warned and 115 has been called.
• You are trained to use the extinguisher.
• No smoke or heat is blocking your way out.
• If the fire does not go out at once, stop, get out and close the door behind you.

PASS is the general method for most hand-held extinguishers. Class D, wet chemical, clean agent and wheeled units: follow the label and the manufacturer's instructions. How to use a fire extinguisher:`,points:["P = Pull: pull out the safety pin.","A = Aim: aim the nozzle at the base of the fire.","S = Squeeze: squeeze the extinguisher handle firmly.","S = Sweep: sweep from side to side at the base of the fire.","Distance: start from the distance shown on the extinguisher label or in the manufacturer's instructions, with your exit behind you, and move closer only as the fire gets smaller.","Keep your exit behind you; if the fire does not go out at once or starts to spread, get out, close the door behind you and make sure 115 has been called."]},{heading:"4. Alarm systems",body:"Systems required in offices:",points:["Fire siren: must be audible on every floor.","Manual alarm button (Pull Station): near the exits.","Smoke detection: as required by the building's fire code (the requirement for Duhok offices is not yet confirmed).","Emergency lighting: switches on automatically when the power goes out.","Post the number 115 on the wall of every room."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/gov_fire_prevention.mp4",title:"Fire prevention",description:"Complete guidance for offices"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/gov_pass_method.png",title:"The PASS technique",description:"Step by step"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/gov_extinguisher_types.png",title:"Types of fire extinguishers",description:"ABC, CO₂, water"}],quiz:[{question:"Which fire extinguisher is best for a computer fire?",options:[{text:"Water fire extinguisher",correct:!1},{text:"CO₂ fire extinguisher",correct:!0},{text:"Foam fire extinguisher",correct:!1},{text:"A blanket",correct:!1}],explanation:"CO₂ does not damage computers and does not conduct electricity."},{question:"In the PASS technique, what does A stand for?",options:[{text:"Alert",correct:!1},{text:"Aim",correct:!0},{text:"Action",correct:!1},{text:"Away",correct:!1}],explanation:"A = Aim: aim the nozzle at the base of the fire."},{question:"The UK code BS 5306-8 recommends at least how many fire extinguishers on each floor?",options:[{text:"1 fire extinguisher",correct:!1},{text:"2 fire extinguishers",correct:!0},{text:"3 fire extinguishers",correct:!1},{text:"5 fire extinguishers",correct:!1}],explanation:"The UK code BS 5306-8 recommends at least two fire extinguishers on each floor (very small floors excepted), and the travel distance to reach them must also stay within the limit. The rule for offices in Duhok is set by the Civil Defense Directorate and is not yet confirmed."},{question:"You are trained and holding the right extinguisher, but smoke has started to fill the corridor that is your way out. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I leave at once, because my way out is in danger",correct:!0},{text:"I use the extinguisher quickly before the smoke gets worse",correct:!1},{text:"I stay low and remain in the room until I put the fire out",correct:!1},{text:"I ask a colleague to open the window so the smoke can escape",correct:!1}],explanation:'In the go/no-go check, smoke or heat blocking your way out is a "no"; then you do not use the extinguisher and you get out.'},{question:"You put out a small fire with an extinguisher and half of it is left. What do you do with it afterwards?",options:[{text:"I report it so that it is recharged or replaced",correct:!0},{text:"I hang it back in place because it is still half full",correct:!1},{text:"I keep it by my desk in case of another fire",correct:!1},{text:"I put the safety pin back and keep using it as it is",correct:!1}],explanation:"A used extinguisher may have lost pressure and run empty quickly at the next fire; it must be recharged after any use."},{question:"You discover a fire in the office. Which action comes before any attempt to put it out?",options:[{text:"Raising the alarm and making sure 115 has been called",correct:!0},{text:"Opening the windows to let the smoke out of the room",correct:!1},{text:"Collecting the important documents from nearby desks",correct:!1},{text:"Phoning the manager to ask for permission",correct:!1}],explanation:"If the attempt fails, others must already be warned and 115 must be on the way; that is why the alarm always comes first."}],steps:[{title:"Raise the alarm first",detail:'Shout "Fire!", use the manual alarm button and call 115, or make sure a named person has called. This comes before any attempt to fight the fire.',critical:!0},{title:"The go/no-go check",detail:'Is the fire small and just starting? Is the extinguisher the right type? Is your exit behind you? Has the alarm been raised? Are you trained? Is your way out free of smoke and heat? If any answer is "no", get out.',critical:!0},{title:"PASS safely",detail:"Start from the distance on the label, with your exit behind you. Pull the safety pin, aim the nozzle at the base of the fire, squeeze the handle and sweep from side to side. Move closer only as the fire gets smaller."},{title:"If it does not go out at once, leave",detail:"If the fire does not go out at once, spreads or the smoke increases: stop, get out and close the door behind you. At the assembly point, tell the 115 team where the fire is.",critical:!0},{title:"After use",detail:"Report every extinguisher that has been used, even a little, so that it is recharged or replaced. Do not hang it back in its place."}],warnings:["Do not use water or a water extinguisher on an electrical fire or a cooking oil fire: water conducts electricity and makes burning oil burst and spread.","Never let the fire get between you and your way out: if the extinguisher runs empty or the fire grows, you will be trapped.","Do not block extinguishers or alarm buttons with desks, boxes or furniture: every second counts in an emergency."],tips:["Know where the two nearest extinguishers and the nearest alarm button are on your floor.","In the small kitchen, do not walk away from anything on the heat, and switch off kettles and heaters at the end of the day.","Keep heaters away from curtains, paper and desks, and never dry clothes on them.","If the monthly check shows a missing pin or seal, or the pressure gauge outside the green, report it at once."],activity:{prompt:"In the office's small kitchen, oil in a pan catches fire while a colleague is cooking. The nearest extinguisher is a water one and the pan's lid is on the counter. What do you do?",options:[{text:"If it is safe, I turn off the heat and slide the lid over the pan; if the fire does not go out, I leave and call 115",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the lid cuts off the air and turning off the heat stops feeding the fire; do not move the pan."},{text:"I use the water extinguisher on the pan",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: water on burning oil makes it burst and throws flaming oil around the kitchen."},{text:"I pick up the pan and carry it outside quickly",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: hot oil can spill, burn you and others and spread the fire along the way."},{text:"I throw a cloth soaked in water over the pan",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: a wet cloth adds water to the oil and can spread the fire; use a metal lid or a fire blanket."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"A small fire starts in a wastebasket in the meeting room. You are trained and a dry powder (ABC) extinguisher is by the door. Nobody has raised the alarm yet. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I shout a warning and tell someone to call 115, then use the extinguisher after the go/no-go check",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the alarm comes before fighting the fire, so everyone is ready if the attempt fails."},{text:"I quickly spray the top of the flames without warning anyone",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: aim at the base, not the top; and without an alarm nobody is ready if the fire does not go out."},{text:"I close the door and go back to my desk",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: closing the door is good, but without the alarm and 115 the fire grows and nobody knows."},{text:"I pour my tea over the wastebasket",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: a cup of tea will not put out a paper fire and time is lost."}]},{prompt:"You started with PASS. The fire got smaller but suddenly spread to the curtain and the smoke is increasing. The extinguisher is almost empty. What do you do now?",options:[{text:"I stop, get out, close the door and report to the 115 team at the assembly point",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: a spreading fire is no longer small; a closed door slows fire and smoke and gives people time to get out."},{text:"I fetch another extinguisher from another floor and go back in alone",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: meanwhile the fire and smoke grow and may cut off your way out."},{text:"I open the window to let the smoke out and keep going",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: fresh air feeds the fire and makes it spread faster."},{text:"I stay in the room and wait for the 115 team",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: staying in a room where the fire is spreading can end with passing out from the smoke."}]}],realCase:'In a government office, a heater was left on near a curtain and the curtain caught fire. An employee shouted "Fire!" and a colleague pressed the alarm button and called 115. A trained employee went through the go/no-go check: the fire was small, an ABC extinguisher was at hand and the door was behind him. He put the fire out and the 115 team checked the room. The used extinguisher was replaced the same day. Lesson: the alarm comes before fighting the fire, and a used extinguisher is replaced at once.'},{id:6,code:"GOV-106",icon:"Activity",accent:"#d97706",title:"Earthquakes in government buildings",subtitle:"Earthquake safety in government buildings",summary:"How to protect yourself and evacuate safely during an earthquake",blocks:[{body:"The Kurdistan Region lies in a seismically active zone. Government buildings, especially older ones, are at risk. Preparedness and knowledge of the procedures save lives."},{heading:"1. During an earthquake",body:"If you are inside the office:",points:["Drop: immediately drop onto your hands and knees.","Cover: get under a sturdy table, away from windows and heavy cabinets (not in a doorway), and protect your head and neck.","Hold On: hold on to the table leg until the shaking stops.","Do not run outside — many injuries happen while running and from falling debris.","Do not use the elevator — it may stop and trap you inside.","If you are in an open area: stay away from buildings, power lines and poles."]},{heading:"2. After an earthquake",body:"Steps after an earthquake:",points:["Check yourself and your colleagues for injuries.","Exit by the stairs (not the elevator!)","If you smell gas or hear gas escaping: get out and call 115 from outside; only trained, designated staff shut off the gas at the main valve, and only if they can reach it safely, without entering the area where the gas has collected and without operating any switch.","Go to the assembly point and count people.","If there are injured people or people trapped under rubble, call 115 and 122.","Be alert for aftershocks.","Do not go back into the building until experts have confirmed that it is safe."]},{heading:"3. Advance preparedness",body:"Required preparations:",points:["Prepare an earthquake plan and post it on the wall.","Secure cabinets, shelves and heavy equipment to the wall.","Prepare an emergency kit on every floor.","Annual inspection of the building by a qualified engineer.","Drop, Cover, Hold On drill every 3 months.","Designate two assembly points (one near and one far)."]},{heading:"4. Responsibilities of managers",body:"Managers must:",points:["Designate an earthquake warden for every floor.","Keep a staff list ready for headcounts.","Establish a special plan for people with special needs.","Inspect the alarm system regularly.","Post the emergency numbers (115, 122, 104) in every room."]},{heading:"5. If you are trapped under debris",body:"Save your strength and signal for help in these ways:",points:["Cover your mouth and nose with a cloth or your clothing so you do not breathe in dust.","Tap on a pipe or wall or use a whistle so that rescuers can find you.","Shout only as a last resort: shouting draws dust into your lungs and uses up your strength.","Do not light a match or any flame, because there may be gas nearby; use your phone's light."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/gov_earthquake.mp4",title:"Earthquakes in the office",description:"Practical drill"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/gov_drop_cover_hold.png",title:"Drop, Cover, Hold On",description:"Step by step"}],quiz:[{question:"During an earthquake, what is the first thing to do?",options:[{text:"Run outside",correct:!1},{text:"Get into the elevator",correct:!1},{text:"Drop, Cover, Hold On",correct:!0},{text:"Open the windows",correct:!1}],explanation:"Drop under a sturdy table, cover yourself and hold on until the shaking stops."},{question:"After an earthquake, what do we use to exit the building?",options:[{text:"The elevator",correct:!1},{text:"The building's stairs",correct:!0},{text:"The windows",correct:!1},{text:"It makes no difference",correct:!1}],explanation:"Always exit by the stairs — the elevator may stop."},{question:"According to this program, how often is an earthquake drill required in the office?",options:[{text:"Every year",correct:!1},{text:"Every 6 months",correct:!1},{text:"Every 3 months",correct:!0},{text:"Only once",correct:!1}],explanation:"This program recommends conducting a practical drill once every 3 months."},{question:"An earthquake starts while you are in a corridor with no tables or chairs. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I crouch by an inside wall and protect my head and neck with my arms",correct:!0},{text:"I run as fast as I can to the nearest exit door",correct:!1},{text:"I stand by a window to see what is happening outside",correct:!1},{text:"I get into the elevator to reach the ground floor fast",correct:!1}],explanation:"If there is no table, crouch by an inside wall away from windows and protect your head and neck; running, windows and elevators are dangerous."},{question:"After the earthquake you smell gas in the corridor, and the main valve is at the end of the corridor where the smell is strongest. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I do not go near the valve; I get out, warn others and call 115 from outside",correct:!0},{text:"I hurry to the end of the corridor and shut the valve before anything else",correct:!1},{text:"I call 115 on my phone from inside the corridor so that no time is lost",correct:!1},{text:"I turn on the corridor lights to see clearly where the gas is leaking",correct:!1}],explanation:"The valve is closed only if it is close and can be reached without entering the area where gas has collected and without any switch. Here the valve is where the gas is, so get out and call from outside."},{question:"A colleague who uses a wheelchair is at her desk when an earthquake starts. What should she do?",options:[{text:"Lock the wheels, bend forward and protect her head and neck",correct:!0},{text:"Wheel herself quickly to the elevator",correct:!1},{text:"Get out of the chair onto the floor under a desk",correct:!1},{text:"Move to the doorway of the room and stay there",correct:!1}],explanation:"A wheelchair user locks the wheels so the chair does not roll, bends forward and protects the head and neck; moving during the shaking, doorways and elevators are dangerous."}],steps:[{title:"Drop, Cover, Hold On",detail:"When the shaking starts, drop where you are onto your hands and knees, get under a sturdy table away from windows and heavy cabinets, and hold on to a table leg until the shaking stops. If there is no table, crouch next to an inside wall and protect your head and neck with your arms.",critical:!0},{title:"Stay put until the shaking stops",detail:"Do not run outside during the shaking and do not use the elevator. If you use a wheelchair, lock the wheels, bend forward and protect your head and neck.",critical:!0},{title:"After the shaking: look and check",detail:"Check yourself and your colleagues for injuries. If you smell gas or hear it escaping: do not use any switch, light or phone inside; get out and call 115 from outside.",critical:!0},{title:"Leave carefully by the stairs",detail:"Leave by the stairs, watching for debris, broken glass and fallen wires, and once outside stay away from walls, windows and power lines. Expect aftershocks."},{title:"Count and report",detail:"Count people at the assembly point. Give the 115 team the name and last known location of anyone missing or trapped, and call 122 if there are injured people. Nobody goes back in to search.",critical:!0},{title:"Return after inspection",detail:"The building stays empty until the designated experts confirm it is safe, even if no damage can be seen from outside."}],warnings:['Do not stand in a doorway or rely on the "triangle of life": a doorway does not protect you from falling objects; Drop, Cover, Hold On.',"If there is a smell of gas after the earthquake, do not strike a match or use lights or switches, and do not go into the area where the gas has collected to close the valve: one spark can cause an explosion.","Do not go back into the building after the earthquake for documents or belongings: aftershocks can bring down a weakened building."],tips:["Keep files and heavy items on low shelves, and do not hang anything heavy above desks.","Keep a flashlight, a whistle and sturdy shoes under your desk.","In drills, treat one of the two assembly points as closed so staff learn the way to the other.","Plan in advance with staff who have special needs: who will help them and by which route."],activity:{prompt:"You are at the service counter and several citizens are standing by large glass windows. An earthquake starts. What do you do?",options:[{text:'I call out "Drop, Cover, Hold On, away from the windows" and take cover under the desk myself',correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: visitors need a short, clear instruction; glass breaks and causes serious injuries."},{text:"I rush the citizens toward the main door and outside",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: many injuries happen while running and from glass and debris falling near entrances."},{text:"I stand in the doorway because it is the strongest place",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: in modern buildings a doorway is no stronger than other places and does not protect you from falling objects."},{text:"I take the citizens to the elevator so they can get down quickly",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: the elevator can stop during an earthquake and trap people inside."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"The shaking has stopped. Ceiling tiles have fallen and there is dust everywhere. A colleague has a small cut on his hand, and there is a smell of gas from the floor's kitchen. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I get everyone out by the stairs, with no switches or phones used inside, and call 115 from outside",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: a gas smell after an earthquake means a risk of explosion; the small cut can be treated outside."},{text:"I go into the kitchen to shut the gas valve",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: the valve is closed only if it is close and can be reached without entering the area where gas has collected; here the gas is in the kitchen."},{text:"I switch on the lights to see the damage better",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: a switch can make a spark that ignites the gas."},{text:"We stay at our desks because aftershocks may come",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: once the shaking stops you should leave carefully, especially when there is a smell of gas."}]},{prompt:"Everyone is at the near assembly point. A large crack appears in the building wall above it, and the manager wants staff to go back in for important files. What do you propose?",options:[{text:"We move to the far assembly point, count everyone and nobody goes in until experts confirm the building",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: this is why there is a far assembly point; a cracked wall can fall in an aftershock."},{text:"Only two volunteers go in quickly and bring the files out",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: volunteering does not protect a life; files can be restored from copies, lives cannot."},{text:"We go into the ground floor only, between aftershocks",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: nobody knows when the next aftershock will come, and the ground floor can collapse too."},{text:"We stay at the near point and wait, because it is the planned one",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: an assembly point must be safe; when a wall above it cracks, everyone moves to the far point."}]}],realCase:'During working hours, a moderate earthquake shook an old government building. Staff had practised "Drop, Cover, Hold On" several times and took cover under their desks at once. Cabinets fixed to the walls stayed in place, but one cabinet that was not fixed fell onto an empty chair. After the shaking, the floor wardens led people out by the stairs. An employee smelled gas; nobody used a switch and 115 was called from outside. The building was closed until engineers inspected it. Lesson: fixing cabinets and practising save lives.'},{id:7,code:"GOV-107",icon:"Wind",accent:"#475569",title:"Gas safety and ventilation",subtitle:"Gas safety and ventilation",summary:"Gas hazards in offices and the importance of proper ventilation",blocks:[{body:"In winter, gas and kerosene stoves and heaters are widely used in offices. A gas leak can cause an explosion and fire, and incomplete combustion produces the toxic gas carbon monoxide (CO), which is colorless and odorless and can kill. Proper ventilation and recognizing the hazards save lives."},{heading:"1. The danger of carbon monoxide (CO)",body:"CO is a silent killer:",points:["CO is colorless, odorless and tasteless — it cannot be detected by the senses.","Symptoms: headache, dizziness, nausea and vomiting, confusion; then loss of consciousness and death.","Sources: gas and kerosene stoves in rooms without ventilation.","Winter is the most dangerous season, because doors and windows are kept closed.","The OSHA limit for workers is 50 ppm (8-hour average), but in workrooms CO should be close to zero; any level of CO is a sign of inadequate ventilation."]},{heading:"2. Rules for proper ventilation",body:"Ventilation required in offices:",points:["When a flueless stove is running, keep a window slightly open to let in fresh air.","Rooms with a chimney (flued) stove: do not run an extractor fan at the same time unless a qualified technician has checked that the stove still vents safely; the fan can pull smoke and CO back into the room, even when the fan is in another room.","The chimney: must be intact and open, not blocked.","Do not block corridors and evacuation routes with stoves.","Turn off flueless stoves before sleeping and when you leave the room, and air out the room from time to time.","Kitchens and restrooms must have dedicated ventilation."]},{heading:"3. Gas leak — what should I do?",body:"If you smell gas:",points:["Open the windows and doors on your way out, if you can.","Do not light any matches and do not touch electrical switches (neither on nor off)!","Do not use a phone inside the building — a spark can cause an explosion.","Get out and get everyone else out.","Call 115 from outside the building.","Only trained, designated staff shut off the gas at the main valve, and only if they can reach it safely without entering the area where the gas has collected; never go back inside to reach it."]},{heading:"4. Monthly inspection",body:"Inspection:",points:["Check the stove's flue pipe — it must not be blocked.","Check gas pipe connections with soap and water (bubble test); never test with a flame.","Install a CO detector (Carbon Monoxide Detector).","A qualified technician should inspect the stoves every year.","Keep a record of inspections."]},{heading:"5. If the CO alarm sounds or symptoms appear",body:"CO has no smell, so the alarm or the symptoms are enough to act on:",points:["Get everyone out into fresh air at once; do not wait for a smell.","Call 115 from outside, and also 122 if anyone has a headache, dizziness, vomiting or confusion.","Give nothing by mouth to anyone unconscious or confused; if they are not breathing normally, start CPR.","Nobody goes back in until the place is cleared and a qualified technician has checked the heaters."]},{heading:"6. Who does what?",body:"Responsibilities must be clear before an incident:",points:["All staff: no switches or flames, get out, warn others, 115 from outside.","Designated staff: the main valve only if they can reach it safely from outside the gas area.","Floor warden: count people at the assembly point and brief the 115 team.","Manager: have a qualified technician check heaters and hoses and keep the inspection records."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/gov_gas_safety.mp4",title:"Gas hazards",description:"How to recognize them and what to do"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/gov_co_detector.png",title:"CO detector",description:"Where to install it"}],quiz:[{question:"What property does CO (carbon monoxide) have?",options:[{text:"It has a strong smell like rotten eggs",correct:!1},{text:"It can be seen as a gray-blue smoke",correct:!1},{text:"It is colorless and odorless",correct:!0},{text:"It has no properties",correct:!1}],explanation:"CO is colorless, odorless and tasteless — that is why it is dangerous and a detector is needed."},{question:"If you smell gas, what is the first thing to do?",options:[{text:"Switch on the light to see where the gas is coming from",correct:!1},{text:"Get out, opening the windows on your way",correct:!0},{text:"Call 115 on your phone while you are still inside",correct:!1},{text:"Light the stove",correct:!1}],explanation:"Do not touch flames or switches, open the windows on your way out if you can, get out and call 115 from outside."},{question:"When should a flueless gas stove be turned off?",options:[{text:"Only when the gas cylinder runs out completely",correct:!1},{text:"Before sleeping and when you leave the room",correct:!0},{text:"Only in summer",correct:!1},{text:"Only when the room becomes too warm",correct:!1}],explanation:"Never leave a flueless stove on while sleeping or when no one is in the room; air out the room from time to time."},{question:"You smell gas and the main valve is at the end of the corridor, where the smell is strongest. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I stay away from it; I get out, warn others and call 115 from outside",correct:!0},{text:"I hold my breath and run to the end of the corridor to close the main valve myself",correct:!1},{text:"I go with my phone's flashlight to see the valve clearly in the dark and close it",correct:!1},{text:"I press the electric fire alarm first, then go along and close the main valve",correct:!1}],explanation:"The valve is closed only if it is close and can be reached without entering the gas area and without switches. Here it is inside the gas area; a phone or an electric alarm can also make a spark."},{question:"The CO detector in your room goes off, but nobody smells anything and everyone feels fine. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Everyone goes out into fresh air and we call 115 from outside",correct:!0},{text:"I take the battery out of the detector because it is obviously wrong",correct:!1},{text:"I open the window and everyone stays where they are",correct:!1},{text:"We wait until one of us gets a headache",correct:!1}],explanation:"CO is colorless, odorless and tasteless; the detector's alarm is the only way to know. Everyone leaves and nobody returns until the place is cleared."},{question:"During the monthly check, a colleague wants to test the cylinder hose joint with a lighter to see whether gas is escaping. What do you tell him?",options:[{text:"Stop; put soapy water on the joint and watch for bubbles",correct:!0},{text:"Fine, but do it slowly and keep away from the window and curtains",correct:!1},{text:"Fine, as long as the cylinder is not full and the valve is half closed",correct:!1},{text:"Use a match instead of the lighter because its flame is smaller",correct:!1}],explanation:"Gas joints are tested with soap and water (the bubble test), never with a flame; a flame can ignite escaping gas. If bubbles appear, close the cylinder if it is safe and call a technician."}],steps:[{title:"No sparks",detail:"If you smell gas: do not strike a match or light any flame, do not touch switches, lights or doorbells (neither on nor off), and do not use phones or electrical devices inside the building.",critical:!0},{title:"The valve only if it is safe",detail:"Close the gas valve only if it is close and you can reach it without going into the area where the gas has collected and without touching any switch or spark source. Otherwise, do not go near it. Only trained, designated staff close the building's main valve.",critical:!0},{title:"Get out and warn others",detail:"Get out and warn the people nearby by voice so that they leave, not with an electric bell. If it is easy, open windows and doors on your way, but do not stay behind to do it.",critical:!0},{title:"Call 115 from outside",detail:"Call 115 away from the building: the location, where the smell is, whether anyone is still inside and whether the valve has been closed.",critical:!0},{title:"Stay outside",detail:"Nobody goes back in, not for the valve and not for belongings, until the 115 team says the building is safe. After that, only a qualified technician turns the gas system back on."}],warnings:["Never look for a gas leak with a match or lighter: a flame can make the gas explode at once.","Do not go back into the building to close the valve: gas builds up over time and any spark can cause an explosion.","Do not run a flueless gas heater in a closed room or while sleeping: CO has no smell and can kill without warning."],tips:["Mark the location of the main gas valve, keep the way to it clear and post the names of the designated staff nearby.","Put a CO detector in every room with a fuel-burning stove or heater and test it regularly.","Check gas hoses: if they are cracked, hard or old, have a qualified technician replace them.","In drills, check that staff know what not to do when they smell gas: no switches, phones or bells."],activity:{prompt:"When you arrive at the office in the morning, you smell gas strongly in the corridor near the kitchen. The stove in the kitchen is connected to a cylinder by a hose, and the light switch is by the door. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I leave the switch alone, get out, warn colleagues arriving and call 115 from outside",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the smell is strong, so gas has collected in the kitchen and corridor; the cylinder is inside the gas area and you must not go near it."},{text:"I go into the kitchen and close the cylinder valve",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: the valve is in the area where gas has collected; by the rule you do not approach it, you get out."},{text:"I switch on the corridor lights to see where the leak is",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: a switch makes a small spark that can ignite the gas."},{text:"I call 115 on my phone from the corridor so they know exactly where it is",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: a phone inside the gas area can make a spark; you can describe the place exactly from outside too."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"On a winter afternoon, two employees in a closed room with a flueless gas heater running say they have a headache and feel dizzy; one of them is confused and not answering properly. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I get everyone out into fresh air at once and call 122 and 115 from outside",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: headache, dizziness and confusion in a closed room with a heater are signs of CO; fresh air and medical help come first."},{text:"I open a window and tell them to carry on working",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: the confused person needs medical help, and CO does not leave the body quickly."},{text:"I give them headache tablets and water",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: this is poisoning, not an ordinary headache; tablets do not remove the danger, and a confused person gets nothing by mouth."},{text:"I tell them to rest on chairs in the same room",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: staying in the room with CO makes the poisoning worse and can kill."}]},{prompt:'Outside, the confused employee feels a little better. The manager says: "Once the room is aired out we can go back and light the heater again." What is the best decision?',options:[{text:"The heater stays off until a qualified technician checks it and the ventilation, and those with symptoms are checked by the medical team",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the cause of the CO has not been fixed; feeling better outside is not a sign that it is safe."},{text:"We go back after the windows have been open for a short time",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: a short airing does not fix the cause; once the heater runs again, the CO comes back."},{text:"We light the heater with the door open and carry on working",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: an open door does not make it safe; a faulty heater can still produce dangerous CO."},{text:"We move the heater to another, smaller room",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: CO builds up faster in a smaller room; the danger grows."}]}],realCase:"In winter, a flueless gas heater was running in a closed-window room of a government office. After some hours, several employees felt a headache and dizziness. An employee who had been trained about CO suspected it. Everyone went out into fresh air, and 122 and 115 were called from outside. The ambulance team checked the staff. A qualified technician found that the ventilation was not enough. The office installed CO detectors and applied the ventilation rule. Lesson: the signs of CO look like an ordinary headache."},{id:8,code:"GOV-108",icon:"Lock",accent:"#0891b2",title:"Cybersecurity and data protection",subtitle:"Cybersecurity and data protection",summary:"Protecting government computer systems and data from cyberattacks and viruses",blocks:[{body:"In the digital age, cyberattacks are a major threat to government offices. Citizens' personal data, official documents and financial systems are all targets. Preparedness and staff training are essential."},{heading:"1. Types of cyber threats",body:"Main threats:",points:["Phishing: fake emails and messages that try to steal information.","Viruses and malicious software (Malware).","Ransomware: locking data and demanding money.","Social engineering: deceiving staff to obtain information.","Unsecured WiFi networks: theft of information over the network.","Unknown USB flash drives: can transmit viruses."]},{heading:"2. Password rules",body:"A strong password:",points:["Length: at least 15 characters (NIST SP 800-63B-4).","A long phrase made of several words (Passphrase) is easy to remember and strong.","Do not change your password on a fixed schedule; change it only when there is suspicion that it has been exposed.","Do not use names, phone numbers or 123456.","Enable two-factor authentication (2FA).","Use a different password for each system."]},{heading:"3. Safe email",body:"How to protect yourself on email:",points:["Do not open unknown links — check the sender's address.","Do not open attachments from unknown addresses.","If a suspicious email arrives, notify the IT department.","Do not send passwords or bank account numbers by email.","Use only official email for office business."]},{heading:"4. Physical security",body:"Protecting devices:",points:["Lock your computer when you step away from your desk (Win+L).","Do not let others view information on your screen.","Do not insert unknown USB flash drives into a computer.","Destroy confidential papers with a shredder.","Protect your work phone with a PIN or fingerprint."]},{heading:"5. Signs of phishing",body:"A message is suspicious if:",points:['It pushes you to hurry or threatens you: "today", "immediately", "your account will be closed".',"The sender's address looks like the real one, but a letter or the domain is different.","It asks for a password, a verification code or bank details.","It contains an unexpected link or attachment, or asks for gift cards or a money transfer."]},{heading:"6. Reporting a cyber incident",body:"An early, clear report shortens the time IT needs:",points:["What happened and when: the message, the link, the file or the password you typed.","Which computer or account was involved.","Do not delete the evidence: leave the message where it is until IT tells you what to do.","Follow IT's instructions, for example disconnecting the device from the network if they say so."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/gov_cybersecurity.mp4",title:"Cybersecurity",description:"Principles of protection"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/gov_phishing.png",title:"Recognizing phishing",description:"Examples of fake emails"}],quiz:[{question:"What is phishing?",options:[{text:"Software that protects the computer from viruses",correct:!1},{text:"Fake emails used to steal information",correct:!0},{text:"A type of fire",correct:!1},{text:"A program that makes automatic backup copies",correct:!1}],explanation:"Phishing is fake emails that appear to be official in order to steal information."},{question:"At least how many characters should a strong password have?",options:[{text:"10",correct:!1},{text:"12",correct:!1},{text:"8",correct:!1},{text:"15",correct:!0}],explanation:"NIST (SP 800-63B-4) requires at least 15 characters for a password used without 2FA; length matters more than mixing character types."},{question:"If you find an unknown USB flash drive on the floor, what should you do?",options:[{text:"Insert it into a computer",correct:!1},{text:"Give it to the IT officer",correct:!0},{text:"Take it home",correct:!1},{text:"It makes no difference",correct:!1}],explanation:"Unknown flash drives may contain viruses — give it to the IT department."},{question:"Someone calls, says they are from IT and asks for the verification code that has just arrived on your phone. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I do not give the code; I hang up and report it through the official channel",correct:!0},{text:"I give the code if he knows my full name and my department",correct:!1},{text:"I give him only half of the code to check whether he is genuine",correct:!1},{text:"I send him the code by email instead of saying it on the phone",correct:!1}],explanation:"The verification code is for you alone; whoever has it can get into your account. IT never asks for it, and knowing your name does not prove anything."},{question:`An urgent email in the director's name says: "I am in a meeting; buy gift cards today and send me their codes." What do you do?`,options:[{text:"I check with the director another way: a call to the known number or in person",correct:!0},{text:"I buy them quickly because the director is in a meeting and cannot answer",correct:!1},{text:"I reply to the same email and ask exactly how many cards and of what value",correct:!1},{text:"I buy just one card so that the office's loss is small if it is a scam",correct:!1}],explanation:"Urgency, secrecy and a request for gift cards are signs of a scam. Always check through another channel you already know, and report it to IT."},{question:"You clicked a link and then realized it might be fake. Nothing bad has shown up yet. What is the best action?",options:[{text:"I tell IT straight away what happened",correct:!0},{text:"I wait until a problem appears and report it then",correct:!1},{text:"I download a cleaning program from the internet and fix it myself",correct:!1},{text:"I clear the browsing history so that no trace is left",correct:!1}],explanation:"Much malicious software works silently; an early report lets IT stop it before it spreads. Downloading programs from the internet and erasing traces make the problem worse."}],steps:[{title:"Stop and check",detail:"Before you click on any unexpected message, check the sender's address, the link (hover over it before clicking) and any pressure to hurry or any threat."},{title:"Do not open or reply",detail:"If in doubt, do not open links or attachments and do not reply. If the message seems to come from someone you know, check with them on a number you already know or in person, not the number in the message."},{title:"Report it to IT",detail:"Report the suspicious message through the channel your IT department has set, then delete it as they instruct. Do not forward it to colleagues, because that spreads the link."},{title:"If you clicked: tell IT at once",detail:"If you clicked a link, opened a file or typed your password, tell IT at once and change your password as they direct. Do not try to fix it yourself and do not hide it; every hour makes the damage worse."},{title:"Protect devices and data",detail:"Lock your computer when you leave your desk, do not plug in unknown USB drives, allow the updates IT sends, and use only official email for office business."}],warnings:["Never give your password or verification code (2FA) to anyone, even someone who says they are from IT: IT never asks for your password.","Do not plug a USB drive found on the floor or in the street into a computer: it may have been left on purpose to spread malicious software.","Do not hide a mistake (such as clicking a fake link): reporting early can stop an attack before it reaches the whole network."],tips:["Confirm any request to change bank details or transfer money by calling a number you already know.","Before clicking, hover over the link and see where the real address goes.","Use a long passphrase and a different one for each system; if IT provides an approved password manager, use it.","In public places, do not connect to office systems over unsecured WiFi networks."],activity:{prompt:`An email signed "IT department" says: "Your mailbox will be closed today unless you sign in through this link." The sender's address is outside the office domain. What do you do?`,options:[{text:"I do not click the link and I report it to IT through the official channel",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: urgency, a threat and an outside address are signs of phishing; the report lets IT protect all staff."},{text:"I sign in quickly so my mailbox is not closed",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: the fake page steals your password, and the attacker can then send messages in your name."},{text:"I reply to the email and ask whether it is real",correct:!1,feedback:'Incorrect: the reply goes to the attacker, who will say "yes, it is real"; check with IT another way.'},{text:"I forward it to my colleagues to warn them",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: forwarding spreads the link and someone may click it; warning staff is IT's job."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"You clicked a link in an email that seemed to come from the director's office and typed your password. Then you realized the page was fake. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I tell IT at once and change my password as they direct",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the password has been exposed; changing it quickly and telling IT shut the attacker out."},{text:"I say nothing and wait to see whether anything happens",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: the attacker can use your account straight away; delay makes the damage bigger."},{text:"I delete the email so that nobody sees it",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: deleting does not protect the password and destroys the evidence IT needs."},{text:"I switch off the computer and work on another one",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: the attacker still has the password; switching computers does not protect the account."}]},{prompt:`The next morning, files on the shared drive show strange names and a message on a colleague's screen says: "Your data has been locked; pay to get it back." What do you do?`,options:[{text:"We stop using that computer, do not pay, alert IT at once and follow their instructions",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: this is ransomware; IT can cut the devices off the network and restore data from protected copies."},{text:"We pay so that the data comes back quickly",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: there is no guarantee the data will come back, and paying encourages more attacks; the decision belongs to the responsible authority, not to staff."},{text:"We restart the computers again and again until the message goes away",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: restarting does not remove the malware and may destroy information that IT needs."},{text:"We copy the remaining files to a USB drive to save them",correct:!1,feedback:"Incorrect: the USB drive can carry the malware to other computers; IT protects the data with secured copies."}]}],realCase:"In a government office, an employee in the finance section received an email that seemed to come from a known supplier. It asked her to change the bank account number for paying an invoice and said the matter was urgent. She called the supplier on the number in the office's own records. The supplier said they had sent no such request. She reported it to IT, who warned all finance staff. Lesson: a request to change a payment is always checked through another channel."},{id:9,code:"GOV-109",icon:"ShieldAlert",accent:"#7c3aed",title:"Personal security and violence prevention",subtitle:"Personal safety and workplace security",summary:"Protecting staff from security threats and workplace violence",blocks:[{body:"Staff safety is not only about fire and electricity. Workplace violence, threats and security incidents also require preparedness. Every staff member must know how to protect themselves in these situations."},{heading:"1. Security threats in offices",body:"Security situations:",points:["Angry and dissatisfied visitors.","Physical threats.","Suspicious packages and items.","Armed attack, theft or looting.","Hostage-taking.","Threats by telephone or over the internet."]},{heading:"2. Run, Hide, Tell",body:"If a security incident occurs:",points:["Run: if there is a safe route, leave the building quickly.","Always know the nearest exit route.","Hide: if you cannot run, hide in a room and lock the door.","Silence your phone and turn off vibration as well.","Tell: once you are in a safe place, call 104 (Police).","Give all information in detail: location, number of people and their description."]},{heading:"3. Dealing with angry visitors",body:"De-escalation techniques:",points:["Speak in a calm, low voice.","Listen and show that you understand.","Keep a safe distance (1.5–2 meters).","Always keep your own exit route clear.","Alert a colleague to come and help you.","If the situation gets worse, press the panic button or call 104."]},{heading:"4. Office preparedness",body:"Security preparations:",points:["Install surveillance cameras (CCTV).","An entry control system (Access Control).","A panic button under the reception desks.","Staff training every 6 months.","Update and test the security plan.","Establish a direct line of communication with the nearest police station."]},{heading:"5. Suspicious packages and threatening calls",body:"Two situations that any staff member may face:",points:["Suspicious package: do not touch, open or move it.","Move people away from the area and keep them away.","Tell the security officer and call 104 from a safe place.","Threatening call: stay calm, listen and let the caller talk.","Write down the exact words, the time, the voice and any background sounds.","After the call, inform your manager and 104 at once."]},{heading:"6. After an incident",body:"What the office does after any threat or act of violence:",points:["Check that everyone is safe and give first aid if needed (call 122).","Write a report: what happened, when, who was involved and what was said.","Keep messages, recordings and objects for the police; do not clean up the scene if the police are coming.","Support the staff who were affected; talking to a trusted person helps.","Review the security plan and fix what did not work."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/gov_security.mp4",title:"Personal security",description:"Run, Hide, Tell"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/gov_deescalation.png",title:"De-escalation techniques",description:"Dealing with angry visitors"}],quiz:[{question:"In the Run, Hide, Tell procedure, what is the first step?",options:[{text:"Hide",correct:!1},{text:"Run",correct:!0},{text:"Fight back",correct:!1},{text:"Call the police",correct:!1}],explanation:"The first action: Run, if there is a safe route."},{question:"When dealing with an angry visitor, what should you do?",options:[{text:"Raise your voice so they stop shouting",correct:!1},{text:"Speak calmly and listen",correct:!0},{text:"Ignore them",correct:!1},{text:"Tell them to come back another day",correct:!1}],explanation:"Speak calmly and listen — this de-escalates the situation."},{question:"What is the police number in the Kurdistan Region?",options:[{text:"115",correct:!1},{text:"122",correct:!1},{text:"104",correct:!0},{text:"911",correct:!1}],explanation:"104 is the police number in the Kurdistan Region."},{question:"A visitor becomes angry and moves toward you across the counter. What should you protect first?",options:[{text:"Your distance and a clear route to the exit",correct:!0},{text:"The files and papers on the desk in front of you",correct:!1},{text:"Your chance to explain the office rules to him",correct:!1},{text:"The visitor's place in the waiting queue",correct:!1}],explanation:"Distance and a free way out keep you safe if the visitor becomes violent; papers and explanations can wait."},{question:"You find an unattended bag with wires showing near the office entrance. What is right?",options:[{text:"Carry it outside so that no one inside is hurt",correct:!1},{text:"Open it carefully to see what is inside",correct:!1},{text:"Move people away and call 104 from a safe place",correct:!0},{text:"Cover it with a box and carry on working",correct:!1}],explanation:"Never touch or move a suspicious item: moving it may set it off. Clear the area and let the police deal with it."},{question:"During an attack you are hiding in a locked room. Which action is wrong?",options:[{text:"Silencing your phone and turning off vibration",correct:!1},{text:"Staying away from the door and keeping quiet",correct:!1},{text:"Calling 104 quietly when it is safe to speak",correct:!1},{text:"Opening the door to see if the attacker has left",correct:!0}],explanation:"Opening the door can reveal where you are hiding. Stay hidden until the police tell you it is safe."}],steps:[{title:"Notice the warning signs early",detail:"A raised voice, clenched fists, pacing, threats or refusal to listen show that a situation may turn violent. Act early: stay calm and do not argue."},{title:"Protect your distance and your exit",detail:"Keep a safe distance (1.5–2 meters), keep the desk or counter between you and never let the person stand between you and the door.",critical:!0},{title:"Calm the situation",detail:"Speak in a calm, low voice, listen, repeat the problem back in your own words and offer a realistic next step. Do not make promises you cannot keep."},{title:"Call for help early",detail:"Alert a colleague with the agreed phrase or signal and press the panic button if there is one. If the person becomes threatening, leave the room and call 104 from a safe place.",critical:!0},{title:"If there is an attack: Run, Hide, Tell",detail:"Run if there is a safe route; if not, hide behind a locked door with your phone silenced; call 104 when it is safe to speak. Never try to stop or disarm an attacker.",critical:!0},{title:"Report and record",detail:"After every incident, even if no one was hurt, tell your manager and the security officer and write down what happened, when, who was involved and what was said."}],warnings:["Do not argue, shout back, touch or block an angry person: it makes violence more likely. Let them leave if they want to.","Never try to stop, search or disarm an attacker or an armed person. Your task is to get away, hide and call 104.","Do not touch, open or move a suspicious package or item. Move people away from it and call 104 from a safe place."],tips:["Agree with your colleagues on a code phrase that means: come to my office now and call security.","Arrange your desk so that you can reach the door without passing the visitor.","Save 104 and the security officer's number in your phone.","Tell your manager about any threat you receive by phone or message, even if it sounds like a joke."],activity:{prompt:"A visitor at the reception desk starts shouting because his file has been delayed. He hits the desk and comes closer to you. Your colleague is in the next room. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I shout back so that he understands he cannot behave like this",correct:!1,feedback:"Shouting back raises the tension and makes an attack more likely; a calm, low voice lowers it."},{text:"I step back to keep my distance and my exit, speak calmly and signal my colleague to come",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: distance and a clear exit protect you, a calm voice lowers the tension and a colleague brings support and a witness."},{text:"I take hold of his arm and lead him to the door",correct:!1,feedback:"Touching an angry person can turn shouting into a fight. Keep your distance and call for help instead."},{text:"I ignore him and keep working until he calms down",correct:!1,feedback:"Ignoring him makes him feel unheard and often angrier. Listen, show that you understand and call for support."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"You work on the second floor of a public office. You hear shouting and a loud bang from the ground-floor entrance, and people are running. You do not know what is happening. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"I go down to the entrance to see what happened",correct:!1,feedback:"Going toward an unknown threat puts you in danger. Move away from it."},{text:"If the stairs away from the entrance are safe, I lead the people near me out; if not, we hide in a room and lock the door",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: Run if there is a safe route; if there is not, Hide."},{text:"I call my family to ask them what I should do",correct:!1,feedback:"Calls to family waste time. Get to safety first, then call 104."}]},{prompt:"You and three colleagues are hiding in a locked office. The shouting continues in the corridor. One colleague wants to open the door to look. What now?",options:[{text:"We keep the door locked, silence our phones, stay away from the door and one of us quietly calls 104 with our location",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: stay hidden and quiet, and tell 104 where you are and what you heard when it is safe to speak."},{text:"We open the door a little to check whether the danger has passed",correct:!1,feedback:"Opening the door can reveal your hiding place. Wait until the police tell you it is safe."},{text:"We go out together and try to stop the person",correct:!1,feedback:"Never confront an attacker. Your task is to stay safe and give information to 104."}]}],realCase:"A woman came to a public office to ask about her pension, which had been delayed for months. At the counter she began to shout and threatened the employee. The employee kept the counter between them, lowered her own voice, listened and repeated the problem back to her. At the same time she used the agreed code phrase on the internal phone, and a colleague and the security guard came. The visitor calmed down and was given an appointment with the department manager. Afterwards the employee wrote a short report, and the office moved the waiting chairs so that staff could always reach the back door."},{id:10,code:"GOV-110",icon:"Armchair",accent:"#16a34a",title:"Workplace health and safety",subtitle:"Occupational health and ergonomics",summary:"Protecting staff health at work and setting up the desk and chair correctly",blocks:[{body:"Government staff often sit in front of a computer for 8 hours or more. This causes back, eye and shoulder pain. A properly organized workstation (Ergonomics) and physical activity protect the health of employees."},{heading:"1. Setting up the desk and chair (ergonomics)",body:"A correct workstation:",points:["Chair: it should have back support; adjust the height so that your feet are fully on the floor.","Screen: the top of the screen should be at eye level or slightly lower, at a distance of 50–70 cm.","Keyboard: elbows at an angle of about 90°.","Mouse: close to the keyboard and at the same level.","Desk height: must suit your height.","Lighting: light should not fall directly on the screen."]},{heading:"2. Protecting the eyes",body:"Eye strain from screens (Computer Vision Syndrome):",points:["The 20-20-20 rule: every 20 minutes, look at something 20 feet (about 6 meters) away for 20 seconds.","Blink often — in front of a screen blinking decreases and the eyes become dry.","Reduce screen brightness in the evenings.","Enable night mode.","Glasses that block blue light have no proven benefit (AAO); if your eyes are dry or tired, see an eye doctor.","Have an eye examination every year."]},{heading:"3. Back and shoulder pain",body:"Prevention and treatment:",points:["Every 45 minutes, stand up and move around a little.","Neck stretch: slowly tilt your head toward each shoulder.","Back stretch: slowly rotate your body while seated in the chair.","When appropriate, take the stairs instead of the elevator.","Drink water regularly throughout the day.","Correct sitting posture: back straight, shoulders relaxed, feet on the floor."]},{heading:"4. Mental health",body:"The psychological well-being of staff:",points:["Work stress: identify the main causes.","Time management: keep work time and break time separate.","Talking: talk with your colleagues about problems.","Take a 15-minute break outdoors every day.","Do not use your phone for work during breaks.","If symptoms persist: contact a mental health professional."]},{heading:"5. Slips, trips and falls in the office",body:"Simple habits prevent many injuries:",points:["Keep corridors, stairs and exits clear of boxes, bags and cables.","Close drawers and cabinet doors after use.","Wipe up spilled water or tea at once, or put up a warning sign.","Use a step stool to reach high shelves, never a chair.","Report loose carpet, broken tiles and dark corners to the building manager."]},{heading:"6. Lifting and carrying",body:"Boxes of paper and equipment are often heavier than they look:",points:["Check the weight before you lift and ask for help if in doubt.","Bend your knees and keep your back straight.","Hold the load close to your body.","Turn with your feet; do not twist your back.","Use a trolley for heavy loads or many loads."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/gov_ergonomics.mp4",title:"Ergonomics",description:"Setting up the desk and chair"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/gov_posture.png",title:"Correct sitting posture",description:"An instructional image"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/gov_eye_care.png",title:"The 20-20-20 rule",description:"Protecting the eyes"}],quiz:[{question:"What is the 20-20-20 rule for?",options:[{text:"Protecting the ears",correct:!1},{text:"Protecting the eyes",correct:!0},{text:"Protecting the back",correct:!1},{text:"Protecting the hands",correct:!1}],explanation:"Every 20 minutes, look at something 20 feet (about 6 meters) away for 20 seconds — to protect the eyes (AAO)."},{question:"How far from the eyes should a computer screen be?",options:[{text:"20–30 cm",correct:!1},{text:"30–40 cm",correct:!1},{text:"50–70 cm",correct:!0},{text:"1 meter",correct:!1}],explanation:"A distance of 50–70 cm (about an arm's length) is most suitable."},{question:"How often should staff get up from their desk and move around?",options:[{text:"Every 20 minutes",correct:!1},{text:"Every 45 minutes",correct:!0},{text:"Every 2 hours",correct:!1},{text:"Every 1 hour",correct:!1}],explanation:"It is recommended to get up and move around a little every 45 minutes."},{question:"After moving to a new desk you get neck pain. Your screen sits low and you look down at it all day. What do you change first?",options:[{text:"Raise the screen so that its top is at or just below eye level",correct:!0},{text:"Move the screen closer, to about 20–30 cm from your eyes",correct:!1},{text:"Turn the screen brightness up as high as it will go",correct:!1},{text:"Lower your chair so that the screen seems higher",correct:!1}],explanation:"Looking down bends the neck. The top of the screen should be at or slightly below eye level, 50–70 cm away."},{question:"Which is the safest way to reach files on a high shelf?",options:[{text:"Stand on your office chair with wheels",correct:!1},{text:"Climb on the lower shelves of the cabinet",correct:!1},{text:"Use a proper step stool or ask for help",correct:!0},{text:"Reach up on tiptoe and pull the box down",correct:!1}],explanation:"Wheeled chairs and shelves are not made to stand on and can roll or tip; a step stool is stable."},{question:"A colleague trips over a cable across the corridor. After first aid, what is the most important next step?",options:[{text:"Remind everyone to watch where they walk",correct:!1},{text:"Move the cable out of the walkway and record the incident",correct:!0},{text:"Put a rug over the cable so that it is hidden",correct:!1},{text:"Wait and see whether anyone else trips over it",correct:!1}],explanation:"The cause must be removed and the incident recorded; reminders and rugs leave the hazard in place."}],steps:[{title:"Set up your workstation",detail:"Adjust the chair so that your feet rest flat on the floor and your back is supported; put the top of the screen at or slightly below eye level, 50–70 cm away, and the mouse next to the keyboard."},{title:"Rest your eyes",detail:"Use the 20-20-20 rule: every 20 minutes, look at something about 6 meters away for 20 seconds, and blink often."},{title:"Move regularly",detail:"Stand up and move around a little every 45 minutes. Do the neck and back stretches slowly and stop if they hurt."},{title:"Keep walkways and exits clear",detail:"Keep cables, boxes and bags out of corridors and away from exits, close drawers after use and wipe up spills at once; report loose carpet, broken tiles and poor lighting.",critical:!0},{title:"Lift and carry safely",detail:"Keep the load close to your body, bend your knees, do not twist your back and use a trolley or ask for help with heavy or bulky loads such as boxes of paper."},{title:"Look after your mental health",detail:"Take your breaks, talk with colleagues or your manager about work stress and contact a mental health professional if the symptoms continue."}],warnings:["Do not run cables across walkways or stack boxes in corridors and in front of exits: people trip over them, and in an emergency they block the way out.","Never stand on a chair with wheels to reach a high shelf, and do not lift a load you are not sure you can carry: use a step stool, a trolley or a colleague."],tips:["Check the height of your chair and screen whenever you move to a new desk.","Place the printer and the waste bin a few steps away so that you stand up more often.","Keep a bottle of water on your desk as a reminder to drink.","Tell your manager early if pain in your back, neck or wrist does not go away."],activity:{prompt:"A new colleague complains of neck pain and tired eyes. Her laptop sits flat on the desk, the window is behind the screen and she works for hours without a break. What do you advise first?",options:[{text:"Raise the screen to eye level, turn the desk so that window light does not fall on the screen and take short breaks",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: screen height, glare and long periods without breaks are the main causes of her pain and eye strain."},{text:"Buy glasses that block blue light",correct:!1,feedback:"Blue-light glasses have no proven benefit (AAO). Fix the screen position and lighting, and see an eye doctor if the problem continues."},{text:"Work faster so that she can finish early and rest at home",correct:!1,feedback:"Working for hours without a break is part of the problem; short breaks and movement during the day help."},{text:"Put the laptop on her lap so that the screen is closer",correct:!1,feedback:"This bends the neck even more. The top of the screen should be at or slightly below eye level, 50–70 cm away."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"You are the safety focal point of your office. During a walk-round you find an extension cable running across the corridor to a heater, and boxes of files stacked in front of the emergency exit. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"I have the boxes moved away from the exit at once and the cable taken out of the walkway, then report both to the manager",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: a blocked exit is a life-safety problem that must be fixed now; the trip hazard follows straight after."},{text:"I note both in my monthly report and deal with them later",correct:!1,feedback:"A blocked exit cannot wait for a report: in a fire people could be trapped."},{text:"I cover the cable with a rug so that nobody trips",correct:!1,feedback:"People still trip over a rug, and a cable under a rug can overheat unseen. Move the cable out of the walkway."}]},{prompt:"The boxes have been moved, but staff say there is no other place for the heater and the files. A colleague has already tripped and hurt her wrist. What next?",options:[{text:"I tell the staff to be more careful when they walk",correct:!1,feedback:"A warning alone does not remove the hazard; the cause must be fixed."},{text:"I make sure the colleague gets first aid, record the incident and ask the manager for a socket near the desk and storage space for the files",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: care for the injured person, record what happened and remove the cause with a lasting solution."},{text:"I let each employee decide where to put things",correct:!1,feedback:"Without a decision the hazard comes back; the manager must provide a safe solution."}]}],realCase:"In a busy government department, several staff complained of back and neck pain. Most of them worked on laptops placed flat on the desk and rarely stood up during the day. The safety focal point asked each person to check the height of the chair, raised the screens to eye level with simple stands and moved the printer to the end of the room. The manager agreed to short movement breaks. During the same check, two cables that crossed the corridor were moved and a box blocking a fire exit was cleared. Staff were asked to report whether the changes helped."},{id:11,code:"GOV-111",icon:"Waves",accent:"#2563eb",title:"Floods and water damage",subtitle:"Flood response and water damage",summary:"Preparing for and responding to floods and water damage in offices",blocks:[{body:"Water can reach offices from several sources: rain and natural floods, burst water pipes, air-conditioning system problems or water leaking from the floor above. Preparedness and awareness reduce the damage."},{heading:"1. Sources of water in offices",body:"Where water comes from:",points:["Rain and natural floods: especially in low-lying areas.","Burst water pipes: old and corroded pipes.","Melting snow: snow turns into water and enters the building.","Water leaking from the floor above: from restrooms and kitchens.","Air-conditioning system: accumulated water and leaks.","Water tanks: a tank bursting or leaking."]},{heading:"2. Before a flood — preparedness",body:"Required preparations:",points:["Keep important documents on upper floors and in high cabinets.","Do not place computers on the floor — keep them at least 30 cm above the floor.","Electrical cables should not lie on the floor.","Know the plumbing layout and the location of the main water valve.","Have a water drainage pump (Sump Pump) ready.","Keep a list of technical and emergency numbers ready."]},{heading:"3. During a flood",body:"If water enters the office:",points:["If you are not standing in water and can reach the switch safely, cut off the main power — electricity and water together are deadly.","Call 115 and notify the building manager.","All staff should move safely to higher ground.","If the water is shallow: move documents and computers to a higher location.","Do not enter flood water: 15 cm of fast-moving water can knock a person down and 30 cm can carry a car away; there is also a risk of electrocution and disease.","Shut off the main water valve."]},{heading:"4. After a flood",body:"After the water recedes:",points:["Building inspection: before returning, a building expert should inspect the building.","Do not switch the power on until a technician confirms that it is safe.","Disinfection: flood water is contaminated and carries bacteria and diseases.","Ventilation: open all windows and doors.","Take photos for records and insurance.","Remove damaged items safely."]},{heading:"5. Burst pipes and leaks inside the building",body:"Not every flood comes from outside:",points:["Know where the main water valve is and who can close it.","Close the valve only if you can reach it from a dry place.","Keep people away from wet floors near sockets and equipment.","Do not unplug or switch on anything while standing in water.","Tell the building manager and record where the leak started."]},{heading:"6. Flood warnings and staff safety",body:"When heavy rain or a flood warning is announced:",points:["Follow the instructions of Civil Defense and official announcements.","Staff should not walk or drive through flooded roads; wait or take another safe route.","Move vehicles, records and equipment to higher ground early.","Account for all staff and visitors at the assembly point.","Help colleagues who need assistance to reach higher floors."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/gov_flood_response.mp4",title:"Flood response",description:"What to do when water comes in"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/gov_flood_prep.png",title:"Flood preparedness",description:"Step by step"}],quiz:[{question:"During a flood in the office, what is the first thing to do?",options:[{text:"Gather the documents and carry them outside",correct:!1},{text:"Cut off the main power (if it is safe)",correct:!0},{text:"Take photos",correct:!1},{text:"Carry the computers down to the basement",correct:!1}],explanation:"Electricity and water together create a risk of death. If you are not standing in water and can reach the switch safely, cut off the main power immediately; if not, get out and call 115."},{question:"Where should important documents be kept in an office at risk of flooding?",options:[{text:"On the floor, in cardboard boxes",correct:!1},{text:"In the basement, in closed metal cabinets",correct:!1},{text:"On upper floors and in high cabinets",correct:!0},{text:"It makes no difference",correct:!1}],explanation:"Always keep important documents in a high and safe location."},{question:"Why is disinfection necessary after a flood?",options:[{text:"To remove the bad smell left by the mud",correct:!1},{text:"Flood water carries bacteria and diseases",correct:!0},{text:"To make the walls and floors look clean again",correct:!1},{text:"It is not necessary",correct:!1}],explanation:"Flood water is highly contaminated and carries bacteria and diseases."},{question:"Water is rising in your office and the main switch is in a wet corner. What is right?",options:[{text:"Wade to the switch quickly and turn off the power",correct:!1},{text:"Leave the switch, move everyone to a higher floor and call 115",correct:!0},{text:"Unplug each computer before you leave the room",correct:!1},{text:"Stay and lift the files onto the desks first",correct:!1}],explanation:"You may cut the power only from a dry place. If the switch is in a wet area, leave it, get people to higher ground and call 115."},{question:"The road outside is covered by fast-moving water that looks only ankle-deep. A colleague wants to drive home through it. What do you tell him?",options:[{text:"Drive fast so that the engine does not stall",correct:!1},{text:"Do not drive through; wait or take another safe route",correct:!0},{text:"Walk through it instead, because it is shallow",correct:!1},{text:"Follow a bigger car closely through the water",correct:!1}],explanation:"15 cm of fast-moving water can knock a person down and 30 cm can carry a car away. Never walk or drive through flood water."},{question:"The water has gone down. When may staff switch the office power back on?",options:[{text:"As soon as the floor looks dry again",correct:!1},{text:"After a technician confirms that it is safe",correct:!0},{text:"When the building manager arrives in the morning",correct:!1},{text:"After all the computers have been unplugged",correct:!1}],explanation:"Water can damage wiring and equipment in ways you cannot see. Only a technician decides when the power is safe."}],steps:[{title:"Make the power safe, only from a dry place",detail:"If you are not standing in water and can reach the switch safely, cut off the main power. If the floor is wet or the switch is near the water, do not touch it: leave the area.",critical:!0},{title:"Raise the alarm and call 115",detail:"Warn everyone on the floor, notify the building manager and call 115: give the location, how deep the water is and whether anyone is trapped.",critical:!0},{title:"Move people to higher ground",detail:"Staff and visitors move by the stairs to a higher floor or to a safe high place, following the instructions of Civil Defense. Help colleagues and visitors who need assistance.",critical:!0},{title:"Protect documents only if it is safe",detail:"If the water is shallow and you are not at risk, move files and computers higher. Never stay behind or wade into water to save equipment."},{title:"Stay out of flood water",detail:"15 cm of fast-moving water can knock a person down and 30 cm can carry a car away; the water may also be electrified or contaminated. Never walk or drive through it.",critical:!0},{title:"Return only when it is declared safe",detail:"Return after a building expert has inspected the building. Only a technician switches the power back on. Wear gloves and boots for the clean-up and disinfect everything the water touched."}],warnings:["Never touch a switch, socket or electrical device while you are standing in water or the floor is wet: electricity and water together kill.","Never walk or drive through flood water, even if it looks shallow: 15 cm of fast-moving water can knock a person down.","Do not go back into the building to save files or equipment once the water is rising."],tips:["Mark the main power switch and the main water valve on the office emergency plan.","Store important records in high cabinets and keep copies outside the building.","Keep a waterproof bag ready for essential documents.","After heavy rain, check the basement and ground floor before staff start work."],activity:{prompt:"Heavy rain is falling and water starts to flow under the door of your ground-floor office. It is ankle-deep and rising. The electrical panel is on the far side of the room, and the floor near it is already wet. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I wade across to the panel and switch off the power",correct:!1,feedback:"You would be standing in water while touching the panel; electricity and water together can kill. Leave it."},{text:"I do not go near the panel; I get everyone up the stairs to a higher floor, warn the building manager and call 115",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the switch cannot be reached from a dry place, so people go to higher ground and the power is dealt with safely by the building manager and 115."},{text:"I carry the computers down to the basement storeroom",correct:!1,feedback:"The basement floods first, and staying behind to move equipment wastes time and puts you at risk."},{text:"I stay at my desk and wait for the rain to stop",correct:!1,feedback:"Flood water can rise fast. Move to a higher floor now."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"You are the floor warden on the ground floor. A water pipe has burst in the storeroom, and water is spreading along the corridor toward the server room and the sockets near the floor. No one is hurt. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"I keep people away from the wet area and the sockets, have the main water valve closed if it can be reached from a dry place and warn the building manager",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: protect people from electricity first, then stop the water at its source if it is safe."},{text:"I unplug the computers in the wet corridor one by one",correct:!1,feedback:"Touching plugs in a wet area risks electric shock. Keep away and let the power be cut safely."},{text:"I mop the water toward the drain myself",correct:!1,feedback:"Mopping does not stop the water and keeps you in a wet area near sockets. Stop the source and keep people away."}]},{prompt:"The water valve has been closed, but the water has reached a socket and there is a burning smell from the wall. What next?",options:[{text:"I pour a bucket of water on the socket",correct:!1,feedback:"Water on an electrical fault can cause electric shock and spread the danger; never use water on live electrical equipment."},{text:"I wait to see whether the smell goes away",correct:!1,feedback:"A burning smell from a wet socket may be the start of an electrical fire. Act now."},{text:"I raise the fire alarm, get everyone off the floor and call 115; the power is cut at the main switch only if it can be reached from a dry place",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: a possible electrical fire is a fire emergency. Evacuate, call 115 and cut the power only if it is safe."}]}],realCase:"After a night of heavy rain, staff arrived at a public office to find water in the basement archive and on the ground floor. One employee wanted to go down the basement stairs to rescue the files. The building manager stopped him: the basement lights were still on and the water had reached the sockets. He kept everyone on the upper floors and called 115. Once the power had been cut and the building checked, staff wore gloves and boots to remove the wet files. The office then moved its archive to an upper floor."},{id:12,code:"GOV-112",icon:"FlaskConical",accent:"#65a30d",title:"Chemicals and cleaning agents",subtitle:"Chemical safety and cleaning agents",summary:"Safe use of cleaning agents and chemicals in offices",blocks:[{body:"Government offices use many types of cleaning agents. Mixing some of these substances can produce toxic and dangerous gases. Knowing the substances and using them correctly saves lives."},{heading:"1. Hazardous substances in offices",body:"These substances are found in many offices:",points:["Chlorine bleach: for cleaning restrooms — if mixed with acid or ammonia, it produces toxic gases.","Ammonia: for cleaning windows — harmful to breathing.","Acid: for removing limescale — burns the skin.","Printer toner: a fine powder — a hazard to the lungs.","Correction fluid: flammable, and its vapors are harmful.","Battery acid: chemical burns."]},{heading:"2. The never-mix rule!",body:"Never mix these substances:",points:["Bleach + ammonia = chloramine gas (toxic and deadly).","Bleach + acid = chlorine gas (toxic and deadly).","Bleach + vinegar (which is an acid) = chlorine gas.","Use each substance on its own.","Dilute only with water, following the instructions on the container — not with another substance.","Always read the instructions on the container (label)."]},{heading:"3. Personal protective equipment (PPE)",body:"Protective equipment when using chemicals:",points:["Gloves: required for all chemicals.","Mask: when using bleach and strong-smelling substances.","Safety goggles: for acids and sprays.","Protective clothing: for strong substances.","If a substance gets on the skin or in the eyes: rinse with running water for at least 20 minutes; if there is a burn or poisoning, call 122.","Ventilation: open the windows while cleaning."]},{heading:"4. Storing and disposing of chemicals",body:"Rules for storing chemicals:",points:["Keep them in a locked location.","Away from sunlight and heat.","Write the name of the substance on all bottles and containers.","Keep Safety Data Sheets (SDS) available.","Dispose of expired substances properly — do not pour them down the drain.","Keep them away from food and drinking water."]},{heading:"5. Small spills and leaks",body:"If a cleaning product spills in the office:",points:["Keep people away and open the windows if it is safe.","Put on gloves before you touch anything.","Soak up a small spill with paper or cloth and put it in a closed bag.","Do not wash it into the drain unless the label allows it.","For a large spill, a strong smell or people feeling unwell: leave the room, close the door and call 115 and 122."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/gov_chemical_safety.mp4",title:"Chemical safety",description:"Correct use"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/gov_mixing_danger.png",title:"The danger of mixing",description:"Which substances must not be mixed"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/gov_ppe_cleaning.png",title:"PPE for cleaning",description:"Protective equipment"}],quiz:[{question:"What happens if we mix bleach and ammonia?",options:[{text:"It makes a stronger cleaner for tough stains",correct:!1},{text:"It produces chloramine gas (toxic)",correct:!0},{text:"Nothing happens",correct:!1},{text:"It produces a harmless white foam",correct:!1}],explanation:"Mixing bleach and ammonia produces chloramine gas — there is a risk of death."},{question:"If a chemical gets on the skin, what should we do?",options:[{text:"Wipe it off with a dry tissue and cover it with a bandage",correct:!1},{text:"Rinse with water for at least 20 minutes",correct:!0},{text:"Apply a thick layer of cream to it",correct:!1},{text:"Do nothing",correct:!1}],explanation:"Immediately rinse with running water for at least 20 minutes and remove contaminated clothing; if there is a burn, call 122."},{question:"Where should cleaning agents be kept in the office?",options:[{text:"On an open shelf near the heater, so they stay dry",correct:!1},{text:"In the kitchen, next to the food and drinks",correct:!1},{text:"In a locked location, away from heat",correct:!0},{text:"It makes no difference",correct:!1}],explanation:"Keep them in a locked location, away from heat and food."},{question:"A cleaner splashes limescale remover into her eye. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"Rinse the eye with running water for at least 20 minutes",correct:!0},{text:"Put in eye drops and let her rest for a while",correct:!1},{text:"Rub the eye gently with a clean dry tissue",correct:!1},{text:"Cover the eye and wait until it stops hurting",correct:!1}],explanation:"Rinse at once with running water for at least 20 minutes; call 122, because the eye may be burned."},{question:"A colleague swallowed cleaning liquid from an unlabeled water bottle. What is right?",options:[{text:"Make him vomit as soon as possible",correct:!1},{text:"Give him milk to dilute the liquid",correct:!1},{text:"Do not make him vomit; keep the bottle and call 122",correct:!0},{text:"Give him salt water and let him rest",correct:!1}],explanation:"Vomiting can burn the throat again and harm the lungs. Give nothing by mouth unless a doctor says so, keep the container and call 122."},{question:"Which storage arrangement is wrong?",options:[{text:"Bleach and acid cleaners on separate shelves in a locked store",correct:!1},{text:"Each container labeled with the name of the product",correct:!1},{text:"Diluted cleaner kept in an old water bottle in the kitchen",correct:!0},{text:"SDS sheets kept where the cleaning staff can find them",correct:!1}],explanation:"A chemical in a drink bottle or near food can be swallowed by mistake. Keep chemicals labeled, locked away and away from food."}],steps:[{title:"Read the label and the SDS first",detail:"Before you use a product, read the label: what it is for, how to dilute it and what protection you need. The Safety Data Sheet (SDS) gives the full details."},{title:"Ventilate and wear protection",detail:"Open the windows and wear gloves; add goggles or a mask when the label says so, for example with bleach and acids."},{title:"Use one product at a time",detail:"Never mix products. Dilute only with water as the label says, and rinse the bucket and cloth between products.",critical:!0},{title:"Splash on the skin or in the eyes",detail:"Rinse at once with running water for at least 20 minutes and remove contaminated clothing; call 122 if there is a burn or the eyes are affected.",critical:!0},{title:"Fumes or someone feels unwell",detail:"If a sharp smell appears or someone coughs or feels dizzy, everyone leaves to fresh air, the door is closed and 122 is called. Do not go back in to air the room.",critical:!0},{title:"Swallowed by mistake",detail:"Do not make the person vomit and give nothing to eat or drink unless a doctor says so. Keep the container and call 122.",critical:!0}],warnings:["Never mix bleach with ammonia, acids or vinegar: the gases they produce can kill.","Never put chemicals into water or soft-drink bottles or unlabeled containers: someone may drink them by mistake.","Do not make a person who swallowed a chemical vomit, and do not give milk, oil or salt water."],tips:["Keep the cleaning store locked and the key with a responsible person.","Keep a copy of each product's SDS where the cleaning staff can find it.","Choose ready-to-use products where possible, so that staff do not need to dilute or mix.","Teach new cleaning staff the never-mix rule on their first day."],activity:{prompt:"A cleaner is cleaning the restroom with bleach. The stains will not come off, so she is about to pour a limescale remover (an acid) into the same bucket. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I let her do it but open the window first",correct:!1,feedback:"An open window does not make it safe: bleach and acid together produce chlorine gas, which can kill."},{text:"I stop her at once and explain that bleach and acid make a toxic gas, so each product is used on its own and rinsed off before the next",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: never mix. Use one product, rinse, then use the other."},{text:"I tell her to add hot water so that the mixture is weaker",correct:!1,feedback:"Adding water does not stop the reaction; the gas is still produced."},{text:"I suggest she uses ammonia instead of the acid",correct:!1,feedback:"Bleach and ammonia produce chloramine gas, which is also toxic. Bleach is mixed with nothing but water."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"In an office restroom a cleaner has mixed two products. A sharp smell fills the corridor; the cleaner is coughing and her eyes are watering. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"I get her and everyone nearby out to fresh air, close the restroom door and call 122",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: get people away from the gas first and call for medical help."},{text:"I go in with a wet cloth over my face to pour the mixture down the drain",correct:!1,feedback:"A wet cloth does not protect you from the gas; you could become the second casualty."},{text:"I spray air freshener in the corridor to cover the smell",correct:!1,feedback:"Air freshener does not remove a toxic gas and adds another chemical. Get people out."}]},{prompt:"The cleaner is now outside in fresh air. She is still coughing, and some of the liquid splashed on her forearm. The ambulance is on its way. What next?",options:[{text:"I give her milk to drink to soothe her throat",correct:!1,feedback:"Nothing to drink unless a doctor says so; milk does not treat chemical exposure."},{text:"I rinse her arm with running water for at least 20 minutes, remove contaminated clothing and keep the product containers for the ambulance team",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: rinse the skin and give the ambulance team the product names so that they know what she was exposed to."},{text:"I tell her to go home and rest",correct:!1,feedback:"A person who is coughing after a chemical exposure needs a medical check. Wait for 122."}]}],realCase:"A cleaner in a government building wanted the restroom floor to be extra clean, so she poured bleach and then a strong limescale remover into the same bucket. Within moments a sharp smell spread into the corridor. A colleague who had attended the office safety training told everyone to leave, closed the door and called 122. The cleaner, who was coughing, was taken outside to fresh air and checked by the ambulance team. The office later locked the cleaning store, put the never-mix rule on its door and trained all the cleaning staff."},{id:13,code:"GOV-113",icon:"ArrowUpDown",accent:"#4f46e5",title:"Elevator and stairway safety",subtitle:"Elevator and stairway safety",summary:"Using elevators safely and preventing falls on stairs",blocks:[{body:"Elevators and stairways are common locations of incidents in government buildings. Falls on stairs are among the most common workplace incidents, and being trapped in an elevator is a serious situation. Knowing the rules reduces the risk."},{heading:"1. Elevator safety",body:"Rules for using the elevator:",points:["If the elevator is slow to arrive, be patient — do not press the button repeatedly.","Do not open the elevator door by hand — let it open by itself.","Do not load more than the permitted capacity (as written on the elevator).","Do not use it during a power outage.","Do not use it during a fire or an earthquake — use the stairs only.","Know where the emergency button is."]},{heading:"2. Being trapped in an elevator",body:"If you are trapped in an elevator:",points:["Stay calm — do not panic.","Press the emergency button.","Use the telephone inside the elevator.","If there is cell phone coverage, call 115.","Do not try to open the door or climb out — it is dangerous.","Wait until the technician or the 115 team arrives — the air inside the elevator will not run out."]},{heading:"3. Stairway safety",body:"Preventing falls on stairs:",points:["Always hold the handrail.","Watch where you are stepping — do not use your phone.","One step at a time — do not jump.","Walk carefully on wet stairs.","When carrying a load, keep one hand free to hold the handrail.","Stairway lighting must be good — replace burned-out bulbs."]},{heading:"4. Inspection and maintenance",body:"Regular inspections:",points:["Elevators: monthly maintenance by a qualified company and annual inspection by an approved authority.","Post the inspection certificate inside the elevator.","Check the emergency lighting of the stairways.","Repair broken stairs immediately.","Place warning signs at slippery spots.","Keep stairs clear — do not leave any items on the stairs."]},{heading:"5. When someone is trapped: what staff outside do",body:"If you hear the elevator alarm:",points:["Talk to the people inside and tell them help is coming.","Ask how many they are and whether anyone is unwell.","Call the elevator technician and 115; call 122 if someone is unwell.","Do not open the doors with keys or tools unless you are trained and authorized.","Keep the area in front of the doors clear for the rescuers."]},{heading:"6. People who cannot use the stairs",body:"Plan ahead for colleagues and visitors with reduced mobility:",points:["Each person who needs help has a named helper and a written plan.","The plan says where they wait if they cannot go down and how they are reached.","The floor warden tells the 115 team where they are waiting.","Untrained staff do not carry people down the stairs.","Practice the plan in every evacuation drill."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/gov_elevator_safety.mp4",title:"Elevator safety",description:"Correct use and being trapped inside"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/gov_stair_safety.png",title:"Stairway safety",description:"Rules for preventing falls"}],quiz:[{question:"During a fire, what should we use to get out?",options:[{text:"The elevator",correct:!1},{text:"The building's stairs",correct:!0},{text:"It makes no difference",correct:!1},{text:"The windows",correct:!1}],explanation:"Never use the elevator during a fire or an earthquake — exit by the stairs only."},{question:"If you are trapped in an elevator, what should you do?",options:[{text:"Force the doors open and climb out yourself",correct:!1},{text:"Scream in panic",correct:!1},{text:"Press the emergency button and stay calm",correct:!0},{text:"Jump up and down to restart the elevator",correct:!1}],explanation:"Stay calm and press the emergency button. Do not try to open the door."},{question:"To prevent falls on stairs, what should we do?",options:[{text:"Jump",correct:!1},{text:"Walk quickly and read messages on your phone as you go",correct:!1},{text:"Hold the handrail and watch where you are stepping",correct:!0},{text:"Carry large boxes that block your view of the steps",correct:!1}],explanation:"Always hold the handrail and watch where you are stepping."},{question:"You are trapped in an elevator with a colleague who feels faint. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Force the doors open to let in fresh air",correct:!1},{text:"Press the emergency button and call 115 and 122",correct:!0},{text:"Climb out through the roof hatch to get help",correct:!1},{text:"Jump to make the elevator start moving again",correct:!1}],explanation:"Raise the alarm and call for help; 122 is needed because someone is unwell. Forcing the doors or climbing out is dangerous."},{question:"A power cut stops the elevator between floors. Staff outside want to pull the doors open to free the people inside. What is right?",options:[{text:"Pull the doors open together and lift them out",correct:!1},{text:"Use a key from the security desk to open the doors",correct:!1},{text:"Talk to them, call the technician and 115, and wait",correct:!0},{text:"Keep pressing the call button until the car moves",correct:!1}],explanation:"People can fall into the shaft or be crushed if the car moves. Only a technician or the 115 team frees them."},{question:"The fire alarm sounds. A colleague who uses a wheelchair works on the third floor. What does a good plan say?",options:[{text:"She takes the elevator alone before the others",correct:!1},{text:"Two untrained colleagues carry her down the stairs",correct:!1},{text:"She waits in the planned place with a helper and 115 is told",correct:!0},{text:"She stays in her office with the door left open",correct:!1}],explanation:"A person who cannot use the stairs follows a written plan: a helper, a planned waiting place and the 115 team told where she is."}],steps:[{title:"Use the stairs in an emergency",detail:"During a fire, an earthquake or a power cut, never use the elevator; use the stairs and hold the handrail.",critical:!0},{title:"If you are trapped, stay calm",detail:"The air will not run out. Press the emergency button and use the elevator telephone.",critical:!0},{title:"Call for help",detail:"If there is phone coverage, call 115: give the building, the elevator and the floor where it stopped, how many people are inside and whether anyone is unwell (then call 122 as well)."},{title:"Do not force the doors",detail:"Do not try to open the doors or climb out through the roof or a gap: the car can move suddenly. Wait for the technician or the 115 team.",critical:!0},{title:"Outside the elevator: reassure and report",detail:"If someone is trapped, talk to them through the door, tell them help is coming, call the elevator technician and 115 and keep others away from the doors."},{title:"Report every fault",detail:"Report a door that does not close properly, a car that jolts, a broken handrail or a damaged step, and keep it out of use until it is repaired."}],warnings:["Never use the elevator during a fire or an earthquake: it can stop between floors or open on the fire floor, and the shaft can fill with smoke.","Never pry open elevator doors or pull people out of a car stopped between floors: they can fall into the shaft or be crushed if the car moves."],tips:["Know where the elevator emergency button is and check that the elevator telephone works.","Display the elevator company's emergency number next to the elevator and at the security desk.","Keep one hand free for the handrail on the stairs, even when you are in a hurry.","Report a burned-out stairway light on the same day."],activity:{prompt:"The elevator you are in stops between two floors and the light flickers. Three other people are inside, and one of them panics and tries to pull the doors apart. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I calmly ask him to stop, press the emergency button, use the elevator telephone and explain that the air will not run out and help is coming",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: calm the others, raise the alarm and wait; forcing the doors is dangerous."},{text:"I help him open the doors so that we can climb out",correct:!1,feedback:"If the car moves while you climb, you can fall into the shaft or be crushed."},{text:"We all jump together to restart the elevator",correct:!1,feedback:"Jumping can trip the safety system and cause injuries; it never restarts an elevator."},{text:"We keep quiet and wait without calling anyone",correct:!1,feedback:"Nobody may know you are trapped. Press the emergency button and call for help."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"You are the floor warden. The fire alarm sounds and, out of habit, staff on your floor walk to the elevator. A colleague who uses a wheelchair is in her office. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"I stop people at the elevator, send them to the stairs and go to my colleague to help her follow her evacuation plan",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the elevator is not used in a fire, and a person who cannot use the stairs needs her plan and a helper."},{text:"I let them use the elevator because it is faster",correct:!1,feedback:"An elevator can stop or open on the fire floor. Never use it in a fire."},{text:"I go down first to check that the exit is clear",correct:!1,feedback:"As floor warden you make sure that everyone on your floor leaves; do not leave your colleague behind."}]},{prompt:"Your colleague and her helper have reached the place where her plan tells her to wait for the rescuers. Everyone else is going down the stairs. What next?",options:[{text:"I tell the helper to carry her down the stairs at once",correct:!1,feedback:"Carrying a person down stairs without training and equipment can injure both of them. Follow the plan."},{text:"I go down and tell the 115 team and the incident commander exactly where she is waiting and who is with her",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the rescuers must know where people who could not go down are waiting, so that they can reach them quickly."},{text:"I go back up and wait with her, and leave the rest of my floor",correct:!1,feedback:"Her helper stays with her; your job is to report her location and account for your floor at the assembly point."}]}],realCase:"On a hot afternoon a power cut stopped an elevator between two floors of a government building with four staff inside. One of them, who had attended the safety course, reminded the others that the air would not run out and pressed the emergency button. The security guard heard the alarm, talked to them through the door and called the elevator company and 115. A colleague wanted to open the doors with a key from the guard's desk, but the guard stopped him. The technician brought the car level with the floor and opened the doors safely."},{id:14,code:"GOV-114",icon:"Network",accent:"#e11d48",title:"Crisis management",subtitle:"Crisis management and ICS",summary:"Establishing a crisis management team and the Incident Command System (ICS)",blocks:[{body:"Every government office must have a Crisis Management Team. A crisis can be a fire, an earthquake, a flood, a security attack or even a cyberattack. The Incident Command System (ICS) is an international tool for managing any incident."},{heading:"1. The crisis management team (CMT)",body:"Team composition:",points:["Team leader (Crisis Commander): makes decisions and gives direction.","Operations officer: organizes activities.","Communications officer: liaison with external parties and the media.","Logistics officer: supplies and equipment.","Records officer: documents the incidents.","First aid officer: assists the injured."]},{heading:"2. The Incident Command System (ICS)",body:"ICS (Incident Command System):",points:["Developed in the United States and now used in many countries.","One incident commander — the chain of command is clear.","Assignment of roles: everyone knows their role and reports to one supervisor.","Unified communication: one communication channel and plain language.","Flexible organization (Modular): the organization expands or contracts according to the size of the incident.","Reporting: reports are updated at set times."]},{heading:"3. Crisis response steps",body:"General procedure:",points:["1. Alert: activate the alarm system.","2. Assessment: find out what has happened and how large it is.","3. Activation: convene the CMT.","4. Management: make decisions and carry them out.","5. Communication: inform all parties.","6. Recovery: carry out the plan for returning to normal operations."]},{heading:"4. Training and testing",body:"Ongoing preparedness:",points:["A tabletop exercise every 3 months.","A full-scale drill every 6 months.","Update the crisis plan every year.","Test the communication system.","Cooperation with Civil Defense (115) and the Police (104).","After every exercise, conduct an evaluation and improve the work."]},{heading:"5. Priorities and handover",body:"In every crisis the order of priorities is the same:",points:["First: the lives and safety of staff, visitors and responders.","Second: stopping the incident from growing.","Third: protecting property, records and the environment.","When Civil Defense arrives, brief them and follow their instructions.","Give them the floor plans, the missing persons and the known hazards."]},{heading:"6. Keeping essential services running",body:"Continuity after a crisis:",points:["List the services that must not stop and the staff they need.","Keep copies of key records outside the building.","Name an alternative workplace in advance.","Plan how the public will be informed if the office is closed."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/gov_crisis_management.mp4",title:"Crisis management",description:"The ICS system and the CMT"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/gov_ics_chart.png",title:"ICS chart",description:"The command structure"}],quiz:[{question:"In the ICS system, how many incident commanders are there?",options:[{text:"3",correct:!1},{text:"5",correct:!1},{text:"1",correct:!0},{text:"10",correct:!1}],explanation:"In ICS there is one incident commander, so that the chain of command is clear. If several agencies are involved, a Unified Command works under a single plan."},{question:"According to this program, how often is a tabletop exercise required?",options:[{text:"Every year",correct:!1},{text:"Every 6 months",correct:!1},{text:"Every 3 months",correct:!0},{text:"Only once",correct:!1}],explanation:"This program recommends conducting a tabletop exercise every 3 months."},{question:"What is the first step in responding to a crisis?",options:[{text:"Holding a management meeting to discuss the situation",correct:!1},{text:"Communication",correct:!1},{text:"Alerting and activating the alarm system",correct:!0},{text:"Recovery and repairing the damage",correct:!1}],explanation:"The first step: alert — activate the alarm system."},{question:"During an incident two managers give staff opposite orders. Which ICS principle is being broken?",options:[{text:"A flexible organization that grows with the incident",correct:!1},{text:"One incident commander and one supervisor for each person",correct:!0},{text:"Reports that are updated at set times",correct:!1},{text:"Plain language on a single communication channel",correct:!1}],explanation:"ICS has one incident commander, and each person reports to one supervisor, so that orders do not conflict."},{question:"An earthquake has damaged your building and staff are missing. In what order are the priorities?",options:[{text:"Property and records first, then people",correct:!1},{text:"Lives first, then stopping the incident, then property",correct:!0},{text:"The official report first, then the rescue",correct:!1},{text:"Stopping the incident first, then saving lives",correct:!1}],explanation:"Life safety always comes first, then stabilizing the incident, then protecting property and records."},{question:"Civil Defense (115) arrives at a fire in your office building. What should the office incident commander do?",options:[{text:"Brief them on hazards and missing people, then support them",correct:!0},{text:"Keep command and tell the firefighters where to go",correct:!1},{text:"Leave the scene, since the professionals have arrived",correct:!1},{text:"Send staff back inside to help the firefighters",correct:!1}],explanation:"Civil Defense needs the office's information about hazards and missing people; the office team then supports them and follows their instructions."}],steps:[{title:"Raise the alarm and call for help",detail:"Whoever sees the incident activates the alarm and calls 115, 122 or 104. Life safety comes before anything else.",critical:!0},{title:"Take command",detail:"One incident commander takes charge, chooses a safe place for the command post and announces it; everyone reports to one supervisor."},{title:"Assess and set priorities",detail:"Find out what happened, how many people are affected and what is still dangerous. Priorities: lives first, then stopping the incident from growing, then property and records.",critical:!0},{title:"Activate the team",detail:"Call the crisis management team and give each member a task: operations, communications, logistics, records and first aid."},{title:"Work with Civil Defense",detail:"When 115 arrives, the incident commander briefs them on the situation, missing people and hazards and follows their instructions; the office team supports them.",critical:!0},{title:"Recover and learn",detail:"Restart essential services, look after the staff, write an after-action report and update the plan."}],warnings:["Do not let several managers give different orders: without a single commander, people receive conflicting instructions and lose time.","Do not delay the alarm or the call to 115 until a meeting has decided: the alarm and the call come first, the meeting afterwards."],tips:["Keep a printed contact list of the crisis team and their deputies in case the network is down.","Name a deputy for every role, because the role holder may be absent.","Keep a crisis box ready: the plan, floor plans, contact list, vests, a flashlight and a notebook.","After each drill, write down what went well and what must improve."],activity:{prompt:"A fire alarm sounds in your office building. The director is away, and three department heads give different orders in the corridor. The plan names you as the deputy crisis commander. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I announce that I am taking command as the plan says, send staff out by the evacuation routes and ask the department heads to take their crisis-team roles",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: one commander named in advance ends conflicting orders, and the others take defined roles."},{text:"I wait for the director to answer his phone before deciding anything",correct:!1,feedback:"Waiting for an absent person wastes time; that is why a deputy is named."},{text:"I let each department head manage his own staff in his own way",correct:!1,feedback:"Different orders lead to confusion. ICS needs one incident commander."},{text:"I call a meeting in the conference room to agree on the plan",correct:!1,feedback:"During an alarm people evacuate first; decisions are made at the command post outside."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"An earthquake shakes your office building in the morning. When the shaking stops there are cracks in the walls, one employee is injured on the stairs and two staff are missing from the headcount at the assembly point. You are the crisis commander. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"I send staff back into the building to look for the missing colleagues",correct:!1,feedback:"Sending untrained staff into a damaged building can create more casualties. Tell 115."},{text:"I make sure 115 and 122 have been called, have first aid given to the injured employee and give the rescuers the names and last known location of the missing staff",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: life safety first. Rescuers with the right equipment search the building using your information."},{text:"I start writing the official report to the general directorate",correct:!1,feedback:"The report matters, but it comes after people are safe."}]},{prompt:"The 115 team is searching the building. Journalists and worried families are arriving at the gate, and staff are posting rumors on social media. What next?",options:[{text:"I ask the communications officer to give families and the media short, confirmed updates and tell staff not to post unconfirmed information",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: one spokesperson with confirmed facts stops rumors and protects the families."},{text:"I let every staff member answer the journalists' questions",correct:!1,feedback:"Different and unconfirmed versions spread confusion; only the communications officer speaks."},{text:"I tell everyone that all staff are safe so that they calm down",correct:!1,feedback:"Never give information that is not confirmed: two staff are still missing."}]}],realCase:"A small fire in an office kitchen filled one floor of a government building with smoke. The crisis plan named a commander and a deputy. Because the commander was on leave, the deputy took charge at once, set up the command post at the assembly point and gave each team member a task. The floor wardens reported their headcounts, and the deputy gave the 115 team the floor plans and the names of two staff who were not yet accounted for. Both were later found safe in another building. The after-action review added a second assembly point to the plan."},{id:15,code:"GOV-115",icon:"Megaphone",accent:"#c026d3",title:"Emergency communication",subtitle:"Emergency communication",summary:"The communication and alerting system during incidents",blocks:[{body:"During incidents, accurate and rapid communication saves lives. In many incidents, the lack of good communication has increased the number of casualties. Every government office must have a robust communication system."},{heading:"1. Alerting systems",body:"There are several systems:",points:["Internal siren: the fire and emergency alarm.","Public address system (PA System): for sending messages to all floors.","Mobile text messages (SMS Group): for staff.","Urgent email: for official notifications.","WhatsApp and Telegram groups: for rapid coordination.","Two-way radio (Walkie-Talkie): for the emergency team."]},{heading:"2. The communication chain",body:"Who sends the message:",points:["1. The reporter: the person who saw the incident — calls 115 immediately (and 122 if there are injured people).","2. Floor warden: the staff member responsible for the floor and its rooms.","3. Crisis commander: makes decisions and gives direction.","4. Communications officer: liaison with 115 and external parties.","5. Office director: informs the higher level.","All messages must be precise and contain complete information."]},{heading:"3. Content of an emergency message",body:"A good message contains:",points:["Location: where it happened (building, floor, room).","What: what happened (fire, earthquake, attack...)","People: how many people are injured or trapped.","Hazard: is there an ongoing hazard?","Needs: what help is needed.","Contact: your phone number for follow-up."]},{heading:"4. Communicating with external parties",body:"Contact with external parties:",points:["115 — Civil Defense: fire and rescue.","122 — Ambulance: injuries and illness.","104 — Police: security situations.","Media: only the communications officer speaks.","Staff families: notified by the simplest means.","General directorate: the official report."]},{heading:"5. Testing the system",body:"Ongoing checks:",points:["Test the internal siren every month.","Send a test message to the group every week.","Check the batteries and range of the two-way radios.","Update the list of emergency numbers.","Evaluate the communication system after every drill.","Backup plan: if a system fails, what replaces it?"]},{heading:"6. When the systems fail",body:"Plan for a power cut or a network failure:",points:["Charged two-way radios for the floor wardens and the commander.","A megaphone at the assembly point.","Runners who carry messages between floors.","Printed contact lists, because phones may not work.","An agreed meeting point for the crisis team."]},{heading:"7. Messages to staff and visitors",body:"A good announcement is short and clear:",points:["Say what has happened in a few words.","Say exactly what people must do and which route to use.","Repeat the message slowly and calmly.","Use the languages that staff and visitors understand.","Give updates when the situation changes."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/gov_emergency_comm.mp4",title:"Emergency communication",description:"The communication system and its chain"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/gov_comm_chain.png",title:"The communication chain",description:"Who contacts whom"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/gov_message_template.png",title:"Sample message",description:"The content of a correct message"}],quiz:[{question:"In an emergency, which system is fastest for alerting all floors?",options:[{text:"Email",correct:!1},{text:"A WhatsApp group message",correct:!1},{text:"Public address system (PA)",correct:!0},{text:"A paper notice on each floor's board",correct:!1}],explanation:"The PA system is the fastest because it is heard immediately on all floors."},{question:"What information should an emergency message contain?",options:[{text:"Only the caller's name, job title and phone number",correct:!1},{text:"Only the number of injured and their names",correct:!1},{text:"Location, what happened, people, hazard, needs",correct:!0},{text:"Only the number 115",correct:!1}],explanation:"A complete message: location + what happened + people + hazard + needs + contact number."},{question:"During a crisis, who is authorized to speak to the media?",options:[{text:"Any staff member who saw what happened",correct:!1},{text:"Only the communications officer",correct:!0},{text:"Visitors",correct:!1},{text:"Only the head of the security guards",correct:!1}],explanation:"Only the communications officer is authorized to speak to the media, so that incorrect information is not spread."},{question:"You call 115 about a fire in your office. What do you say first?",options:[{text:"Your full name, your job title and your department",correct:!1},{text:"The location: area, street, landmark, building, floor",correct:!0},{text:"How you think the fire started and who noticed it",correct:!1},{text:"Which offices and departments are on that floor",correct:!1}],explanation:"Location comes first, so that help can be sent even if the call is cut off; then what happened, people and dangers, then your name and number."},{question:"After calling 115, which action is wrong?",options:[{text:"Keeping your phone free for the call back",correct:!1},{text:"Telling your floor warden that you have called",correct:!1},{text:"Hanging up before the operator tells you to",correct:!0},{text:"Sending someone to guide the fire engines to the entrance",correct:!1}],explanation:"Hanging up early can lose the exact location or important instructions. Stay on the line until you are told to hang up."},{question:"During a power cut the PA system and the phones stop working while the building must be evacuated. What is the best backup?",options:[{text:"Wait until the power comes back to make the announcement",correct:!1},{text:"Floor wardens use radios and runners to warn every floor",correct:!0},{text:"Send an email to all staff about the evacuation",correct:!1},{text:"Post the evacuation order in the staff social media group",correct:!1}],explanation:"Radios and runners do not depend on the power or the network; email and online groups may not reach anyone."}],steps:[{title:"Raise the internal alarm",detail:"Activate the fire alarm or the PA system so that everyone in the building is warned at once.",critical:!0},{title:"Call from a safe place",detail:"Call 115 for fire and rescue, 122 for injured or ill people and 104 for security. With both fire and casualties, call 115 and 122.",critical:!0},{title:"Give the location first",detail:"Say the area, the street, a well-known landmark, the building and the floor; then what happened, how many people are hurt or trapped and any dangers.",critical:!0},{title:"Give your name and number and do not hang up",detail:"Answer the operator's questions and stay on the line until you are told to hang up; afterwards keep your phone free for the call back.",critical:!0},{title:"Report up the chain",detail:"Tell your floor warden and the crisis commander what you have reported, so that the message is not repeated or lost."},{title:"One voice to the outside",detail:"Only the communications officer speaks to the media and gives updates to families; staff do not post unconfirmed information."}],warnings:["Do not hang up before the operator tells you to: they may still need the exact location or have instructions for you.","Do not assume that someone else has already called 115: call yourself, or make sure that someone has.","Do not post photos, casualties' names or rumors on social media during an incident: families must hear the news from the office, not from the internet."],tips:["Put a card with the building's full address and nearby landmarks next to each office phone.","Keep the two-way radios charged and test them in every drill.","Use plain words in announcements so that visitors also understand what to do.","Agree on a backup: if the phones and the internet fail, runners carry messages between floors."],activity:{prompt:"You see smoke coming from the archive room on your floor. Several colleagues stand in the corridor saying that someone has probably called already. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I wait a minute, because someone has probably called already",correct:!1,feedback:"Everyone may be thinking the same; a call that nobody makes costs precious time."},{text:"I activate the fire alarm, call 115 from a safe place and tell my floor warden that I have called",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: alarm, call and inform the warden, so that everyone is warned and the call is neither forgotten nor repeated."},{text:"I post a message in the staff group asking whether anyone has called",correct:!1,feedback:"Messages are slow and may not be read. Raise the alarm and call 115 yourself."},{text:"I go into the archive to see how big the fire is",correct:!1,feedback:"Entering a smoke-filled room is dangerous; the location and the smoke are enough to report."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"A colleague collapses in the office meeting room. You call 122. What do you say first?",options:[{text:"I describe in detail what she was doing before she collapsed",correct:!1,feedback:"That can help later; first the ambulance needs to know where to go."},{text:"The location: area, street, landmark, building name and floor, then that a person has collapsed and whether she is breathing",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: location first, so that help can be sent even if the call is cut off."},{text:"My name and my job title",correct:!1,feedback:"Give your name and number, but after the location and what happened."}]},{prompt:"The operator says the ambulance is on its way and asks you to stay on the line. A colleague tells you to hang up and call the director. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I stay on the line, follow the operator's instructions and ask the colleague to inform the director and meet the ambulance at the entrance",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the operator may guide you through first aid, while others inform the director and guide the ambulance in."},{text:"I hang up and call the director, because he must decide",correct:!1,feedback:"The director does not need to approve an ambulance, and hanging up can lose important instructions."},{text:"I hang up and call 115 as well, to be sure",correct:!1,feedback:"One emergency call is enough for a collapse; hang up only when the operator tells you to."}]}],realCase:"When smoke appeared in the basement of a public office, three employees each assumed that another had called for help. A cleaner finally pressed the fire alarm and called 115. Because she gave the street, a nearby landmark and the entrance at the back of the building, the fire engines came straight to the right door. Meanwhile, the communications officer used the PA system to tell staff which stairs to use and later gave families short updates by phone. The review after the incident added address cards next to every office phone."},{id:16,code:"GOV-116",icon:"Heart",accent:"#db2777",title:"First aid and CPR",subtitle:"First aid and CPR — complete training",summary:"Complete training in first aid, CPR and emergency response in offices",blocks:[{body:"First aid is the initial help given to an injured or ill person before medical teams arrive. In offices there is a risk of fire, earthquakes and sudden illness. Every staff member must know how to perform CPR, stop bleeding and protect an injured person until the ambulance (122) arrives."},{heading:"1. CPR — cardiopulmonary resuscitation",body:"CPR is a life-saving procedure used when a person's heart or breathing stops:",points:["1. Scene safety: is the scene safe? (Is it free of live electricity, fire and gas?)",'2. Responsiveness: gently shake their shoulders and ask in a loud voice: "Are you all right? Can you hear me?"',"3. Call 122: if they do not respond and are not breathing normally, call 122 immediately and get an AED or ask someone to bring one.","4. Chest compressions: 30 compressions in the center of the chest — depth 5–6 cm, rate 100–120 compressions per minute.","5. Breaths: tilt the head back and lift the chin, pinch the nose and give 2 breaths of 1 second each; if you have not been trained, give chest compressions only.","6. Continue (30:2): until the ambulance team (122) arrives.","7. AED: if one is available, switch it on, place the pads on the bare chest and follow the voice prompts."]},{heading:"2. Stopping bleeding",body:"Severe bleeding can quickly lead to death:",points:["Direct pressure: place a clean cloth on the wound and press firmly without stopping; if the cloth becomes soaked with blood, do not remove it — place another one on top.","Wound packing: if the wound is deep (such as in the thigh, armpit or neck), pack clean gauze into the wound and press firmly.","Tourniquet: use it early for life-threatening bleeding from an arm or leg, 5–7 cm above the wound; write down the time it was applied and never loosen it.","Embedded object: if metal or glass is lodged in the wound — do not remove it! Stabilize it in place and call 122."]},{heading:"3. Treating burns",body:"Burns in offices are caused by heat, chemicals and electricity:",points:["Thermal burns: run cool water over the burn for 20 minutes (not ice!)","Chemical burns: rinse with plenty of running water for 20 minutes.","Electrical burns: first cut off the power! Then cool it like a thermal burn and call 122.","Do not burst burn blisters; do not use oil, ointment or toothpaste."]},{heading:"4. Fractures and spinal injuries",body:"After a fall:",points:["Splinting: do not move the injured limb — immobilize it with a piece of wood or cardboard.","Protecting the spine: if a person has fallen from a height, do not move them — keep them still and call 122. Move them only if the place is unsafe (fire, traffic, collapse, gas, rising water), keeping the head, neck and back in line; if they need CPR, lay them on their back on a firm surface. Do not put them on their side (recovery position).","Cooling: wrap ice in a piece of cloth and place it on the painful area (not directly on the skin)."]},{heading:"5. Electric shock",body:"Electricity can stop the heart:",points:["Cut off the power immediately! Do not touch the casualty until the power is off.","If you cannot cut off the power and it is the building's normal supply (low voltage): move the cable away from the person with a dry wooden or plastic object; with high voltage, stay away and call 115.","Everyone who has received an electric shock must go to the hospital — call 122; if they are not breathing, start CPR."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/gov_cpr.mp4",title:"CPR and first aid",description:"CPR training for staff"}],quiz:[{question:"In CPR, how many chest compressions should be given per minute?",options:[{text:"50–70",correct:!1},{text:"80–90",correct:!1},{text:"100–120",correct:!0},{text:"140–160",correct:!1}],explanation:"There should be 100–120 compressions per minute (AHA 2025)."},{question:"If a sharp object is lodged in a wound, what do you do?",options:[{text:"I pull it out gently and press on the wound",correct:!1},{text:"I leave it in place and call 122",correct:!0},{text:"I step back and do nothing",correct:!1},{text:"I wash it with water",correct:!1}],explanation:"Removing the object will increase the bleeding; stabilize it in place and call 122."},{question:"If a person has received an electric shock, what is the first thing to do?",options:[{text:"I grab their hand",correct:!1},{text:"I perform CPR",correct:!1},{text:"I cut off the power",correct:!0},{text:"I pour water on them",correct:!1}],explanation:"The power must be cut off first, so that you do not become a casualty yourself."},{question:"An employee faints at her desk without falling or hitting anything. She is unresponsive but breathing normally. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Recovery position, call 122 and watch her breathing",correct:!0},{text:"Sit her up on a chair and give her sweet tea",correct:!1},{text:"Start chest compressions straight away",correct:!1},{text:"Leave her to rest and check on her later",correct:!1}],explanation:"Unresponsive with normal breathing and no suspected neck, back, hip or pelvis injury: recovery position, call 122 and keep checking the breathing; nothing by mouth."},{question:"You have never been trained in rescue breaths and find a colleague who is unresponsive and not breathing normally. What is right?",options:[{text:"Do nothing until a trained person arrives",correct:!1},{text:"Call 122 and give continuous chest compressions",correct:!0},{text:"Give rescue breaths only, without compressions",correct:!1},{text:"Shake him hard until he starts to respond",correct:!1}],explanation:"Hands-Only CPR: an untrained rescuer gives compressions only, 100–120 a minute, until the AED or the ambulance team arrives."},{question:"A colleague's forearm is bleeding heavily after a glass door broke. Blood soaks through the first cloth you pressed on. What now?",options:[{text:"Remove the soaked cloth and press with a clean one",correct:!1},{text:"Put another cloth on top and keep pressing firmly",correct:!0},{text:"Raise the arm and stop pressing on the wound",correct:!1},{text:"Wash the wound under the tap before pressing",correct:!1}],explanation:"A soaked dressing is not removed: add more on top and keep firm pressure. If the bleeding is life-threatening and does not stop, use a tourniquet early."}],steps:[{title:"Make sure the scene is safe",detail:"Before you touch the person, check for live electricity, fire, gas or traffic. If the danger cannot be removed, do not go in: call 115 and 122.",critical:!0},{title:"Check response and breathing, then call",detail:"Tap the shoulders and shout. If there is no response and no normal breathing (gasping is not normal), call 122 at once and send someone for the AED.",critical:!0},{title:"Push hard and fast in the center of the chest",detail:"Give 30 compressions with the heel of the hand: depth 5–6 cm, rate 100–120 a minute, letting the chest come back up fully each time.",critical:!0},{title:"Breaths if trained, otherwise compressions only",detail:"If you are trained, give 2 breaths of 1 second each and continue 30:2. If you are not trained, give compressions only (Hands-Only CPR) without stopping.",critical:!0},{title:"Use the AED as soon as it arrives",detail:"Switch it on, place the pads on the bare chest and follow the voice prompts. Continue CPR until the ambulance team (122) takes over.",critical:!0},{title:"Unresponsive but breathing normally",detail:"Put the person in the recovery position only if there is no suspected injury to the neck, back, hip or pelvis; call 122, give nothing by mouth and keep checking the breathing.",critical:!0}],warnings:["Do not mistake occasional gasping for breathing: a person who is unresponsive and only gasping needs CPR, not the recovery position.","After a fall from a height or a blow to the head, neck or back, do not move the person or put them on their side, unless the place is unsafe or CPR is needed.","Give nothing to eat or drink to a person who is unconscious or drowsy: it can block the airway."],tips:["Know where the nearest AED and first-aid kit are on your floor.","Check the first-aid kit regularly and replace anything that has been used.","When two trained people are present, swap the compressions when the first one tires, with as short a pause as possible.","Wear the gloves from the kit when you deal with bleeding."],activity:{prompt:"A colleague collapses at his desk. He does not respond when you tap his shoulders and shout, and he is only gasping now and then. Another colleague is with you. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I put him on his side and wait for him to wake up",correct:!1,feedback:"Occasional gasping is not normal breathing; he needs CPR, not the recovery position."},{text:"I ask my colleague to call 122 and bring the AED, and I start chest compressions in the center of the chest",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: no response and no normal breathing mean cardiac arrest. Call, get the AED and start compressions at once."},{text:"I give him water to drink to wake him up",correct:!1,feedback:"Never give anything by mouth to an unresponsive person; it can block the airway."},{text:"I wait for the ambulance before touching him",correct:!1,feedback:"Every minute without compressions lowers his chance of survival. Start CPR now."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"A visitor falls down the stairs of your building and lies at the bottom. She does not respond to your voice or touch, but she is breathing normally. There is no fire, traffic or other danger. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I turn her onto her side in the recovery position",correct:!1,feedback:"After a fall a neck or back injury is possible, so the recovery position is not used."},{text:"I keep her still in the position I found her, call 122 and keep checking her breathing",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: do not move a person after a fall unless the place is unsafe or CPR is needed; watch her breathing until help arrives."},{text:"I sit her up against the wall so that she can breathe better",correct:!1,feedback:"Moving her can make a spinal injury worse. Keep her still."}]},{prompt:"While you wait, her breathing changes: she stops breathing normally and only gasps. The ambulance has not arrived yet. What do you do now?",options:[{text:"I roll her carefully onto her back on the floor, keeping the head, neck and back in line, and start chest compressions while a colleague brings the AED",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: CPR now comes first. Move her only as much as needed to lay her on her back on a firm surface."},{text:"I keep her still because of the possible spinal injury",correct:!1,feedback:"Without normal breathing she needs CPR; the need for CPR is the exception that allows moving her."},{text:"I give her two rescue breaths and then wait",correct:!1,feedback:"Breaths alone are not enough. Start compressions (30:2 if trained, compressions only if not) and continue."}]}],realCase:"During a morning meeting in a government office, a senior employee suddenly collapsed and stopped breathing normally. A colleague who had attended the office first-aid course sent one person to call 122 and another to bring the AED from the corridor, then started chest compressions. When the AED arrived, she switched it on, placed the pads on his bare chest and followed the voice prompts, while a second trained colleague took turns with her on the compressions. The ambulance team then took over. After the incident the office put up signs showing the way to the AED and trained more staff."},{id:17,code:"GOV-117",icon:"FlameKindling",accent:"#dc2626",title:"Classification of fire types",subtitle:"Fire classification — NFPA 10 / BS EN 2",summary:"The international classification of fire types and choosing the right fire extinguisher",blocks:[{body:"Fire classification is the foundation of Civil Defense work. NFPA 10 (the American standard) and EN 2 (the European standard) are the two main systems, and we must always state which system is meant. Classes here use NFPA 10 letters as the project reference; the official system for Duhok is not yet confirmed. EN 2 equivalents: NFPA A = EN A; NFPA B (liquids) = EN B; NFPA B (gases) = EN C; NFPA C (live electrical equipment) has no EN class; NFPA D = EN D; NFPA K = EN F. Choosing the wrong fire extinguisher can make a fire bigger or cause injuries. This lesson explains each class according to international standards."},{heading:"1. Class A — ordinary solid materials (solid combustibles)",body:"Organic solid materials that leave ash behind:",points:["Fuel: wood, paper, cloth, plastic, rubber, trash.","Auto-ignition temperature: paper about 218–246°C.","Characteristics: leaves ash and embers behind, and the fire can persist deep inside the material.","Fire extinguishers: water, ABC powder, foam — read the label and its class symbols (color is not the fire class).","In offices: most combustible materials are paper, files and room furnishings.","Standards: NFPA 10, EN 2.","Technique: use an extinguisher only if all of this is true: the fire is small and just starting, the extinguisher is the right type, your exit is behind you, the alarm has been raised and 115 called, you are trained, and no smoke or heat blocks your way out. Then PASS: start from the distance shown on the extinguisher label and aim the nozzle at the base of the fire; if the fire does not go out at once, get out and close the door."]},{heading:"2. Class B — flammable liquids",body:"Liquids that give off flammable vapors at normal temperatures (in NFPA, flammable gases are also included):",points:["Fuel: gasoline, diesel, kerosene, alcohol, thinner, paint.","Flash point (depends on the exact fuel): gasoline about −43°C, diesel about 52–96°C.","Characteristics: the vapor of the liquid burns, not the liquid itself.","Fire extinguishers for liquids: foam, CO₂ or ABC powder; for alcohol and other polar solvents: CO₂, ABC powder or alcohol-resistant (AR) foam, because ordinary foam breaks down on them. Gas fire (LPG, natural gas): the priority is to stop the gas; a qualified person closes the gas valve only if it is safe to reach. Do not put out a gas flame if the gas cannot be stopped safely: unburned gas can build up and explode. If it is not safe, get everyone out, raise the alarm and call 115. Foam is not the general choice for gas fires.","Do not spray water from a hose onto a burning liquid: it splashes and spreads the burning liquid (Slop-over / Boil-over). BLEVE is something different: it is the explosion of a pressurized container after it has been heated, and cooling the container with water from a distance can help prevent it; this is a task for the 115 team, and everyone else evacuates.","In offices: fuel storage for generators and service vehicles.","Standard: NFPA 30 (Flammable and Combustible Liquids Code).","Method: cutting off the oxygen (smothering), not just cooling."]},{heading:"3. Class C in NFPA — energized electrical equipment",body:"Fires in electrical equipment while it is energized:",points:["Fuel: computers, servers, outlets, electrical panels.","Arc flash temperature: reaches about 19,400°C (35,000°F) — about 4 times hotter than the surface of the sun (OSHA).","Fire extinguishers: CO₂, ABC powder, clean agents such as FK-5-1-12.","Do not use water or foam on live electrical equipment unless the label shows a Class C rating or an electrical-test marking (some water-mist units) — risk of fatal electric shock.","First, if it is safe to reach without passing the fire, switch off the power at the panel; then treat the fire by the burning material.","Standard: NFPA 70E (Electrical Safety in the Workplace).","In ICT rooms: clean agents are best — they do not damage valuable equipment.","Note: in EN 2, Class C is gases, not electricity; in Europe electrical fires are not a separate class, and Class E (electrical) is used only in Australia."]},{heading:"4. Class D — combustible metals",body:"Combustible metals — one of the most dangerous types:",points:["Fuel: magnesium, titanium, sodium, potassium, lithium metal.","Temperature: magnesium burns at about 3,100°C (5,610°F).","Never use water! Water + magnesium = hydrogen and explosion.","Never use CO₂! Magnesium burns even in CO₂ and the fire intensifies.","Only: an extinguisher or agent whose label lists that metal (Class D powders such as sodium chloride or graphite powder); the agent differs from metal to metal (lithium, for example, needs an agent listed for lithium). Never ordinary powder or foam. Dry sand only if the site fire plan or the metal's safety data sheet names it and it is completely dry.","In offices: chemical laboratories and metalworking areas. Ordinary lithium-ion batteries contain no lithium metal, and cooling with water is recommended for them.","Standard: NFPA 484 (Combustible Metals).","Call 115 immediately — a specialized team is required."]},{heading:"5. Class F (EN 2) / Class K (NFPA) — cooking oils",body:"Cooking oils and fats — one of the most common kitchen fires:",points:["Fuel: cooking oils (vegetable and animal) and fats.","Auto-ignition temperature: generally above 340°C, depending on the oil.","Extinguisher: wet chemical (potassium salts) — made for cooking oils and fats; many are also rated for Class A, so read the label and use it as the label shows (in the UK it has a yellow band, BS 7863).","Never use water! Water in hot oil turns to steam instantly and violently throws out the burning oil (a fireball).","ABC powder is not suitable for cooking oil — the fire can reignite.","Method: if the fire is small, turn off the heat, cover it with a lid or a fire blanket and do not remove it.","In the office cafeteria: a Wet Chemical fire extinguisher near the cooking area (NFPA 10: no more than 9 meters away).","Standard: BS EN 3-7, NFPA 10, NFPA 17A (Wet Chemical Systems)."]},{heading:"6. Choosing the right fire extinguisher — decision matrix",body:"Basis: 1) the burning material, 2) the size of the fire, 3) the location:",points:["Matrix: A: water or ABC | B liquids: foam, CO₂ or ABC (polar solvents: AR foam, CO₂ or ABC) | B gas: stop the gas first if it is safe; never put out a gas flame you cannot stop | C: CO₂, Clean Agent or ABC powder (powder damages electronics) | D: a Class D extinguisher listed for that metal | F/K: Wet Chemical.","Size: use a fire extinguisher only on a small fire that is just starting; do not use it if the fire is bigger than a wastebasket, is spreading or smoke is building up: get out and call 115.","Location: ICT rooms: Clean Agent or CO₂ | paper storerooms: water or ABC | cafeteria: Wet Chemical.","For a typical office: ABC and CO₂, plus Wet Chemical in the kitchen.","Discharge time: small extinguishers empty quickly, often in a few seconds, and the exact discharge time is on the label — be within the range shown on the label, with your exit behind you, before you start.","Inspection and service (NFPA 10 and OSHA; the rules for Duhok are not yet confirmed): visual check monthly; inspection and maintenance yearly by a qualified technician; recharge after any use; stored-pressure dry chemical: emptied and checked inside every 6 years; hydrostatic test every 5 years for water, foam, CO₂, Wet Chemical and stainless-steel dry chemical, every 12 years for stored-pressure dry chemical with mild steel, brazed brass or aluminum shells."]},{heading:"7. Stages of fire development",body:"Every fire goes through 4 stages:",points:["1. Incipient: little smoke and a small fire.","2. Growth: the heat increases and the fire spreads.","3. Fully developed: all materials in the room are burning and the temperature can exceed 1,000°C.","4. Decay: the fuel decreases, but there is a risk of Backdraft.","Flashover between stages 2 and 3: when the temperature of the upper gas layer reaches about 500–600°C, all materials in the room ignite rapidly.","Backdraft: opening a door to an oxygen-starved room with fire inside lets oxygen in and causes an explosive ignition.","Staff response: extinguishing a fire is safe only at stage 1 (Incipient)."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/gov_fire_classes.mp4",title:"Classification of fire types",description:"Showing the fire classes according to NFPA and EN 2"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/gov_fire_decision.png",title:"Decision matrix",description:"Choosing the right fire extinguisher"}],quiz:[{question:"Which fire extinguisher is used on a computer fire (Class C in NFPA)?",options:[{text:"Water",correct:!1},{text:"CO₂ or clean agent",correct:!0},{text:"Foam",correct:!1},{text:"Class D extinguisher",correct:!1}],explanation:"CO₂ or Clean Agent — they leave no residue. ABC powder also works but damages electronics. Ordinary water and foam conduct electricity and can cause electric shock."},{question:"What is Class D?",options:[{text:"Flammable liquids",correct:!1},{text:"Wood and paper",correct:!1},{text:"Combustible metals",correct:!0},{text:"Gases under pressure",correct:!1}],explanation:"Class D = combustible metals (magnesium, titanium...). Only an extinguisher or agent whose label lists that metal; dry sand only if the site fire plan or the metal's safety data sheet names it and it is completely dry."},{question:"Why must water not be used on a cooking oil fire?",options:[{text:"It only cools the pan, so the fire carries on burning slowly",correct:!1},{text:"The water turns to steam and violently throws out the burning oil",correct:!0},{text:"It is too heavy",correct:!1},{text:"The water mixes into the oil and makes the flames burn much longer",correct:!1}],explanation:"Very hot oil instantly turns water into steam; the steam throws out the burning oil and creates a fireball."},{question:"What is NFPA 10 the standard for?",options:[{text:"Classification of employees",correct:!1},{text:"Portable fire extinguishers",correct:!0},{text:"Water sprinkler systems",correct:!1},{text:"Data protection",correct:!1}],explanation:"NFPA 10 is the American standard for portable fire extinguishers: selection, installation, inspection and maintenance."},{question:"Which stage of fire is the only stage at which a staff member can safely extinguish the fire?",options:[{text:"Decay",correct:!1},{text:"Fully developed",correct:!1},{text:"Incipient",correct:!0},{text:"Flashover",correct:!1}],explanation:"Staff can safely extinguish a fire only at the initial (incipient) stage."},{question:"At about what temperature of the upper gas layer does flashover generally occur?",options:[{text:"About 200–300°C",correct:!1},{text:"About 300–400°C",correct:!1},{text:"About 500–600°C",correct:!0},{text:"About 800–900°C",correct:!1}],explanation:"Flashover occurs when the temperature of the upper gas layer reaches about 500–600°C — all materials in the room ignite rapidly."},{question:"A small spill of generator diesel is burning on the ground in the yard. The alarm has been raised and your exit is behind you. Which extinguisher do you choose?",options:[{text:"A water extinguisher whose label shows Class A only, because water cools any fire",correct:!1},{text:"Any red extinguisher, because a red body shows it is suitable for every class",correct:!1},{text:"One whose label shows Class B, such as foam or ABC powder, used from its label range",correct:!0},{text:"A Class D powder extinguisher, because it is the strongest agent for every fuel",correct:!1}],explanation:"Diesel is a flammable liquid, Class B. Choose by the class symbols on the label, not the color; a water stream spreads burning liquid, and Class D agents are only for the metals listed on their label."}],steps:[{title:"Raise the alarm first",detail:"Push the fire alarm, warn the people around you and make sure someone has called 115 before you think about an extinguisher.",critical:!0},{title:"Name what is burning",detail:"Ordinary solids such as paper and wood are Class A; flammable liquids are Class B; gases are Class B in NFPA and Class C in EN 2; live electrical equipment is NFPA Class C; metals are Class D; cooking oil is NFPA Class K (= EN Class F). If you cannot tell what is burning, do not fight it: get out."},{title:"Remove the source only if it is safe",detail:"Switch off the power at the panel only if you can reach it without passing the fire. A gas supply is stopped only by a qualified person and only if it is safe; never put out a gas flame that cannot be stopped.",critical:!0},{title:"Match the label to the class",detail:"Read the class symbols on the extinguisher label; the color is never the class. ABC powder covers A, B and C only, not D (metals) and not K/F (cooking oil); a Class D fire needs an agent whose label lists that metal."},{title:"Go or no-go",detail:"Use the extinguisher only if the fire is small and just starting, the extinguisher suits it, your exit is behind you, 115 has been called, you are trained and no smoke or heat blocks your way out. Start from the distance shown on the label. If any answer is no, or the fire does not go out at once, get out and close the door.",critical:!0}],warnings:["Never put water on burning cooking oil: the water turns to steam at once and throws the burning oil out over people.","Never use water or foam on live electrical equipment unless the label shows it is rated for it: the stream can carry a fatal electric shock.","Never put out a gas flame if the gas cannot be stopped safely: unburned gas builds up and can explode."],tips:["Walk your floor and note which classes could occur in each room: files and furniture (A), generator fuel (B), server and electrical rooms (NFPA C), the kitchen (NFPA K = EN F).","Check that the extinguisher in each area shows the class symbols for that area’s risk on its label.","When you call 115, say what is burning: it helps the team choose the right agent and protect themselves.","When a supplier or a document names a class, ask which system it uses: NFPA letters and EN 2 letters do not always mean the same thing."],activity:{prompt:"You are the trained fire warden of your floor. A pan of cooking oil has caught fire on the stove in the staff kitchen. The alarm has been raised, the fire is still small and the door is behind you. Within reach are a water extinguisher, an ABC powder extinguisher, a wet chemical extinguisher whose label shows Class K (= EN F) and a pan lid. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Use the water extinguisher, because it is the closest one to the stove",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: water on burning oil turns to steam at once and throws the burning oil over you and the room."},{text:"Turn off the heat if it is safe, then cover the pan with the lid or use the wet chemical extinguisher gently, as its label shows",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: cooking oil is NFPA Class K (= EN F). A lid or the wet chemical agent smothers and cools the oil without splashing it; if the fire does not go out at once, get out and close the door."},{text:"Use the ABC powder extinguisher, because ABC covers every kind of fire",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: ABC powder covers A, B and C only, not K/F; the hot oil can flare up again after the powder settles."},{text:"Carry the burning pan out of the kitchen to the open yard",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: moving a burning pan spills burning oil on you and on others along the way."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"Smoke and sparks are coming from a small electrical distribution board in the corridor of your directorate. A colleague is calling 115. A CO₂ extinguisher and a water extinguisher hang nearby, and the main switch for the floor is by the stairs, away from the smoke. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"Spray the water extinguisher into the board, because it holds the most agent",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the board is live (NFPA Class C); water can carry a fatal electric shock to you."},{text:"Switch off the floor’s power at the main switch, which you reach without passing the fire, then check go/no-go before using the CO₂ extinguisher",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: cut the power only when you can reach it safely, then decide; CO₂ is suitable for electrical equipment and leaves no residue."},{text:"Open the board’s cover to see exactly which cable is burning",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: opening the cover lets in air and brings your face and hands close to live parts and flames."}]},{prompt:"You used the CO₂ extinguisher from the distance shown on its label and the flames went out. A short time later, smoke starts again, the CO₂ extinguisher is empty and the corridor is filling with smoke. What now?",options:[{text:"Take the water extinguisher and go back to the board, since the power is now off",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the fire did not stay out and the smoke is growing, so the answer is no-go; do not fight it any longer."},{text:"Stay in the corridor and fan the smoke away with a folder until 115 arrives",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: smoke is toxic and fanning it gives the fire more air; you must leave."},{text:"Get out, close the doors behind you, make sure 115 is on the way and tell the team what burned and what you used",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: if the fire does not go out at once or comes back, get out and close the door. The 115 team needs to know it was an electrical board and that CO₂ was used."}]}],realCase:"Illustrative scenario: in a government office, smoke rose from a power strip under a desk piled with files. A staff member grabbed the nearest extinguisher, a water unit, but the floor’s fire warden stopped him: the strip was still live. The warden asked a colleague to raise the alarm and call 115, switched off the power at the floor’s main switch by the stairs, away from the smoke, and checked the label of a CO₂ extinguisher before using it on the small fire. The flames went out. The warden backed away, watched the papers in case they smoldered and told the 115 team what had burned. The lesson: name what is burning before you choose an extinguisher."},{id:18,code:"GOV-118",icon:"FireExtinguisher",accent:"#ea580c",title:"Fire extinguishers — types and selection",subtitle:"Fire extinguisher types — NFPA 10 + BS EN 3",summary:"The six main types of portable fire extinguishers according to international standards and how to choose them for offices",blocks:[{body:"A fire extinguisher in an office must be chosen according to the type of fire that could occur. For most offices, ABC powder and CO₂ together are the basis, plus Wet Chemical in the kitchen. This lesson explains the six main types, their colors, sizes, the PASS technique and the inspection schedule. Classes here use NFPA 10 letters as the project reference; the official system for Duhok is not yet confirmed. EN 2 equivalents: NFPA A = EN A; NFPA B (liquids) = EN B; NFPA B (gases) = EN C; NFPA C (live electrical equipment) has no EN class; NFPA D = EN D; NFPA K = EN F. Color is not the fire class: the colors below are the UK practice (BS EN 3 red body with a BS 7863 colored band); the color practice in Duhok is not yet confirmed, so always read the label and its class symbols."},{heading:"1. Water fire extinguisher (Class A)",body:"The most common fire extinguisher for Class A — solid materials:",points:["Color in the UK (BS EN 3): all red, no band.","Class: A only (wood, paper, cloth, plastic).","How it works: it cools the fire and reduces the heat.","Common sizes: 6 and 9 liters.","Distance: start from the distance shown on the extinguisher label, with your exit behind you, and move closer only as the fire gets smaller.","Never use it on electricity (shock), liquids or cooking oil (it spreads the fire).","In the office: corridors and archive rooms.","It is inexpensive, but suitable only for Class A.","Inspection: monthly visual check (pressure gauge if there is one, pin and seal) and yearly inspection and maintenance by a qualified person."]},{heading:"2. Foam fire extinguisher (Classes A and B)",body:"AFFF foam (Aqueous Film-Forming Foam):",points:["Color in the UK (BS EN 3 / BS 7863): red with a cream band.","Class: A and B (flammable liquids) — not for alcohol or other polar solvents unless it is alcohol-resistant (AR) foam, and not the general choice for gas fires.","How it works: the foam forms a layer on top of the liquid, cuts off the oxygen and cools.","Common sizes: 6 and 9 liters.","Distance: start from the distance shown on the label and move closer only as the fire gets smaller.","Suitable for: fuel storage areas and generator locations.","Do not use it on energized electrical equipment — like water, it conducts electricity.","In the office: the vehicle parking area and the diesel storage.","AFFF contains PFAS substances that harm the environment and health; fluorine-free foam is the new replacement."]},{heading:"3. CO₂ (carbon dioxide) fire extinguisher (Class B and electrical fires)",body:"Best for electrical equipment and liquids:",points:["Color in the UK (BS EN 3 / BS 7863): red with a black band.","Class: B and live electrical equipment (NFPA B and C; under EN 2, C means gas and CO₂ is not rated for gas fires).","How it works: CO₂ gas displaces the oxygen around the fire (smothering).","Common sizes: 2 and 5 kg.","Distance: CO₂ has a short range; start from the distance shown on the label and move closer only as the fire gets smaller.","Suitable for: ICT rooms, electrical panels, the server room.","It leaves no residue and does not damage valuable equipment; but it is not good for deep-seated Class A fires.","Hazard: in enclosed rooms it reduces the oxygen — after use, evacuate the room and ventilate it.","Its horn becomes extremely cold (about −78°C) — do not touch the horn, hold only the handle.","In the office: the ICT department, the server room and near electrical panels."]},{heading:"4. ABC dry chemical powder fire extinguisher",body:"The most common fire extinguisher — for Classes A, B and C, not for D and K/F:",points:["Color in the UK (BS EN 3 / BS 7863): red with a blue band.","Class: A, B and C — not D (metals) and not K/F (cooking oil).","Agent: monoammonium phosphate.","How it works: the powder stops the chemical reaction of the fire.","Common sizes: 1, 2, 6 and 9 kg.","Distance: start from the distance shown on the label and move closer only as the fire gets smaller.","Suitable for: corridors, garages and storerooms — not for cooking oil.","The powder spreads through the room, reduces visibility and is difficult to clean up.","The powder damages electronic equipment — for computers and ICT, CO₂ is better.","In the office: corridors, garages and near exits.","It is inexpensive and is commonly installed in offices."]},{heading:"5. Wet chemical fire extinguisher (Class F/K) — kitchen",body:"Designed specifically for hot cooking oils and fats:",points:["Color in the UK (BS EN 3 / BS 7863): red with a yellow band.","Class: K (NFPA) / F (EN 2) — cooking oil.","Agent: potassium salts (potassium acetate or potassium citrate).","How it works: it turns the oil into a soap-like layer (Saponification) that cools the oil and cuts off the oxygen.","Common sizes: 2 and 6 liters.","Use it as the label shows: apply it gently onto the oil with the long applicator so that the oil does not splash.","Made for cooking oils and fats (NFPA K / EN F); many are also rated for Class A, so read the label. Not for diesel or kerosene (Class B).","Never put water on hot oil — the water turns to steam and throws out the burning oil!","In the office: the cafeteria and kitchen.","Before use, if it is safe, cut off the gas or electricity to the cooking appliance."]},{heading:"6. Clean agent fire extinguishers (FK-5-1-12 / HFC-227ea)",body:"For ICT and valuable equipment:",points:["Color: red body with the name of the agent written on it (a green label in some countries).","Class: the rating depends on the model; read the label.","Agent: FK-5-1-12 (trade name Novec 1230) or HFC-227ea.","How it works: a clean gas — it leaves no residue and does not damage equipment.","Fixed clean agent systems (NFPA 2001), which are not portable extinguishers, discharge in 10 seconds or less.","Suitable for: the server room, data center, archives.","Portable clean agent extinguisher: the class rating and minimum room size are on the label; leave the room after use. Fixed systems: at design concentrations the agent is safe for people, but the room must be evacuated because of the smoke and products of combustion.","It does not damage the ozone layer and has replaced Halon 1301.","It is expensive; in server rooms it is often installed as a fixed total flooding system, which is separate from portable extinguishers.","In the office: only in sensitive areas — the server room and the Emergency Operations Center (EOC).","Standard: NFPA 2001 (Clean Agent Fire Extinguishing Systems)."]},{heading:"7. PASS — how to use a fire extinguisher",body:`Before you use an extinguisher (every answer must be yes):
• The fire is small and just starting, like a fire in a wastebasket or a pan.
• The extinguisher is the right type for this fire.
• Your exit is behind you and the fire is not between you and the exit.
• The alarm has been raised: others are warned and 115 has been called.
• You are trained to use the extinguisher.
• No smoke or heat is blocking your way out.
• If the fire does not go out at once, stop, get out and close the door behind you.

PASS is the general method for most hand-held extinguishers. Class D, wet chemical, clean agent and wheeled units: follow the label and the manufacturer's instructions. Step by step:`,points:["P = Pull: pull out the safety pin and break the seal.","A = Aim: point the nozzle at the base of the fire, not the top of the flames — start from the distance shown on the extinguisher label and move closer only as the fire gets smaller.","S = Squeeze: squeeze the handle firmly and steadily.","S = Sweep: from side to side, slowly, until the fire goes out.","Small extinguishers empty quickly, often in a few seconds; the exact discharge time is on the label — be prepared.","Where to stand: the exit route (door) must be behind you.","After extinguishing, keep watching the area; if the fire reignites, use PASS again or get out.","If the fire does not go out at once or the extinguisher is empty: get out immediately, close the door behind you and make sure 115 has been called."]},{heading:"8. The right choice for offices",body:"The office fire extinguisher plan:",points:["Example for an office: ABC in the corridors, CO₂ near electrical and ICT equipment, and Wet Chemical in the kitchen (if there is one).","Under NFPA 10, in a light (low) hazard area one A unit covers up to 3,000 ft² (about 279 m²), so a 2-A extinguisher covers up to about 557 m² (6,000 ft²); no single extinguisher may cover more than about 1,045 m² (11,250 ft²), and no one should have to walk more than 23 m (75 ft) to reach one. The UK code BS 5306-8 recommends at least two extinguishers on each floor (very small floors excepted). The requirement for Duhok offices is set by the Civil Defense Directorate and is not yet confirmed.","Travel distance: no more than 23 meters for Class A, 9 or 15 meters for Class B (depending on the extinguisher rating), 9 meters for Class K (NFPA 10).","On the wall and near doors; the top of the fire extinguisher no higher than 1.5 meters above the floor (for fire extinguishers up to 18 kg — NFPA 10).","Place the fire extinguisher sign (ISO 7010 F001) high up so that it can be seen from a distance.","Visual check (monthly, by a designated staff member): in its place, visible and easy to reach; pin and tamper seal intact; label readable and facing out; no damage, rust or leaks; hose and nozzle clear; pressure gauge in the green if there is one. CO₂ extinguishers have no gauge: lift one to feel that it is full.","Inspection and maintenance (yearly, by a qualified technician from an approved company).","Recharge: after any use, even a short one, by a qualified technician.","6-year maintenance: stored-pressure dry chemical (ABC) extinguishers that need a 12-year hydrostatic test are emptied and checked inside every 6 years (NFPA 10) — this is not replacement of the fire extinguisher.","Pressure test of the cylinder (Hydrostatic Test), by type: every 5 years for water, foam (AFFF), CO₂, Wet Chemical and stainless-steel dry chemical extinguishers; every 12 years for stored-pressure dry chemical (ABC) extinguishers with mild steel, brazed brass or aluminum shells. The rules for Duhok are not yet confirmed.","Removal from service: a fire extinguisher is taken out of service if it fails an inspection or test, is damaged, obsolete or recalled, has no readable label, or is a non-rechargeable unit past the service life on its label."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/gov_ext_pass.mp4",title:"The PASS technique",description:"Practicing the PASS technique"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/gov_ext_chart.png",title:"Fire extinguisher selection chart",description:"Selection according to the class of fire"}],quiz:[{question:"Which classes is an ABC fire extinguisher suitable for?",options:[{text:"A only",correct:!1},{text:"A + B + C",correct:!0},{text:"B + C only",correct:!1},{text:"D + K",correct:!1}],explanation:"ABC is for Classes A, B and C (monoammonium phosphate) — not for D (metals) and not for K/F (cooking oil)."},{question:"For a server room, which fire extinguisher is suitable?",options:[{text:"ABC dry powder for all fire types",correct:!1},{text:"Water",correct:!1},{text:"Clean agent (FK-5-1-12)",correct:!0},{text:"Foam",correct:!1}],explanation:"A clean agent leaves no residue and does not damage equipment."},{question:"What does P stand for in PASS?",options:[{text:"Push",correct:!1},{text:"Pull",correct:!0},{text:"Point",correct:!1},{text:"Press",correct:!1}],explanation:"P = Pull (pull out the safety pin)."},{question:"According to NFPA 10, the travel distance to a Class A fire extinguisher must not exceed how much?",options:[{text:"10 meters",correct:!1},{text:"23 meters",correct:!0},{text:"50 meters",correct:!1},{text:"100 meters",correct:!1}],explanation:"NFPA 10: up to 23 meters (75 ft) for Class A; for Class B, 9 or 15 meters depending on the extinguisher rating."},{question:"What is the approximate temperature of CO₂ as it leaves the fire extinguisher?",options:[{text:"0°C",correct:!1},{text:"−78°C",correct:!0},{text:"−30°C",correct:!1},{text:"20°C",correct:!1}],explanation:"CO₂ comes out at a temperature of about −78°C — do not touch the horn, so that your hand is not injured by the cold (frostbite)."},{question:"What is the hazard of AFFF foam?",options:[{text:"There is no hazard",correct:!1},{text:"PFAS — environmental damage",correct:!0},{text:"It is too expensive to use in training",correct:!1},{text:"It is very cold and freezes the skin",correct:!1}],explanation:"AFFF contains fluorinated substances (PFAS) that pose a risk to the environment; fluorine-free foam is the new replacement."},{question:"A colleague says that an extinguisher with a blue band works on every kind of fire. What is the correct reply?",options:[{text:"Yes: in every country a blue band means the extinguisher covers every class",correct:!1},{text:"No: read the class symbols on the label; the color never shows the class",correct:!0},{text:"No: a blue band always shows that the extinguisher is for electrical fires only",correct:!1},{text:"Yes, as long as the gauge needle is in the green and the pin is in place",correct:!1}],explanation:"The color is never the fire class. Even in UK practice the band only shows the agent (blue = powder), and ABC powder does not cover D (metals) or K/F (cooking oil). Always read the label."}],steps:[{title:"Know the risk of each area",detail:"List what could burn in each area: paper and furniture, generator fuel, electrical and ICT equipment, cooking oil in the kitchen."},{title:"Choose by the label, not the color",detail:"The class symbols on the label must match the area’s risk: for most offices ABC powder and CO₂ together, wet chemical in the kitchen, CO₂ or a clean agent for ICT rooms. Follow the requirements of Civil Defense for what your building must have."},{title:"Check every extinguisher each month",detail:"In its place, visible and unblocked; pin and tamper seal in place; the gauge needle in the green if there is a gauge; no damage. Report any fault. Yearly maintenance is done by a qualified person."},{title:"Before use: go or no-go",detail:"Use an extinguisher only if the fire is small and just starting, the extinguisher suits it, your exit is behind you, 115 has been called, you are trained and no smoke or heat blocks your way out.",critical:!0},{title:"Use PASS from the range on the label",detail:"Pull the pin, aim at the base of the fire, squeeze the handle and sweep. Start from the distance shown on the label and move closer only as the fire gets smaller; if it does not go out at once, get out and close the door.",critical:!0},{title:"After any use: report and replace",detail:"An extinguisher used even for a moment is taken out of service and recharged or replaced. After CO₂ is used in a small room, everyone leaves and the room is ventilated."}],warnings:["Never put water or foam on live electrical equipment unless the label shows it is rated for it: risk of a fatal electric shock.","Never use water or ABC powder on cooking oil: water throws the burning oil out, and ABC powder is not rated for Class K (= EN F).","Never hold the horn of a CO₂ extinguisher: it becomes extremely cold and can freeze the skin; hold only the handle."],tips:["Mount each extinguisher near the risk it covers and on the way out, so that you can use it with your exit behind you.","Keep labels facing outward and readable; never hide extinguishers behind doors, furniture or boxes.","Train staff on the type installed in their own area: CO₂ and powder behave differently in the hand.","Record each monthly check on the service tag or in the log, so that a missed check or a fault is noticed."],activity:{prompt:"You are asked to propose extinguishers for a new server room with equipment racks and a large UPS. The room is staffed during the day. What do you recommend?",options:[{text:"Water extinguishers, because they are inexpensive and easy to refill",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: water must not be used on live electrical equipment; it can give a fatal electric shock and ruin the equipment."},{text:"Only ABC powder extinguishers, because they are rated for Class C",correct:!1,feedback:"Not the best choice: powder is rated for electrical fires, but it spreads through the room and damages electronic equipment; CO₂ or a clean agent is better here."},{text:"CO₂ or clean agent extinguishers whose label shows they suit electrical equipment, mounted by the door, with the staff trained to use them",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: CO₂ and clean agents leave no residue on equipment. Mounting them by the door lets staff use them with the exit behind them, and leave at once after CO₂ is used."},{text:"Foam extinguishers, because foam smothers every kind of fire",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: foam conducts electricity like water and is not for live electrical equipment."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"During the monthly check you find that the ABC powder extinguisher in the corridor has its gauge needle in the red and its tamper seal broken. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Leave it on the wall, because it may still work in an emergency",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: a low gauge and a broken seal mean it may not work when it is needed; a fault must be reported."},{text:"Take it out of service, report it so it is recharged or replaced, and make sure the area is covered by another suitable extinguisher until then",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: a faulty extinguisher never stays on the wall as if it worked, and the area must not be left without one."},{text:"Shake it well and hang it back, because powder often settles",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: shaking does not restore lost pressure or a broken seal; it must be checked by a qualified person."}]},{prompt:"Before the replacement arrives, a small fire starts in a wastebasket full of paper in the same corridor. The alarm has been raised and 115 called. You can reach a water extinguisher at the stairs and a CO₂ extinguisher in the ICT room next door. Your exit is behind you. Which do you use?",options:[{text:"The water extinguisher, after the go/no-go check, from the range shown on its label",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: burning paper is Class A, and water cools it deep inside. Keep the exit behind you and watch for flare-up."},{text:"The faulty powder extinguisher, because it is the nearest one",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: you already know it may not work; a failed extinguisher in front of a fire puts you in danger."},{text:"The CO₂ extinguisher, because CO₂ is the best choice for every office fire",correct:!1,feedback:"Not the best choice: CO₂ can knock the flames down, but it is not good for deep-seated Class A fires and the paper can flare up again."},{text:"Carry the burning wastebasket outside to the street",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: carrying it spreads burning paper and puts the flames close to your body."}]}],realCase:"Illustrative scenario: during a review of a government building, the safety officer found that the staff kitchen had only an ABC powder extinguisher and the server room only a water extinguisher. Neither matched the risk of the room. The officer read every label, reported the gaps and, following the requirements of Civil Defense, arranged a wet chemical extinguisher for the kitchen and a CO₂ extinguisher for the server room, each mounted by the door. The staff of both rooms were shown how their own type works and that, after any use, an extinguisher is reported and recharged. The lesson: the right extinguisher is chosen by the risk and the label, not by habit."},{id:19,code:"GOV-119",icon:"HeartHandshake",accent:"#db2777",title:"First aid for office staff",subtitle:"Workplace first aid — primary check + CPR + AED + common emergencies",summary:"The complete first aid program for office employees",blocks:[{body:"In an emergency, the first minutes are critical: if the heart stops, brain damage begins after a few minutes, and every minute of delay in CPR and an AED shock reduces the chance of survival. This lesson explains the primary check, CPR in the office, the AED, choking, bleeding, burns and other common emergencies."},{heading:"1. The primary check — danger, response, breathing",body:"The first procedure after finding a person who has collapsed or is unresponsive:",points:["Danger: before you approach, check the scene: electricity, fire, gas, traffic? Your safety comes first.",'Response: gently shake their shoulders and ask in a loud voice: "Are you all right?"',"If they do not respond: shout for help, call 122 (put the phone on speaker) and send someone to bring an AED.","Breathing: for up to 10 seconds, look at the chest to see whether it rises and falls normally.","If they are not breathing, or there are only abnormal gasping breaths (Gasping): this is cardiac arrest — start chest compressions immediately.","Lay rescuers (non-medical) do not check the pulse — do not waste time checking.","If they are breathing normally and no neck or back injury is suspected: place them on their side (Recovery Position), give nothing by mouth and monitor their breathing until the ambulance arrives."]},{heading:"2. CPR in the office",body:"For employees (non-medical):",points:["Hand position: center of the chest (lower half of the breastbone), one hand on top of the other.","Keep your arms straight with your shoulders directly over the chest; push using the strength of your hips and body.","Depth: 5–6 cm for all adults.","Rate: 100–120 compressions per minute; after each compression, let the chest recoil fully.","Ratio: 30 compressions + 2 breaths; if you have not been trained, give chest compressions only (Hands-Only CPR).","If there are two people: switch places every 2 minutes (5 cycles) so that the force of the compressions does not decrease.","Do not stop until: an AED arrives and is working, the medical team arrives or the person clearly regains consciousness.","Common mistakes: insufficient depth, insufficient rate, long pauses and not letting the chest recoil."]},{heading:"3. AED — automated external defibrillator",body:"It is recommended that every large government building have one:",points:["Location: in a public, visible corridor, so that it can be reached quickly.","Sign: a cabinet with the AED sign (ISO 7010 E010) and clear lighting.","Goal: the AED should reach any location in the building within about 3 minutes.","Steps: 1) switch it on, 2) place the two pads on the bare chest, 3) follow the voice prompts, 4) if it advises a shock, press the button.",'Before the shock, say out loud "Stand clear!" — no one may touch the person.',"Monthly inspection: the battery and the expiration date of the pads.","After the shock: immediately perform 2 minutes of CPR, then the AED analyzes again.","After use: install new pads and batteries so that the device is ready for the next time."]},{heading:"4. Choking",body:"If something is stuck in a person's throat:",points:["Check: can they speak, cough or breathe?","If they are coughing forcefully: encourage them to keep coughing and stay with them.","If they cannot speak, cough or breathe: call 122 (or have someone else call) and start immediately.","5 back blows: lean them forward and strike firmly between the shoulder blades with the heel of your hand.","5 abdominal thrusts (Heimlich): stand behind them, place your fist above the navel and well below the breastbone, grasp it with your other hand and thrust quickly inward and upward.","Repeat 5 back blows and 5 abdominal thrusts until the object comes out; if they become unconscious, start CPR.","Infants under 1 year old: 5 back blows (head lower than the chest) + 5 chest thrusts with the heel of one hand; never abdominal thrusts.","A pregnant woman or a person whose abdomen your arms cannot reach around: chest thrusts instead of abdominal thrusts."]},{heading:"5. Stopping bleeding (Stop the Bleed)",body:"The three main Stop the Bleed techniques:",points:["1. Direct pressure: place a clean cloth on the wound and press firmly with both hands without stopping.","2. Wound packing: for deep wounds (thigh, armpit, neck), pack clean gauze into the wound and press firmly.","3. Tourniquet: for life-threatening bleeding from an arm or leg, apply early; 5–7 cm above the wound, not over a joint.","Write down the time the tourniquet was applied and never loosen or remove it — only the medical team removes it.","Never remove an embedded object from a wound (the bleeding will get worse).","If an object is embedded: wrap cloth around it so that it does not move, and apply pressure around it, not on it.","Head wound: apply gentle pressure; if a skull fracture is suspected, do not apply firm pressure.","Eye wound: do not press on the eye — only cover it loosely with a clean cloth.","For severe bleeding, call 122 immediately; if the cloth becomes soaked with blood, do not remove it — place another one on top."]},{heading:"6. Burns",body:"Burn degrees and total body surface area burned (TBSA):",points:["First degree (superficial): only redness and pain — like sunburn.","Second degree (partial thickness): blisters and severe pain.","Third degree (full thickness): the skin becomes white, brown or black; there may be no pain because the nerves have been burned.","The Rule of Nines (Rule of 9s) for adults: head 9%, each arm 9%, each leg 18%, chest and abdomen 18%, back 18%, genitals 1%.","Immediately cool with cool running water for 20 minutes (not ice, not very cold water).","Then cover loosely with plastic wrap (Cling Film) or a clean non-stick cloth.","Chemical burns: rinse with running water for at least 20 minutes and check the SDS sheet.","Electrical burns: first cut off the power; there is an entry wound and an exit wound, and the internal damage may be greater.","Never: ice, oil, cream or toothpaste; do not burst blisters and do not pull off clothing that is stuck to the skin.","Call 122 if: the burn is large or deep, it is on the face, hands, feet or genitals, it is an electrical burn, or the person has inhaled smoke."]},{heading:"7. Other common emergencies",body:"Common situations in offices:",points:["Fainting: lay them on their back, if they are not injured raise their legs about 30 cm, and let fresh air into the room.","Diabetic emergency: if they are conscious and able to swallow, give them sugar or a sweet drink. If they are unconscious: give nothing by mouth, place them on their side and call 122. Give glucagon only if a doctor has prescribed it for them and you have been trained to use it.","Panic attack: speak calmly with them and help them breathe slowly; breathing into a paper bag is no longer recommended.","Head injury: if a spinal injury is suspected, do not move them unless the place is unsafe or they need CPR (then keep the head, neck and back in line), and do not put them in the recovery position; monitor their consciousness and breathing and call 122.","Fracture: splint the limb in the position it was found (Splint), do not straighten it, apply ice wrapped in cloth for the pain and call 122.","Electric shock: cut off the power, perform CPR if they are not breathing; everyone must have a heart check (ECG) at the hospital.","Poisoning: 1) which substance? 2) how much? 3) when? — call 122, take the substance's container with you and do not make them vomit.","Snake bite: keep the person calm and still and immobilize the limb with a splint; do not tie anything, do not cut and do not suck the blood; call 122 immediately."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/gov_cpr_workplace.mp4",title:"Workplace CPR",description:"CPR in the workplace"},{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/gov_aed_demo.mp4",title:"AED demonstration",description:"Using an AED"}],quiz:[{question:"What is the first step in the primary check?",options:[{text:"Start CPR immediately",correct:!1},{text:"Check for danger",correct:!0},{text:"Call 122",correct:!1},{text:"AED",correct:!1}],explanation:"Before anything else, make sure the scene is safe for you — if you are injured, you cannot help."},{question:"In CPR, what is the compression depth for adults?",options:[{text:"2–3 cm",correct:!1},{text:"5–6 cm",correct:!0},{text:"8–10 cm",correct:!1},{text:"None of these",correct:!1,fixed:!0}],explanation:"5–6 cm for all adults (AHA 2025)."},{question:"If an AED shock is delivered within the first 3–5 minutes after collapse, how high can the chance of survival reach?",options:[{text:"Up to 10–20%",correct:!1},{text:"Up to 50–70%",correct:!0},{text:"Up to 20–30%",correct:!1},{text:"Up to 90–100%",correct:!1}],explanation:"An early shock within 3–5 minutes can raise the survival rate to 50–70%; every minute of delay reduces the chance significantly."},{question:"For an infant under 1 year old who is choking (and conscious), what should be done?",options:[{text:"Abdominal thrusts (Heimlich) immediately",correct:!1},{text:"5 back blows + 5 chest thrusts",correct:!0},{text:"Just leave them",correct:!1},{text:"CPR immediately",correct:!1}],explanation:"5 back blows and 5 chest thrusts with the heel of one hand — never abdominal thrusts for infants (AHA 2025)."},{question:"After applying a tourniquet, what must you do?",options:[{text:"Loosen it every 6 hours",correct:!1},{text:"Write down the time it was applied and never loosen it",correct:!0},{text:"Remove it after 10 minutes",correct:!1},{text:"Loosen it every 20 minutes to let blood reach the limb",correct:!1}],explanation:"Write down the time it was applied (Stop the Bleed). The tourniquet stays in place until the medical team removes it; loosening it restarts the bleeding."},{question:"With what kind of water should a burn be cooled for 20 minutes?",options:[{text:"Warm water",correct:!1},{text:"Cool running water, not ice",correct:!0},{text:"Ice",correct:!1},{text:"Ointment or toothpaste, not water",correct:!1}],explanation:"Cool running water — not warm water (it makes the burn deeper) and not ice (it damages the skin)."},{question:"A colleague has fallen down the stairs. He is lying at the bottom, awake, and says his neck hurts. The stairwell is safe. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Keep him still as you found him, call 122 and watch his breathing",correct:!0},{text:"Help him sit up slowly and walk to a chair where he can rest",correct:!1},{text:"Roll him onto his side in the recovery position straight away",correct:!1},{text:"Lift his head on a folded coat so that he can breathe more easily",correct:!1}],explanation:"After a fall with neck pain, a neck or back injury is suspected: do not move him unless the place is unsafe or CPR is needed, and do not use the recovery position. Call 122 and keep checking his breathing."}],steps:[{title:"Check for danger and response",detail:'Before you approach, check for electricity, fire, gas or traffic. Then tap the person’s shoulders and ask loudly: "Are you all right?"',critical:!0},{title:"Shout, call 122 and send for the AED",detail:"If there is no response, shout for help, call 122 with the phone on speaker and send someone to bring the nearest AED.",critical:!0},{title:"Check breathing for up to 10 seconds",detail:"Look at the chest. No breathing, or only gasping, means cardiac arrest: start CPR at once. Lay rescuers do not check the pulse.",critical:!0},{title:"Push hard and fast in the center of the chest",detail:"Lower half of the breastbone, arms straight, 5–6 cm deep, 100–120 compressions a minute, letting the chest come back up fully. Trained: 30 compressions and 2 breaths; untrained: compressions only.",critical:!0},{title:"Use the AED as soon as it arrives",detail:"Switch it on and follow the voice prompts; pads on the bare chest; make sure nobody touches the person during the analysis and the shock; continue CPR straight after.",critical:!0},{title:"If the person breathes normally",detail:"If no neck or back injury is suspected, place them on their side in the recovery position, give nothing by mouth and keep checking their breathing until the ambulance arrives."}],warnings:["Do not mistake gasping for breathing and do not wait to see if the person recovers: every minute without CPR lowers the chance of survival.","Do not move a person after a fall or a blow to the head, neck or back unless the place is unsafe or CPR is needed.","Never put ice, oil, cream or toothpaste on a burn: cool it with cool running water for 20 minutes."],tips:["Know where the nearest AED and first-aid kit are on your floor, and who the trained first aiders are.","Put the phone on speaker so that you can follow the 122 call taker’s instructions while you help.","With two rescuers, swap every 2 minutes so that the compressions stay deep and fast.","After an incident, restock the first-aid kit and make sure the AED gets new pads and is ready again."],activity:{prompt:'In a meeting, a colleague suddenly slumps in his chair and does not respond when you tap his shoulders. His chest makes occasional gasping movements. Another colleague says: "He is breathing, let us wait a moment." What do you do?',options:[{text:"Wait a minute and give him some water when he opens his eyes",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: he is not breathing normally, and an unresponsive person is never given anything by mouth."},{text:"Shout for help, call 122 on speaker, send someone for the AED, lay him on the floor and start chest compressions",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: occasional gasping is not normal breathing; it is a sign of cardiac arrest. Early CPR and an early AED shock give the best chance of survival."},{text:"Put him in the recovery position and watch his breathing until the ambulance comes",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the recovery position is only for a person who is breathing normally; gasping means CPR now."},{text:"Check his pulse at the neck for a full minute before deciding",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: lay rescuers do not check the pulse; it wastes time and is often wrong."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"At lunch in the staff canteen, a colleague suddenly grabs her throat. She cannot speak, cough or breathe. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"Pat her back gently and give her a glass of water to wash it down",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: water can make it worse and gentle pats do not move a blockage; she needs firm back blows and abdominal thrusts."},{text:"Have someone call 122, then give 5 firm back blows and 5 abdominal thrusts, and repeat until the object comes out",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: she cannot speak, cough or breathe, so the airway is blocked. Fist above the navel and well below the breastbone for the thrusts."},{text:"Wait beside her and encourage her to keep coughing on her own",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: encouraging coughing is right only when the person can cough forcefully; she cannot."}]},{prompt:"Despite several rounds of back blows and abdominal thrusts, she becomes unresponsive and sinks to the floor. A colleague is on the phone with 122. What now?",options:[{text:"Keep giving abdominal thrusts while she lies on the floor",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: when a choking person becomes unresponsive, the sequence changes to CPR."},{text:"Place her in the recovery position and wait for the ambulance",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: she is unresponsive after choking and may not be breathing; the recovery position does not treat that."},{text:"Lower her safely to the floor, start CPR at once and send someone for the AED",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: if a choking person becomes unresponsive, start CPR. The compressions can also help move the object."}]}],realCase:"Illustrative scenario: during a morning meeting in a government directorate, an employee collapsed and did not respond. One colleague called 122 on speaker and another ran for the AED in the corridor. A staff member who had attended first-aid training saw that the man was only gasping and started chest compressions at once, pushing hard and fast in the center of the chest. When the AED arrived, they followed its voice prompts, made sure nobody touched him during the shock and went straight back to compressions until the ambulance team took over. Afterwards the office restocked the kit and replaced the AED pads. The lesson: gasping is not breathing; start CPR without delay."},{id:20,code:"GOV-120",icon:"Siren",accent:"#2563eb",title:"Civil Defense operations and coordination",subtitle:"Civil Defense operations + ICS + multi-agency response",summary:"The role of the office during a Civil Defense (115) response and coordination with other agencies",blocks:[{body:"Offices must know how to work with Civil Defense (115) during emergencies. The NFPA 1660 standard (into which NFPA 1600 has been consolidated) and the FEMA ICS-100/200 courses are the international foundations. Under ICS, one Incident Commander gives the orders, and all medical, police and firefighting teams and the office work under this command."},{heading:"1. The Incident Command System (ICS)",body:"A unified standard for all agencies:",points:["The ICS-100 and ICS-200 courses are required for key staff.","Incident Commander (IC): only one, with overall responsibility for the incident.","Five sections: Command, Operations, Planning, Logistics, Finance and Administration (Finance/Admin).","Span of control: 3–7 people for each supervisor (5 is optimal).","Every incident is given its own name (date + location + type).","Plain language: do not use codes or special abbreviations.","An Incident Action Plan (IAP) for each operational period, which is generally 12 or 24 hours.","If several agencies are responsible: Unified Command — the commanders of the agencies develop one plan together."]},{heading:"2. Calling 115 — what to say",body:"Stay calm, speak clearly and say these things in this order:",points:["1. The exact location first: the building and the name of the office, the street, a nearby landmark and the floor.","2. What happened: fire, explosion, accident, poisoning.","3. How many people are hurt or trapped.","4. Any danger: fire spreading, gas, electricity, chemicals.","5. Your name and your phone number.","6. Answer the questions, follow the instructions and do not hang up until they tell you."]},{heading:"3. Multi-agency response",body:"When several agencies arrive:",points:["115 Civil Defense: firefighting, rescue, hazardous materials (HazMat).","122 Ambulance: medical treatment and transport.","104 Police: securing the perimeter and directing traffic.","Electricity department: cutting off the power.","Water and gas departments: shutting off water and gas if there is a leak.","Ministry of Health: preparing the hospitals.","Environmental authority: chemical spills.","Emergency Operations Center (EOC) for major incidents."]},{heading:"4. The role of the office during an emergency",body:"What you must do until 115 arrives:",points:["1. Call 115 (and 122 if there are injured people) and give the location first.","2. Activate the internal alert (fire alarm or public address system).","3. Staff at the assembly point — headcount (Accountability).","4. Have the building floor plan ready: hazards and the electricity, gas and water shut-offs.","5. Liaison Officer: only one person communicates with 115.","6. Have the SDS sheets and contact lists ready.","7. Traffic control: keep the route open for emergency vehicles.","8. Public Information Officer (PIO): providing information to the public and the media in an organized way.","9. Documentation: photos, videos and witness statements.","10. Transfer of command: when 115 arrives, command is handed over to the 115 commander."]},{heading:"5. The office emergency response team (ERT)",body:"Recommended composition:",points:["Incident Commander (IC): the director or deputy director.","Alarm coordinator: the fire alarm button and communications.","Floor wardens: 2 on every floor, for the evacuation routes.","First aid team: 2–3 employees trained in CPR and AED use.","Search team: checks the rooms and confirms that the building has been evacuated.","Fire extinguisher team: employees trained to extinguish small fires at the initial stage.","Communications: telephone contact with 115 and internal coordination.","Training: initial training and annual refresher."]},{heading:"6. Drills and testing (drills + tabletop exercises)",body:"Types of exercises (FEMA HSEEP):",points:["Evacuation drill: according to this program, once every 3 months.","Tabletop exercise: managers and officers discuss a scenario around a table.","Functional exercise: only the teams and key functions (communications, command) are tested.","Full-scale exercise: the whole building and external agencies, at least once a year.","Write the After-Action Report (AAR) promptly.","Goal: identify what went well, what fell short and what needs to change.","Continuous improvement: incorporate the AAR findings into the standard operating procedures (SOP)."]},{heading:"7. The communication plan",body:"Without communication, nothing works properly:",points:["Primary: office telephones and the public address system (PA).","Secondary: cell phones and two-way radios.","Tertiary: sending people on foot (Runners).","Backup: satellite phone for major incidents.","Testing: every week for the key teams.","Telephone list: available 24/7 and kept up to date.","For the public: telephone hotline, website and social media — only through the PIO.","Note: in major incidents, the mobile network may become congested — SMS messages generally get through better."]},{heading:"8. After-action review",body:"The post-incident review:",points:["Recommendation: within 72 hours after every incident, while memories are still fresh.","Participation of all parties: IC, operations, logistics and external agencies.","4 questions: What was planned? What happened? Why was there a difference? What will we change next time?","Metrics: response time, number of injured, property damage.","Documentation: a Lessons Learned report.","Updating: SOPs, the Emergency Operations Plan (EOP) and training materials.","Follow-up: check implementation after 30, 60 and 90 days.","Sharing: share the findings with other agencies."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/gov_ics_demo.mp4",title:"ICS in action",description:"ICS in a real disaster"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/gov_salute_card.png",title:"115 call checklist card",description:"Reminder card: what to say when you call 115"}],quiz:[{question:"In ICS, how many Incident Commanders (IC) are needed?",options:[{text:"3–5",correct:!1},{text:"Only one",correct:!0},{text:"Every employee",correct:!1},{text:"2–4",correct:!1}],explanation:"In ICS there is one Incident Commander. If several agencies are responsible, a Unified Command works under one shared plan."},{question:"When you call 115, what do you say first?",options:[{text:"Your full name, your job title and the name of your department",correct:!1},{text:"The exact location: the building, the street, a nearby landmark and the floor",correct:!0},{text:"When the incident started",correct:!1},{text:"What equipment you have on site: extinguishers, fire hose reels and first aid kits",correct:!1}],explanation:"Say the exact location first. Then say what happened, how many people are hurt or trapped, any danger, and your name and phone number."},{question:"When 115 arrives, who takes over command?",options:[{text:"The ERT continues",correct:!1},{text:"The 115 commander",correct:!0},{text:"The liaison officer",correct:!1},{text:"The police",correct:!1}],explanation:"Transfer of command: when 115 arrives, command is handed over to the 115 commander and the ERT assists them."},{question:"What is a tabletop exercise?",options:[{text:"A full-building evacuation drill with every member of staff taking part",correct:!1},{text:"Discussing a scenario around a table with the officers in charge",correct:!0},{text:"Testing the siren",correct:!1},{text:"Training in using fire extinguishers",correct:!1}],explanation:"A tabletop exercise is a discussion of a scenario without moving people or equipment, in order to test plans and roles (FEMA HSEEP)."},{question:"Within how many hours is it recommended to conduct the After-Action Review (AAR)?",options:[{text:"24",correct:!1},{text:"48",correct:!1},{text:"72",correct:!0},{text:"168",correct:!1}],explanation:"Within 72 hours — while memories are still fresh and complete."},{question:"What is the span of control in ICS?",options:[{text:"2 people",correct:!1},{text:"3–7 people",correct:!0},{text:"10+ people",correct:!1},{text:"Unlimited",correct:!1}],explanation:"According to NIMS: 3–7 people for each supervisor, and 5 is optimal."},{question:"The 115 commander arrives and asks who is in charge. The director, the security chief and two floor wardens all start talking at once. What should happen?",options:[{text:"Each of them gives the commander their own report, so that nothing is left out",correct:!1},{text:"The security chief briefs the commander, because he knows the building best",correct:!1},{text:"The director keeps command and tells the 115 teams where to go in the building",correct:!1},{text:"The named liaison officer briefs the commander, and command passes to the 115 commander",correct:!0}],explanation:"One voice speaks for the office: the named liaison officer gives a short briefing (headcount, missing people, hazards, shut-offs) and command is handed over to the 115 commander. Several reports at once cause confusion."}],steps:[{title:"Call 115, location first",detail:"Give the exact location first (building, office, street, landmark, floor), then what happened, how many are hurt or trapped and any dangers. Call 122 as well if people are injured. Do not hang up until you are told to.",critical:!0},{title:"Raise the alarm and evacuate by the plan",detail:"Activate the internal alarm or public address system; floor wardens lead staff and visitors out by the planned routes to the assembly point.",critical:!0},{title:"Count everyone at the assembly point",detail:"Each warden counts their people and reports at once: all present, or the names of anyone missing and where they were last seen.",critical:!0},{title:"Prepare for the 115 team",detail:"The liaison officer gets ready the floor plan, the locations of the electricity, gas and water shut-offs, the hazards in the building, the SDS sheets and the contact list."},{title:"Keep the way in clear",detail:"Staff keep the access road and the entrance free for fire engines and ambulances and keep onlookers back."},{title:"Hand over command and support",detail:"When 115 arrives, the liaison officer briefs the 115 commander and command passes to them; the office then supports and follows their instructions, and later holds an after-action review."}],warnings:["Never send staff back into the building to search for someone: give the names and last-known places to the 115 team.","Do not let several people give 115 different information: one liaison officer speaks for the office.","Do not post photos or details of the incident on social media: only the public information officer informs the public, in an organized way."],tips:["Keep a ready folder for the liaison officer: floor plans, shut-off locations, the hazard list, SDS sheets, the staff list and contact numbers.","Practice the 115 call and the handover to the 115 commander in tabletop exercises.","Mark the assembly point clearly and keep cars from parking on the route that emergency vehicles will use.","Plan a back-up way to pass messages when phones fail, such as two-way radios or runners."],activity:{prompt:"The fire alarm is sounding in your directorate and you are the named liaison officer. Staff are at the assembly point and the first Civil Defense vehicles stop at the gate. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Stay at the assembly point and wait for the 115 team to come and find you",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the team needs information at once; the liaison officer goes to meet them at the agreed point."},{text:"Ask the 115 commander to wait until the director arrives to brief them",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: any delay costs time; the named liaison officer briefs the commander straight away."},{text:"Meet the 115 commander at the agreed point with the floor plan, the headcount, any missing people, the hazards and the shut-off locations",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: this is the liaison officer’s role. A short, clear briefing helps the team plan the search and stay safe."},{text:"Lead the firefighters into the building yourself to show them the way",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: you have no protective equipment or training for a burning building; give them the plan and the information instead."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"You are the director, and until 115 arrives you are the incident commander. There is a loud bang and smoke from the basement generator room, and several staff on the ground floor are injured. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"Make sure 115 and 122 are called with the location first, raise the alarm and have the wardens evacuate everyone by the plan",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: calling for help, warning everyone and getting people out come first. The injured are helped away from the danger and 122 is told how many there are."},{text:"Go down to the basement yourself to see what exploded",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the basement may hold fire, smoke, fuel or another explosion; you are needed to lead from a safe place."},{text:"Call the media office first so that the public hears the news from you",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: information for the public comes later and only through the public information officer; lives come first."}]},{prompt:"At the assembly point, a floor warden reports that two staff members from the archive are missing. The 115 commander has just arrived. What now?",options:[{text:"Send two volunteers back into the building quickly to look in the archive",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: untrained people going back into smoke can become more victims; searching is the job of the 115 team."},{text:"Give the 115 commander the two names, where they were last seen, the basement hazards such as fuel and the generator, and hand over command",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: exact information about missing people and hazards lets 115 search quickly and safely. Command now passes to the 115 commander."},{text:"Wait to see whether the two staff members come out on their own before saying anything",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: waiting loses time that the missing people may not have; report it at once."}]}],realCase:"Illustrative scenario: smoke filled a corridor of a government directorate after an electrical fault. The alarm was raised, the wardens led staff to the assembly point and the named caller gave 115 the location first. During the headcount, one employee was missing. Nobody went back in: the liaison officer met the 115 commander at the gate with the floor plan, the shut-off locations and the missing person’s name and office. The team found him in an upper-floor toilet, where he had not heard the alarm. At the after-action review, the office added visual alarms and a check of toilets to the wardens’ route. The lesson: count everyone, never send staff back in, give 115 exact information."},{id:21,code:"GOV-121",icon:"School",accent:"#0e7490",title:"School staff: emergency management and teaching the safety lessons",subtitle:"The school emergency plan, drills, pupils who need help, reunification with families and teaching by age",summary:"What principals, teachers and other school staff do before, during and after an emergency, and how they use the lessons in this app with each age group.",blocks:[{body:"A school holds many children who depend on adults to keep them safe. Every school needs a written emergency plan that all staff know, drills that test the plan and lessons that teach the children what to do. This lesson is for principals, teachers and other school staff. Follow the requirements of Civil Defense and the Ministry of Education. Local rules, such as how often drills must be held, are set by the Ministry of Education and are not yet confirmed, so this lesson gives no number for them."},{heading:"1. The school emergency plan",body:"Write the plan with the staff, keep copies in the office and with the person in charge of each floor, and update it whenever staff, rooms or pupils' needs change. It answers these questions:",points:["Who leads: the principal, or a named deputy when the principal is absent, takes charge until Civil Defense arrives, then hands over to the Civil Defense commander.","Who calls: one named person, with a back-up, calls 115 for fire, collapse, gas or flood and 122 for injured or sick people, and gives the school's location first.",'The alarm: one signal that every class knows means "leave the building now", and the names of the staff who sound it.',"Routes: a main and an alternative way out of every room, always kept clear; exit doors open from the inside without a key.","Assembly points: a main and an alternative point outside, away from the building, glass, power lines and the road that Civil Defense vehicles and ambulances will use.","Roles: each class teacher leads their own class; named staff check the toilets, laboratories, library and yard; named staff help the pupils who need it.","Shut-offs: named adults who know where the gas and electricity shut-offs are. Gas is shut off only if the valve is close and can be reached without going into the area where the gas has collected; otherwise everyone gets out and 115 is called from outside."]},{heading:"2. Roll call and the drill log",body:"A quick, complete count is how you know whether anyone is still inside:",points:["Each teacher takes the class list when leaving and counts the pupils at the assembly point.","Count visitors, kitchen and cleaning staff and other workers too.","Report to the person in charge at once: all present, or the name of anyone missing and where they were last seen.","Never send a pupil or a teacher back inside to look for someone: tell the Civil Defense team, who will search.","Nobody goes back inside until Civil Defense or the person in charge says it is safe.","After every drill, write in the drill log: the date and time, the type of drill, how long it took to leave and to finish the count, who was missing or late, the problems found, and who will fix them by when.","Practice the alternative route too: the plan must say how to leave when the main route cannot be used. How often drills are held is set by the Ministry of Education."]},{heading:"3. Pupils who need help to leave",body:"Plan for each of these pupils before an emergency, together with the pupil and the family:",points:["A personal evacuation plan for each pupil who cannot leave alone or quickly: for example a wheelchair user, a pupil who is blind or deaf, or a pupil with a health condition or a learning difficulty.","Named helpers (buddies): an adult and a back-up for each of these pupils, in every lesson and at break; a classmate may walk with them, but children never carry anyone.","Carrying a pupil down the stairs is done only by trained staff; elevators are not used in a fire unless they are designed and marked for evacuation.","A pupil who cannot use the stairs waits with an adult in the safe place the plan names, and the adult tells 115 where they are.","Pupils who cannot hear the alarm: a flashing light or a named person who tells them; a written card works better than shouting.","Plan how the pupil's own aids go out with them, such as a wheelchair, a hearing aid, or the pupil's own inhaler or adrenaline auto-injector.","Practice the plan in drills; the pupil and the family usually know best what help is needed."]},{heading:"4. Laboratories, heaters and the kitchen",body:"Most school fires can be prevented with simple rules:",points:["Laboratory: pupils never work without a teacher present; the teacher knows where the fire blanket, extinguishers, eyewash and first-aid kit are and how to use them.","Never leave a lit burner unattended; turn off heating equipment and gas valves when they are not in use; keep flammable solvents away when a flame is lit.","Keep a chemical inventory, lock chemicals away and store flammable liquids in an approved flammable-liquid cabinet.","Classroom heaters: in good condition, on a firm floor, at least 1 meter (3 feet) from curtains, paper and bags, away from doors and walkways, never left on in an empty room, and turned off at the end of the day.","Fuel heaters: only the fuel the maker names, refueled outdoors after they have cooled; a heater without a flue needs fresh air in the room, and if people get headaches or feel dizzy or sleepy, take everyone outside and call 122.","School kitchen: never leave cooking unattended; a pan fire is covered with a lid or a fire blanket and the heat is turned off if it is safe, and never water."]},{heading:"5. Reuniting pupils with their families",body:"When pupils cannot go back to class after an evacuation, hand them over to their families in an organized way:",points:["Tell families in advance how reunification works: where to go, what to bring and that children are handed over only at that place.","Hand a child only to a parent or another adult named in the school records, after checking their identity, and write down who took which child and when.","Keep the place where parents check in separate from the pupils' waiting area, and keep both away from the road used by emergency vehicles.","Make sure no pupil leaves alone, update families regularly through the school's official channel, and do not share pupils' names or photos with the media or online.","Keep a list of injured pupils and the hospital they were taken to, and tell their families at once.","Stay calm with children who are upset, stay with them and explain what happened in simple, honest words."]},{heading:"6. Teaching the safety lessons in this app",body:"The app has lessons for each age group. Teach them in class and practice them in drills:",points:["Kindergarten: short stories and pictures. Stay away from fire, matches, lighters and heaters; when the alarm rings, stay with the teacher and go out; tell an adult.","Primary: the escape plan, smoke and staying low, the number 115 and the school drill. Children tell an adult first; if no adult is with them, they call 115 themselves from a safe place.","Secondary: evacuation planning, first aid and leadership. Teenagers can help the teacher count and guide younger pupils, but they never fight fire and never go back inside.","Children never use fire extinguishers, never fight fire, never touch electrical wires or gas valves and never go near objects that may explode: they get out, tell an adult and call 115 only as the lessons teach.","Teach without frightening: use calm words, let children ask questions and repeat the drill until it becomes routine.","Use each lesson's game and quiz to check what the children learned, and share the key messages with parents."]}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/w8_school_emergency_plan.webp",title:"A teacher takes the roll call at the assembly point",description:"Pupils stand in their class lines at the assembly point while the teacher checks the class list and a staff member helps a pupil in a wheelchair."}],quiz:[{question:"At the assembly point, a teacher finds that one pupil is missing. What should the teacher do?",options:[{text:"Tell the person in charge at once: the pupil's name and where they were last seen",correct:!0},{text:"Go back into the building alone at once and search every classroom and every toilet",correct:!1},{text:"Send two older pupils back inside to look for the missing pupil quickly",correct:!1},{text:"Wait until the end of the school day, then tell the principal about it",correct:!1}],explanation:"Never send anyone back inside. The person in charge tells the Civil Defense team, who search the building."},{question:"What should the drill log record after every drill?",options:[{text:"Date and time, drill type, time to leave and count, problems and who fixes them",correct:!0},{text:"Only the names of the teachers on duty on every floor of the building that day",correct:!1},{text:"Nothing, because a drill is only practice and needs no written record at all",correct:!1},{text:"Only whether the alarm bell could be heard clearly in the main corridor and the yard that day",correct:!1}],explanation:"The log shows what worked and what must be fixed: the date and time, the type of drill, how long leaving and counting took, who was missing or late, the problems found, and who will fix them by when."},{question:"During reunification, an adult you do not know asks to take a pupil home. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Check the school records and hand the child only to an adult named there",correct:!0},{text:"Let the child go if the child says they have known the adult well for a long time",correct:!1},{text:"Let the child go if the adult knows the child's full name and class correctly",correct:!1},{text:"Tell the child to walk home alone so that the area becomes less crowded",correct:!1}],explanation:"Hand a child only to a parent or another adult named in the school records, after checking their identity, and write down who took the child and when."},{question:"A pupil who uses a wheelchair has lessons on the second floor. What should the school plan include?",options:[{text:"A personal plan with trained helpers and a safe waiting place, practiced in drills",correct:!0},{text:"Taking the pupil down in the elevator, because it is the fastest way out in a fire",correct:!1},{text:"Leaving the pupil in the classroom with the door open until the fire is put out",correct:!1},{text:"Asking two classmates to carry the pupil down the stairs without any training",correct:!1}],explanation:"Each pupil who cannot leave alone needs a personal plan: named adults and a back-up, a safe place to wait if the stairs cannot be used, 115 told where they are, and practice in drills. Elevators are not used in a fire unless they are designed for evacuation, and children never carry anyone."},{question:"What do the primary lessons teach children to do if they see a fire?",options:[{text:"Get out and tell an adult; call 115 themselves only if no adult is there",correct:!0},{text:"Use the nearest extinguisher, because a small fire is easy for anyone to put out",correct:!1},{text:"Pour water on the fire, then tell the teacher later if it does not go out",correct:!1},{text:"Hide in a quiet corner of the classroom until the teacher comes to find them",correct:!1}],explanation:"Children never fight fire or use extinguishers. They get out, tell an adult, and call 115 from a safe place only if no adult is with them."},{question:"A small pan fire starts on the stove in the school kitchen. The cook is trained and the alarm has been raised. What is the right action?",options:[{text:"Pour a jug of water on the pan at once so that the hot oil cools down quickly",correct:!1},{text:"Carry the burning pan out to the yard, well away from the pupils and the staff",correct:!1},{text:"Turn off the heat if it is safe and cover the pan with a lid or a fire blanket",correct:!0},{text:"Ask an older pupil to bring the extinguisher from the corridor as fast as possible",correct:!1}],explanation:"A small pan fire is covered with a lid or a fire blanket and the heat is turned off if it is safe. Water on burning oil throws the oil out, carrying the pan spills it, and pupils never fight fires or fetch extinguishers for a fire."}],steps:[{title:"Lead your class out",detail:"When the alarm sounds, take the class list, lead the pupils out by the planned route (the alternative route if the main one is blocked), close the door behind you and do not let anyone go back for bags.",critical:!0},{title:"Sweep and help on the way out",detail:"Named staff check the toilets, laboratories, library and yard on their way out, only if it is safe; named helpers bring out the pupils who need help, as their personal plans say.",critical:!0},{title:"Count at the assembly point",detail:"Count your pupils against the class list and report to the person in charge at once: all present, or the name of anyone missing and where they were last seen.",critical:!0},{title:"Nobody goes back in",detail:"Never send a pupil or a teacher back inside to look for someone: the person in charge tells the Civil Defense team, who will search. Nobody re-enters until Civil Defense or the person in charge says it is safe.",critical:!0},{title:"Reunite pupils with their families safely",detail:"If pupils cannot go back to class, hand each child only to a parent or another adult named in the school records, after checking their identity, and write down who took which child and when."},{title:"Record the drill and fix the problems",detail:"After every drill or real event, write in the drill log what happened, who was missing or late, the problems found and who will fix them by when. How often drills are held is set by the Ministry of Education."}],warnings:["Never send a pupil or a teacher back into the building to look for someone: tell the Civil Defense team, who will search.","Pupils never fight fires, use extinguishers or touch gas valves or electric wires: they get out and tell an adult.","Do not use elevators in a fire unless they are designed and marked for evacuation, and children never carry another person."],tips:["Keep the class list and the personal evacuation plans of your pupils by the classroom door, ready to grab.","Hold drills from different rooms, at break time and with the main route blocked, so that the alternative route is practiced too.","Tell families in advance how reunification works: where to go, what to bring and that children are released only there.","After a drill, talk calmly with the children and let them ask questions, so that the drill does not frighten them."],activity:{prompt:'During an evacuation drill, a member of staff stands at the main staircase holding a sign: "This route is blocked." Your class is on the second floor. What do you do?',options:[{text:"Use the main staircase anyway, because it is only a drill and it is quicker",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the drill tests the plan; in a real fire the main stairs may be full of smoke, so the alternative route must be practiced."},{text:"Lead the class by the alternative route in the plan, count the pupils at the assembly point and report",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the plan has an alternative route for exactly this case. Practicing it shows whether it works and how long it takes."},{text:"Keep the class in the classroom until the main staircase is opened again",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: staying inside is not the plan for a fire evacuation; waiting wastes the time pupils need to get out."},{text:"Tell the pupils to find their own way down by any other stairs",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: pupils must stay with their teacher so that the class can be counted and nobody gets lost."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"The fire alarm sounds during break. Smoke is coming from the corridor near the science laboratory. You are the teacher on yard duty; many pupils are in the yard and some are in the toilets near the corridor. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"Move the pupils in the yard to the assembly point away from the building, while the named staff check the toilets on their way out if it is safe, and make sure 115 has been called",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: get the pupils away from the building first. Toilets are checked only by named staff and only if it is safe; someone must confirm the call to 115."},{text:"Go into the laboratory corridor yourself to see what is burning",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: going toward the smoke puts you in danger and leaves the pupils in the yard without an adult."},{text:"Keep the pupils together in the yard next to the building so that they can watch",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the yard next to the building may be reached by smoke, falling glass or fire vehicles; pupils go to the assembly point."}]},{prompt:"At the assembly point, one class is one pupil short. A classmate says the missing pupil went to the toilet near the laboratory just before the alarm. What now?",options:[{text:"Send the classmate back quickly to fetch the missing pupil from the toilet",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: never send a pupil back inside; the classmate could be trapped by the smoke too."},{text:"Tell the person in charge at once the pupil’s name and that they were last seen at the toilet near the laboratory, so that Civil Defense is told",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: an exact name and last-seen place let the Civil Defense team search quickly. Nobody from the school goes back in."},{text:"Go back in yourself quickly, because you know the building well",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: you could be trapped by smoke; searching a burning building is the work of the Civil Defense team."}]}],realCase:'Illustrative scenario: during a planned evacuation drill at a school, the class counts at the assembly point showed one pupil missing from a classroom on the upper floor. The teacher reported the name at once, and the drill leader found the pupil, who is deaf, still in the classroom: she had not heard the bell, and her named helper was absent that day. After the drill the school updated her personal plan with the pupil and her family: a flashing light in the classroom, a back-up helper for every lesson and a written card that says "Leave now". The next drill went smoothly. The lesson: drills find the gaps in the plan before a real fire does.'},{id:22,code:"GOV-122",icon:"Landmark",accent:"#7c3aed",title:"Sensitive institutions: data centers, archives, museums, banks and utilities",subtitle:"Fixed extinguishing systems, records and collections, continuity, controlled evacuation, power and water",summary:"Buildings that hold data, records, collections, money or essential services need special fire protection and a plan to keep working. This lesson explains what staff must know: the alarms of fixed systems, protecting records and collections, business continuity, controlled evacuation, and power and water.",blocks:[{body:"In a data center, an archive, a museum, a bank or a power or water facility, a fire or a flood can destroy what cannot be replaced and stop services that many people depend on. Life always comes first: no record, object, money or machine is worth a person's life. This lesson is at awareness level. Designing, installing and testing fixed systems is the work of qualified specialists; follow the requirements of Civil Defense and the licensing authority. International references: NFPA 75 (information technology equipment), NFPA 76 (telecommunications facilities), NFPA 2001 (clean agent systems), NFPA 909 (museums, libraries and places of worship), NFPA 914 (historic buildings), NFPA 1660 and ISO 22301 (continuity)."},{heading:"1. Fixed gas systems: what every staff member must know",body:"Server rooms, archives and vaults are often protected by a fixed system that fills the whole room with a gas, either a clean agent or CO₂. The gas protects the contents, but people must be out of the room before it is released:",points:["Learn the signals: the fire alarm, the pre-discharge alarm (a distinct sound or flashing light that means the gas is about to be released) and the warning signs at the door.","When the pre-discharge alarm sounds: stop work, leave the room at once by the nearest exit, close the door and warn others. The time delay is there to give people time to get out, not to finish work.","Do not go back in after a discharge until the person in charge or Civil Defense says it is safe: the room may still hold the agent, smoke and products of combustion.","CO₂ systems: the extinguishing concentration can kill people, so they are normally used only in rooms where no one works and are locked out while people are inside.","Never block or cover gas nozzles, detectors, sprinkler heads or manual release and abort stations, and do not stack boxes up to the ceiling. (US OSHA rules keep at least 45.7 cm (18 inches) clear below sprinklers.)","Only trained, authorized staff use the manual release or the abort switch, and only as the site procedure says.","Report at once any fault, an alarm that will not reset or a system shut down for work: until it works again, extra precautions are needed, such as a person watching for fire.","Inspection, testing and maintenance are done by qualified specialists; the required intervals in Duhok are set by Civil Defense and are not yet confirmed."]},{heading:"2. Protecting records and collections",body:"Most losses start with poor housekeeping, a leak or a small fire. Prevention:",points:["Keep storage areas clean and tidy: no rubbish, piles of cardboard or unneeded furniture, and no smoking or open flames where records or collections are kept.","Store flammable liquids, such as cleaning products or generator fuel, in locked metal cabinets away from people and records.","Do not store records directly below or above water, heating or drainage pipes, and keep the lowest shelf off the floor (the UN archives guidance says 15 cm).","Know where water could get in, inspect these places regularly and think about a water-leak alarm in storage rooms.","Check wiring, plugs and cords regularly and replace worn parts.","Label vital records clearly so they can be found and removed at once, and keep copies in another place (the 3-2-1 rule).","Agree on a salvage priority list in advance: first the catalog and the records of the collection, then the most important and rare items."]},{heading:"3. After a fire or flood: safe salvage",body:"Salvage starts only after the building has been declared safe:",points:["Do not try to save documents or objects during the emergency itself: get out.","Never enter an affected area until the responsible authority says it is safe, and take great care where water is near electrical outlets or equipment.","Move fast: in warm, humid conditions, mold can appear within 48 hours.","Do not start removing material until the person in charge has a plan with priorities and the team has been briefed.","Do not open wet books or pull wet sheets apart; records that cannot be dried within the first 48 hours can be frozen and dried later by specialists.","Keep wet material cool with moving air; avoid high heat and strong sunlight, which damage records.","Ask a preservation specialist about valuable items before treating them."]},{heading:"4. Business continuity: keep essential services running",body:"A continuity plan says how the institution keeps its most important work going during and after a disruption:",points:["Identify the critical functions and the resources they need: people, systems, data, records, buildings and suppliers.","Business impact analysis: what happens if each function stops and how quickly it must be restored; the functions with the greatest impact are restored first.","Back up all critical information frequently, keep a copy in another place and scan important paper records.","IT recovery follows the same priorities as the business functions it supports.","Plan alternatives: another site, manual procedures and a way to reach staff and the public.","Test the plan periodically and update it after every exercise and every real incident.","International standards: ISO 22301 (business continuity management systems) and NFPA 1660 (which now includes the former NFPA 1600)."]},{heading:"5. Controlled evacuation and security",body:"In a bank, a vault, a data center or a museum, security and life safety must work together:",points:["Life safety comes first: security locks must never stop people from getting out, and exit doors open from the inside without a key.","Plan who secures what: for example, cash drawers and vault doors are closed only if it takes seconds and puts no one at risk; nobody stays behind to guard valuables.","Escort visitors and contractors out and count them with the staff at the assembly point.","Keep a list of who is inside restricted areas, such as the server room, the vault or the store, so that you can tell Civil Defense if anyone is missing.","Security staff keep entrances under control so that no one goes back in, and meet Civil Defense with keys, access cards and the building plan.","When you call 115, say what the building holds and any special hazards: fixed gas systems, large batteries, fuel or chemicals."]},{heading:"6. Power and water",body:"Power and water facilities, and the power and water supply of every sensitive building, need clear arrangements:",points:["Keep an up-to-date list of emergency contacts: 115 Civil Defense, 122 ambulance, 104 police, and the emergency numbers of the electricity and water departments, confirmed with each department.","Know where the main electricity, water and gas shut-offs are; only trained, authorized staff operate them, and only when it is safe.","Standby generators and UPS systems are installed and serviced by qualified people; a portable generator is used outdoors only, at least 6 meters (20 feet) from windows, doors and vents.","If water leaks and it is safe, stop it at the valve, keep away from water near electricity and tell the person in charge; cover records and equipment with plastic sheeting only if it is safe.","In a power or water facility, follow the site's own emergency procedures and the instructions of Civil Defense; the local requirements for these facilities are not yet confirmed."]}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/w8_sensitive_fixed_system.webp",title:"Staff leave a server room when the pre-discharge alarm sounds",description:"A flashing warning light and a sign above the door of a server room; two staff members walk out calmly and close the door."}],quiz:[{question:"In a server room, the pre-discharge alarm of the fixed gas system starts to sound. What should you do?",options:[{text:"Stop work, leave at once, close the door and warn others",correct:!0},{text:"Stay at your desk and save your work, since the gas will not come out for a while",correct:!1},{text:"Press the abort switch yourself, even though you were never trained to use it",correct:!1},{text:"Open the windows and the door wide so that the gas can escape more quickly",correct:!1}],explanation:"The pre-discharge alarm and the time delay give people time to get out before the gas is released. Leave at once by the nearest exit; only trained, authorized staff use the abort switch."},{question:"After a fixed gas system has discharged, when may staff go back into the room?",options:[{text:"Only when the person in charge or Civil Defense says it is safe",correct:!0},{text:"As soon as the alarm stops, because the gas is always safe to breathe",correct:!1},{text:"After a few minutes, if no flames can be seen through the door window",correct:!1},{text:"Straight away, to check which equipment the fire has damaged",correct:!1}],explanation:"The room may still hold the agent, smoke and products of combustion. Nobody goes back in until the person in charge or Civil Defense says it is safe."},{question:"Which is the best place to store important paper records?",options:[{text:"On shelves off the floor, away from water and drainage pipes",correct:!0},{text:"On the basement floor, where the boxes are out of everyone's way",correct:!1},{text:"In boxes stacked up to the ceiling, right under the sprinklers",correct:!1},{text:"On the top shelves, directly under the main water pipes",correct:!1}],explanation:"Water is a major threat to records: keep them off the floor and away from pipes, and keep the space under sprinklers and gas nozzles clear."},{question:"Why must wet records be dealt with quickly?",options:[{text:"In warm, humid conditions, mold can appear within 48 hours",correct:!0},{text:"Wet paper burns more easily than dry paper once it has dried",correct:!1},{text:"The ink on wet paper always disappears within the first hour",correct:!1},{text:"Wet records become too heavy for their shelves within minutes",correct:!1}],explanation:"Mold can appear within 48 hours in warm, humid conditions. Records that cannot be dried in that time can be frozen and dried later by specialists."},{question:"What is a business impact analysis?",options:[{text:"A study of what happens if each function stops and how fast it must restart",correct:!0},{text:"A yearly report of the money that the institution earned and spent",correct:!1},{text:"A list of the staff who have finished their yearly fire extinguisher training course",correct:!1},{text:"A check of the building's fire exits carried out by the security staff",correct:!1}],explanation:"A business impact analysis finds the critical functions and their resources, the impact of a disruption and how quickly each function must be restored; the functions with the greatest impact come first."},{question:"In a data center, a technician wants to stack boxes of spare equipment up to the ceiling in the protected server room. What is the right reply?",options:[{text:"Yes, as long as the boxes are made of metal and not of cardboard",correct:!1},{text:"Yes, because the fixed gas system protects everything in the room anyway",correct:!1},{text:"No: boxes must not block gas nozzles, detectors or sprinkler heads",correct:!0},{text:"No, but only because the boxes would make the room look untidy",correct:!1}],explanation:"Stacked boxes can block the gas nozzles, detectors and sprinkler heads, so the system may not detect or put out a fire. Keep them clear and do not stack storage up to the ceiling."}],steps:[{title:"Life first: leave when the alarm sounds",detail:"When the fire alarm or the pre-discharge alarm of a fixed gas system sounds, stop work and leave by the nearest exit. Nobody stays behind to save records, equipment, collections or money.",critical:!0},{title:"Secure only what takes seconds",detail:"As the plan says, close a cash drawer or a vault door only if it takes seconds and puts no one at risk; close the room door behind you."},{title:"Account for everyone",detail:"Escort visitors and contractors out, use the list of people inside restricted areas and count everyone at the assembly point with the staff.",critical:!0},{title:"Tell 115 what the building holds",detail:"When you call 115, give the location first and then the special hazards: fixed gas systems, large batteries, fuel or chemicals, and whether anyone may still be inside.",critical:!0},{title:"Control the entrances",detail:"Security staff keep the entrances under control so that nobody goes back in, and meet Civil Defense with keys, access cards and the building plan."},{title:"Recover only when it is safe",detail:"Nobody re-enters until the person in charge or Civil Defense says it is safe. Then salvage follows the priority list and the continuity plan restores the most critical services first."}],warnings:["Never go back into a room after a fixed gas system has discharged until the person in charge or Civil Defense says it is safe: the room may still hold the agent, smoke and products of combustion.","Never let security locks stop people from getting out: exit doors open from the inside without a key.","Never block or cover gas nozzles, detectors, sprinkler heads or manual release and abort stations."],tips:["Rehearse the pre-discharge alarm with everyone who enters protected rooms, including cleaners and contractors.","Keep the salvage priority list and copies of vital records in another place, and check them when the plan is updated.","Report at once any fault in a fixed system or any time it is shut down for work, so that extra precautions can be put in place.","Keep the list of people inside restricted areas up to date during the day, not only at the start of the shift."],activity:{prompt:"You are a cashier in a bank branch. The fire alarm sounds. Your cash drawer is open and three customers are waiting at your counter. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Count the cash and carry it to the vault before leaving",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: counting and carrying money takes time you do not have; nobody stays behind to protect valuables."},{text:"Stay at the counter to guard the money until the security staff arrive",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: life comes first; staying in a building on alarm puts you at risk and delays the headcount."},{text:"Lock the front doors so that nobody can take the money during the confusion",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: exit doors must always open from the inside; locking them can trap people in a fire."},{text:"Close the drawer if it takes a second, then guide the customers out by the nearest exit to the assembly point",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: secure only what takes seconds and puts no one at risk, then get people out; the customers are counted at the assembly point with the staff."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"You are working in the archive stacks, which are protected by a fixed clean agent system. The pre-discharge alarm starts to sound and a light flashes at the door. A colleague wants to carry out two boxes of rare files first. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Help your colleague carry the boxes out quickly, since the gas is not released yet",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the time delay is there to let people get out, not to move records; the boxes can wait, your lives cannot."},{text:"Stop, leave at once with your colleague by the nearest exit, close the door and warn others",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: on the pre-discharge alarm everyone leaves at once. Records are protected by the system and by the salvage plan, not by staff staying inside."},{text:"Press the abort switch, because you cannot see any smoke",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: only trained, authorized staff use the abort switch, and only as the site procedure says; no smoke in view does not mean no fire."}]},{prompt:"The gas has discharged. A little later the alarm stops, and the director wants staff to go in straight away to check the damage to the records. What should happen?",options:[{text:"Nobody goes in until the person in charge or Civil Defense says it is safe; then a briefed team works by the salvage priority list",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the room may still hold the agent, smoke and products of combustion. Salvage starts only when it is safe, with a plan and a briefed team."},{text:"Staff go in now, because the alarm has stopped and the gas is safe to breathe",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the alarm stopping does not mean the room is safe; only the person in charge or Civil Defense can say that."},{text:"Open the door and the windows to air the room, then go in after a few minutes",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: you still enter a room that has not been declared safe; airing and re-entry are decided by those in charge."}]}],realCase:"Illustrative scenario: over a weekend, a water pipe burst above a records store of a government institution. On the next working day, staff found water and several wet boxes. They did not go in at once: the person in charge had the electricity to the room isolated and the area declared safe. Because the lowest shelves were raised off the floor and the vital records had copies in another place, the institution kept working. A briefed team moved the wet boxes by the salvage priority list and kept them cool for specialists to treat. The plan was updated to move records away from the pipe. The lesson: prevention, copies and a salvage plan protect what cannot be replaced."},{id:23,code:"GOV-123",icon:"ClipboardCheck",accent:"#c2410c",title:"Practical extinguisher assessment for fire wardens",subtitle:"What an instructor checks in a hands-on assessment with a training extinguisher or a simulator",summary:"A fire warden must show, not only say, that they can make the right decision and use an extinguisher safely. This lesson describes a typical practical checklist: the go/no-go decision, checking the extinguisher, PASS within the range on the label, backing away safely, reporting the use and keeping the training itself safe.",blocks:[{body:`Knowing PASS by heart is not enough. In a practical assessment, the warden uses a training extinguisher (usually filled with water) or a digital or gas-fired simulator under an instructor. The checklist below is a typical example from international practice, not the Directorate's standard: the pass criteria and who may assess and certify fire wardens in Duhok are not yet confirmed.

Classes here use NFPA 10 letters as the project reference; the official system for Duhok is not yet confirmed. EN 2 equivalents: NFPA A = EN A; NFPA B (liquids) = EN B; NFPA B (gases) = EN C; NFPA C (live electrical equipment) has no EN class; NFPA D = EN D; NFPA K = EN F.`},{heading:"1. Step one: the go/no-go decision",body:"Before touching the extinguisher, the warden answers these questions aloud. Every answer must be yes:",points:["Alarm: has the alarm been raised and has someone called 115?","Size: is the fire small and just starting, in one object such as a wastebasket or a pan?","Type: is this extinguisher right for what is burning? The label shows its classes.","Exit: is there a clear way out behind me, with no smoke or heat blocking it?","Training and fitness: am I trained and physically able to use this extinguisher?","Gas and electricity: for a gas fire, has the gas been stopped, only if that was safe? For an electrical fire, has the power been cut if that was safe? Never put out a gas flame that cannot be stopped.","In a typical checklist, starting before the alarm is raised is a fail. If the scenario shows a spreading fire or a room filling with smoke, the right answer is no: get out, close the door and make sure 115 has been called."]},{heading:"2. Checking the extinguisher before use",body:"In a few seconds, the warden checks:",points:["Label: the classes the extinguisher is rated for, the operating instructions, the warnings, and the range and discharge time if the label gives them.","Pressure gauge, if there is one: the needle is in the green. CO₂ extinguishers have no gauge.","Safety pin and tamper seal in place; body, hose and nozzle undamaged and clear.","Many instructors expect a short test discharge right after the pin is pulled, pointed away from people, to show that the extinguisher works.","If anything is wrong, the warden takes another extinguisher and reports the faulty one."]},{heading:"3. PASS within the range on the label",body:"The instructor watches the technique:",points:["Pull: pulls the pin, which also breaks the tamper seal, with the nozzle pointing away from themselves.","Aim: aims low, at the base of the fire, not at the flames.","Squeeze: squeezes the handle slowly and evenly.","Sweep: sweeps from side to side across the base until the fire appears to be out.","Distance: starts within the range shown on the label and moves closer only as the fire gets smaller; there is no single correct distance for every extinguisher.","Position: keeps the exit behind them and never lets the fire, heat or smoke come between them and the way out.","Agent: CO₂ has a short range and its horn becomes very cold, so hold only the handle; wet chemical is applied gently as its label shows; never water on cooking oil or near live electricity."]},{heading:"4. After the fire: back away, watch and report",body:"The assessment does not end when the flames go out:",points:["Back away from the extinguished fire while still facing it, in case it flares up again; never turn your back on it.","If the fire flares up again and the extinguisher still works, use PASS again; if the extinguisher is empty and the fire is not out, get out at once and close the door.","Tell the Civil Defense team what burned, what was used and where, even if the fire seems to be out.","Report every use: an extinguisher used even for a moment is taken out of service and recharged or replaced; it never goes back on the wall.","Record the use and tell the person in charge, so that a full extinguisher is put in its place at once."]},{heading:"5. A typical assessment checklist",body:"An example of what an instructor may record. This is a typical checklist, not the Directorate's standard:",points:["Decision: gave the right go or no-go answer for the scenario and raised the alarm first.","Selection: chose an extinguisher rated for the class of fire.","Checks: checked the label, the gauge, the pin and the seal.","Technique: used PASS correctly, within the range on the label, aiming at the base.","Safety: kept the exit behind them and backed away facing the fire.","Reporting: reported the use so that the extinguisher is recharged or replaced.","Who may assess and certify fire wardens, the pass mark and how often the assessment is repeated are set by the Civil Defense Directorate and are not yet confirmed. In the US, OSHA requires training for designated staff when they are first assigned and at least once a year after that."]},{heading:"6. Keeping the training session safe",body:"The training itself must not hurt anyone:",points:["Use training extinguishers (usually water) or a digital simulator where possible; a live-fire simulator is run only by a trained instructor according to its manufacturer's instructions.","Outdoors, in an open area away from buildings, vehicles and anything that can burn; the instructor checks the wind so that smoke and agent blow away from the trainees.","The instructor can shut off the fire source at once and keeps a charged extinguisher and a first-aid kit ready.","Brief everyone first: what will happen, the stop signal and where to stand; one trainee at a time.","Ask about health problems such as asthma and adapt the session to each person's abilities; children do not take part in extinguisher training.","Powder and CO₂ are discharged only where the instructor allows: powder clouds reduce visibility and irritate the lungs, and CO₂ is never discharged in a small closed room.","Who may run live-fire training and where it is allowed are set by Civil Defense and the local authorities and are not yet confirmed."],critical:!0}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/w8_extinguisher_assessment.webp",title:"A warden is assessed with a training extinguisher",description:"Outdoors, a trainee aims a training extinguisher at the base of a simulator's flame with the exit route behind them, while the instructor holds a checklist and stands by the shut-off."}],quiz:[{question:"In an assessment scenario, a fire is spreading across a large storeroom and smoke is filling it. What is the correct action?",options:[{text:"Do not use the extinguisher: raise the alarm, get out, close the door, call 115",correct:!0},{text:"Use two extinguishers at the same time to stop the fire before it spreads further",correct:!1},{text:"Open the windows to clear the smoke, then aim the extinguisher at the flames",correct:!1},{text:"Wait inside the storeroom near the door until the Civil Defense team arrives",correct:!1}],explanation:"A spreading fire and a room filling with smoke mean no-go: the right decision is to raise the alarm, get out, close the door and make sure 115 has been called."},{question:"How does the warden know where to start using the extinguisher?",options:[{text:"From the range on the label, exit behind, moving closer as the fire shrinks",correct:!0},{text:"Always from the same fixed distance, whatever the type or size of extinguisher",correct:!1},{text:"As close to the flames as possible, so that none of the agent is wasted",correct:!1},{text:"From wherever the smoke is lightest, even if the exit is then in front of them",correct:!1}],explanation:"There is no fixed distance. Start within the range on the label, keep the exit behind you and move closer only as the fire gets smaller."},{question:"The fire is out. How should the warden move away?",options:[{text:"Back away while still facing the fire, watching in case it flares up again",correct:!0},{text:"Turn around and walk quickly to the exit, because the fire is already completely out",correct:!1},{text:"Stay right beside the burned area and lean over it to feel for heat",correct:!1},{text:"Hang the used extinguisher back on its wall bracket before leaving",correct:!1}],explanation:"Back away from an extinguished fire while facing it, because it can flare up again. A used extinguisher is reported and recharged or replaced, never hung back on the wall."},{question:"An extinguisher was used for only two seconds. What happens next?",options:[{text:"It is reported, taken out of service and recharged or replaced",correct:!0},{text:"It goes back on the wall, because it is still almost completely full",correct:!1},{text:"It is kept for the next fire without telling the person in charge",correct:!1},{text:"The warden shakes it well and tests it again at the end of the day",correct:!1}],explanation:"Extinguishers are meant for one use: after any use, even a short one, the extinguisher is reported and recharged or replaced, and a full one is put in its place."},{question:"Which is a safe practice for the training session itself?",options:[{text:"An open outdoor area, an instructor able to cut the fire, a back-up extinguisher",correct:!0},{text:"A small closed room, so that the trainees learn to work in heavy smoke and real heat",correct:!1},{text:"Several trainees fighting the same live fire together to save some time",correct:!1},{text:"A gas-fired simulator that each trainee lights and runs on their own",correct:!1}],explanation:"Training must not hurt anyone: an open outdoor area away from things that burn, an instructor who controls the fire source and can shut it off, a charged back-up extinguisher, a first-aid kit, a briefing first and one trainee at a time."},{question:"During the assessment, you have backed away from the simulator fire. It flares up again and your extinguisher is now empty. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Fetch another extinguisher from the next floor and come back to fight it on your own",correct:!1},{text:"Get out at once, close the door and make sure 115 has been called",correct:!0},{text:"Throw the empty extinguisher at the base of the flames to smother them",correct:!1},{text:"Move closer and blow on the flames to push them away from the exit",correct:!1}],explanation:"If the extinguisher is empty and the fire is not out, the answer is no-go: get out at once, close the door to hold the fire back and make sure 115 has been called. Never go back alone to keep fighting a growing fire."}],steps:[{title:"Raise the alarm first",detail:"Before touching the extinguisher, raise the alarm and make sure 115 has been called. In a typical checklist, starting before the alarm is a fail.",critical:!0},{title:"Answer the go/no-go questions aloud",detail:"Small and just starting? Right extinguisher for what is burning? Exit behind you, with no smoke or heat blocking it? Trained and able? Gas or power stopped only if safe? If any answer is no, get out and close the door.",critical:!0},{title:"Check the extinguisher",detail:"Read the label (classes, instructions, range); check the gauge needle is in the green if there is a gauge, the pin and tamper seal are in place and the hose and nozzle are undamaged. If anything is wrong, take another one and report the faulty one."},{title:"Use PASS from the range on the label",detail:"Pull the pin, aim low at the base of the fire, squeeze the handle evenly and sweep across the base. Start from the range shown on the label and move closer only as the fire gets smaller, keeping the exit behind you.",critical:!0},{title:"Back away and watch",detail:"When the fire seems out, back away while still facing it. If it flares up and the extinguisher still works, use PASS again; if the extinguisher is empty and the fire is not out, get out at once and close the door.",critical:!0},{title:"Report the use",detail:"Tell the Civil Defense team what burned and what you used. The used extinguisher is taken out of service and recharged or replaced, never hung back on the wall."}],warnings:["Never let the fire, heat or smoke get between you and the way out: if that starts to happen, stop and leave.","Never put water or CO₂ on a cooking-oil fire, never use water near live electricity, and never put out a gas flame that cannot be stopped.","In training, never discharge CO₂ or powder in a small closed room, and never hold the CO₂ horn: hold only the handle."],tips:["Say each go/no-go answer out loud: the instructor assesses your decision, not only your technique.","Practice with the types of extinguisher installed in your own building, so that the handle and range feel familiar.","Ask the instructor for feedback on your aim, stance and distance, and repeat the exercise until it is smooth."],activity:{prompt:'In a practical assessment, the instructor lights a simulator fire marked "cooking oil in a pan". The alarm has been raised and your exit is behind you. On the table are a water training extinguisher, a CO₂ extinguisher and a wet chemical extinguisher whose label shows Class K (NFPA K = EN F). Which do you choose?',options:[{text:"The water training extinguisher, because it is the one used in most drills",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: water on burning oil turns to steam at once and throws the oil out; choosing it would fail the selection item."},{text:"The CO₂ extinguisher, because it smothers the fire without leaving residue",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: CO₂ is not the answer for a cooking-oil fire; its jet can push the oil out, and the hot oil can flare up again."},{text:"The wet chemical extinguisher, applied gently from the range on its label as its label shows",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: wet chemical is made for cooking oil (NFPA K = EN F). Applied gently, it cools the oil and forms a layer that stops it reigniting."},{text:"None of them: throw a wet towel over the pan from where you stand",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: a thrown wet towel can splash burning oil; use a fire blanket or a lid placed carefully, or the right extinguisher."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"Your assessment scenario: a wastebasket fire in an office, with a water training extinguisher beside you. The instructor is watching and has said nothing about the alarm. What do you do before touching the extinguisher?",options:[{text:"Start PASS at once, because the fire is small and every second counts",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: starting before the alarm is raised is a fail in a typical checklist; the alarm and the call to 115 come first."},{text:"Raise the alarm, make sure 115 is called, then answer the go/no-go questions aloud",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the alarm comes first, then the go/no-go decision. The instructor marks the decision as well as the technique."},{text:"Ask the instructor to make the fire bigger so that the test is more realistic",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: the assessment tests a small, starting fire; a growing fire means no-go and getting out."}]},{prompt:"You pick up the water extinguisher and see that the gauge needle is in the red and the tamper seal is broken. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Use it anyway, because the fire is small and there is no time to change",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: it may fail in front of the fire and leave you exposed; a faulty extinguisher is not used."},{text:"Take another suitable extinguisher, report the faulty one and continue only if the go/no-go answers are still yes",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: checking the extinguisher is part of the assessment. A faulty unit is reported, and the decision is checked again before you act."},{text:"Shake it hard and try a test discharge toward the fire",correct:!1,feedback:"Wrong: shaking does not restore lost pressure; using a faulty unit near the fire is unsafe."}]}],realCase:"Illustrative scenario: a fire warden in a government building took the practical assessment. He raised the alarm, answered every go/no-go question aloud, checked the label, gauge, pin and seal, and put out the simulator fire with good technique from the range on the label. Then he turned his back and walked away. The instructor marked the safety item as not achieved: a fire that seems to be out can flare up again. The warden practiced again, backing away while facing the fire and watching it, reported the use so that the extinguisher would be recharged, and passed the item on the next attempt. The lesson: the assessment is not over when the flames go out."},{id:24,code:"GOV-124",icon:"ShieldAlert",accent:"#7f1d1d",title:"Bomb threats and suspicious items",subtitle:"Taking a phone threat, an unknown item, not touching, keeping away, evacuating under the plan and reporting to 104 and 115",summary:"Government offices may receive a bomb threat by phone, letter or online, or find a suspicious item in or around the building. This module shows staff how to take a threat calmly and record the details, how to act near a suspicious item (do not touch, do not go closer, keep people away) and how to evacuate under the office plan and report to 104 and 115.",blocks:[{body:"Many threats turn out to be hoaxes, but every threat is taken seriously until the competent authorities decide. Staff do not judge whether a threat is real; their job is to record the details accurately, report at once and follow instructions. The decision to evacuate is made by the office’s designated official under the emergency plan and the instructions of the police and Civil Defense. The office’s reporting channels are set by the competent authorities; this module gives the general principles."},{heading:"1. A threat by phone",body:"The person who takes the call hears a lot of important information:",points:["Stay calm, do not interrupt and do not hang up; let the caller keep talking.","Write down the exact words, the time, any number shown and background sounds.","If you can, ask: when? where? why? and note the voice (male or female, accent, speed).","Signal a coworker to call 104 from another phone and alert the person in charge.","When the call ends, report to the person in charge and 104 at once and stay by the phone so the police can talk to you."]},{heading:"2. A written or electronic threat",body:"If the threat arrives by letter, email or a social media message, do not delete it and do not forward it to anyone except the authorities. Handle a paper letter as little as possible and put it in a clean place; take a screenshot or photo of the message and note the time. Report to the person in charge and 104 at once. Spreading the threat on social media causes fear and confusion and can hamper the police."},{heading:"3. A suspicious item",body:"Any item with no owner, where no one knows who left it, in an unusual place or hidden in an unusual way can be suspicious. Ask whether its owner is nearby. If doubt remains: do not touch it, move it, open it or cover it. Do not use a phone or radio near it. Get people away from the area and, from a safe place, report to the person in charge, 104 and 115: the exact location, when it was found and what you saw. Even if the item looks harmless, the decision belongs to the authorities.",critical:!0},{heading:"4. Evacuating under the plan",body:"When the designated official decides to evacuate, leave calmly and follow instructions. Choose a route that does not pass near the suspicious item and do not use the elevator. Help visitors and people with disabilities. Gather at the assembly point in the plan, where the person in charge counts staff. Do not go back yourself to search for other items. No one goes back in until the police and Civil Defense say the building is safe."}],steps:[{title:"1. Stay calm and write it down",detail:"During a phone threat, do not hang up and write down the words, time and sounds."},{title:"2. Do not touch",detail:"Do not move, open or cover the suspicious item; do not use a phone or radio near it.",critical:!0},{title:"3. Keep people away",detail:"Get people away from the area and let no one approach.",critical:!0},{title:"4. Report",detail:"From a safe place, report to the person in charge, 104 and 115: the location, the time and what you saw or heard.",critical:!0},{title:"5. Evacuate under the plan",detail:"When the decision is made, leave by a route away from the item, stay at the assembly point and do not return until the authorities allow it."}],warnings:["Never touch, move or carry out a suspicious item: moving it can set it off.","Do not use a phone or radio near a suspicious item; call from a distant, safe place.","Do not post the threat or photos of the item on social media: it causes fear and hampers the police."],tips:["Keep a phone threat checklist next to every reception phone.","Staff who know their work areas well notice unusual items quickly.","In evacuation drills, practice an alternative route and a backup assembly point.","Make sure every staff member knows the reporting route and the designated official’s name."],activity:{prompt:"You work at the office reception. A call comes in and someone says there is a bomb in the building, then goes quiet but stays on the line. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I hang up at once and say it is a hoax.",correct:!1,feedback:"Hanging up loses important information, and staff do not judge whether a threat is real."},{text:"I stay calm and on the line, ask questions and take notes, and signal a coworker to call 104.",correct:!0,feedback:"A longer call gives more information, and 104 is alerted quickly."},{text:"I shout in the hall for everyone to run out.",correct:!1,feedback:"The evacuation decision is made by the designated official under the plan; disorganized running can lead people toward danger."},{text:"I search the building myself for the bomb.",correct:!1,feedback:"That is the authorities’ job; searching can bring you close to a dangerous item and delays reporting."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"In the waiting hall a closed bag is under a chair. No one knows whose it is and no one has come for it. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I open the bag to look for the owner’s ID.",correct:!1,feedback:"Opening or moving a suspicious item can be dangerous; do not touch it."},{text:"I ask people nearby whose it is; if there is no owner, I leave it alone, move people away and report from a safe place.",correct:!0,feedback:"Asking often finds the owner; if not, leaving it, clearing the area and reporting is right."},{text:"I carry the bag outside to protect people.",correct:!1,feedback:"Moving it can increase the danger and makes you the closest person to it."}]},{prompt:"Following police instructions, the designated official has ordered an evacuation. The usual exit route passes the waiting hall. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I use the usual route because it is quicker.",correct:!1,feedback:"This route takes you near the suspicious item; use another route in the plan."},{text:"I leave by another route in the plan, help visitors and stay at the assembly point.",correct:!0,feedback:"A route away from the item and staying at the assembly point make the staff count and the authorities’ work easier."},{text:"I go back to my office for my computer and documents.",correct:!1,feedback:"Going back wastes time and keeps you inside; leave directly under the plan."}]}],realCase:"Training scenario: at a public service office, the receptionist received a threatening call. She did not hang up, used the checklist kept by the phone and signaled a colleague, who called 104 from another phone. Following police instructions, the designated official ordered an evacuation. Staff led visitors out by another route in the plan and were counted at the assembly point. After a search, the authorities declared the building safe. Her accurate notes helped the police.",quiz:[{question:"During a phone threat, which action is right?",options:[{text:"Staying on the line and writing down the exact words",correct:!0},{text:"Hanging up at once so fear does not spread",correct:!1},{text:"Threatening the caller back to scare him",correct:!1},{text:"Putting the call on the loudspeaker to every room",correct:!1}],explanation:"A longer call and accurate notes give the police important information."},{question:"A staff member sees an unattended bag in the corridor and no owner can be found. Which action is wrong?",options:[{text:"Moving people away from the corridor",correct:!1},{text:"Reporting to the person in charge and 104 from a safe place",correct:!1},{text:"Opening the bag to see what is inside",correct:!0},{text:"Noting the time and place the bag was found",correct:!1}],explanation:"Not touching, moving or opening is the basic rule; the decision belongs to the authorities."},{question:"Why is a phone not used near a suspicious item?",options:[{text:"Because it may add to the danger; call from far away",correct:!0},{text:"Because phone signal is weak inside buildings",correct:!1},{text:"Because the police dislike staff taking photos",correct:!1},{text:"Because the phone battery may run down quickly",correct:!1}],explanation:"Safety guidance says not to use phones or radios near a suspicious item and to call from a safe place."},{question:"A threat arrives at the office by email. What is the right action?",options:[{text:"Deleting the email so staff are not scared",correct:!1},{text:"Forwarding it to all staff so they are alert",correct:!1},{text:"Keeping it and reporting it to 104",correct:!0},{text:"Replying to the email to find out who sent it",correct:!1}],explanation:"Preserving the threat as it is matters for the police investigation; deleting or spreading it does harm."},{question:"After the evacuation a staff member wants to go back for her bag. What is right?",options:[{text:"She can go back if she is quick",correct:!1},{text:"She waits until the authorities say it is safe",correct:!0},{text:"She can go back if a colleague goes with her",correct:!1},{text:"She can go back if her office is on the ground floor",correct:!1}],explanation:"No one goes back in until the police and Civil Defense say it is safe, even briefly."},{question:"Who decides to evacuate the building after a bomb threat?",options:[{text:"The staff member who took the call, alone",correct:!1},{text:"The designated official, per the plan",correct:!0},{text:"The first person to shout in the hall",correct:!1},{text:"The visitors in the waiting hall by a vote",correct:!1}],explanation:"An organized decision under the plan and the authorities’ instructions gets people out by a safe route."}]}],t={lessons:e};export{t as default,e as lessons};
